93146834920,"version":1}],"factionRelationships":[],"knowledgeFacts":[{"readerLearnsAtEventId":"yZpgnpjBHSlNECwKUqvZs","originEventId":null,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","title":"The Count will Call the ten thousand deepest accounts into the Cold","description":"Vosk at the Hook: the Wick will fail before year's end. The ten thousand deepest accounts will be released from the Tally's charge and sent into the Cold Wards. Lists posted in thirty days.","tags":[],"id":"3wVyv-0D4jJwyJJDIn8UC","createdAt":1793146612170,"updatedAt":1793146615529,"version":3},{"readerLearnsAtEventId":"a8a4Mt67-JHW0f3gQMomY","originEventId":null,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","title":"The Throat will close in fifty-two days","description":"The Company's announcement: the throat-watch has recorded the onset of a closing phase. The Company withdraws; the indentured stay, their contracts held in trust.","tags":[],"id":"IuIH_ap5aCjp_TsGZT4Xy","createdAt":1793146569621,"updatedAt":1793146572965,"version":3},{"readerLearnsAtEventId":"aV3K3iPiIvRKl7xKEj1nU","originEventId":null,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","title":"The Tally's root opens only to the Director's seal and the Comptroller's key together","description":"The root, behind three doors in the Counting House vault, holds the rules. Two keys together can rewrite the Tally's book; nothing can switch it off.","tags":[],"id":"VHM8xuN0PzEmciuV2N9c7","createdAt":1793146587088,"updatedAt":1793146590430,"version":3},{"readerLearnsAtEventId":"jCCT8EN2Vj2l-O2J-Y878","originEventId":null,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","title":"The Mercy Bell left the Sabra system before the Closing","description":"Fen's family bought it at a wreck auction at Tannet, from an unidentified hulk, thirty years after the Closing. How it came out is unknown.","tags":[],"id":"X86sJFI6tMtII3GPAxjQB","createdAt":1793146654706,"updatedAt":1793146658077,"version":3},{"readerLearnsAtEventId":"HndU3cXz3axOEEAjEX_H4","originEventId":null,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","title":"The Company is moving the aft stores' fuel into the Covenant","description":"Haulers paid in hard Concord credit to carry fuel cassettes from the aft stores to the Covenant's hold. The Company says it leaves three hundred years of fuel for the Wick.","tags":[],"id":"Yzu619FSHx3dp2iajWWTX","createdAt":1793146599625,"updatedAt":1793146602963,"version":3},{"readerLearnsAtEventId":"e2oBYFVEZ5GlPkKSEnkkB","originEventId":null,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","title":"The Tally keys Lantern's air to each person's debt","description":"Every valve on Lantern asks the Tally whose air it is. A balance past the dimming line is dimmed: doors close, rations stop, a lock with an unauthorised occupant goes to recovery.","tags":[],"id":"julz0e6TSybH1t9QZ9cYt","createdAt":1793146473354,"updatedAt":1793146538900,"version":3},{"readerLearnsAtEventId":"QCrXLJWL3UvmPUjnhlrKk","originEventId":null,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","title":"Hold 2 of the Good Title draws cryo-level power","description":"Sealed, certified 'agricultural stock'. The draw is larger than bridge, galley and quarters together: a deep-cold draw.","tags":[],"id":"kjlPwdawmNzSd_o7EtBlt","createdAt":1793146667273,"updatedAt":1793146670627,"version":3},{"readerLearnsAtEventId":"Fb0lH_0HezNb7JbWS3pa9","originEventId":null,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","title":"Agathe Hask's balance carries an error of 2,145 credits","description":"An inheritance surcharge keyed against her whole balance since 299 AC and then compounded. Corrected, she is above the Calling line.","tags":[],"id":"rkf2FOCQTRqek96XpCxKk","createdAt":1793146627185,"updatedAt":1793146630511,"version":3},{"readerLearnsAtEventId":"zP8QlkeX6-5VlVkvPkC8A","originEventId":null,"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","title":"The Caul Account has no holder and was authorised by a key the Tally does not know","description":"First root entry, forty-ninth day of the Closing, 2387: creditor CAUL, all balances assigned in full, account holder NONE. Not the Founders' paired mark.","tags":[],"id":"v1mIcHZInE4ZrFlaP0hJn","createdAt":1793146644633,"updatedAt":1793146647978,"version":3}],"knowledgeReveals":[{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","factId":"Yzu619FSHx3dp2iajWWTX","characterId":"B5N-ORRxzDAoRSjwt4RPw","eventId":"e2oBYFVEZ5GlPkKSEnkkB","note":"","beforeStory":true,"id":"0SYOxVOunqmZVzsoc0Wi9","createdAt":1793146610401,"updatedAt":1793146610401,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","factId":"3wVyv-0D4jJwyJJDIn8UC","characterId":"8FkLsgdUH52stzy3qThJO","eventId":"yZpgnpjBHSlNECwKUqvZs","note":"","id":"0qjuQI_XCGXjTT3QF2cF2","createdAt":1793146622928,"updatedAt":1793146622928,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","factId":"julz0e6TSybH1t9QZ9cYt","characterId":"qBQ4QH8OQcPou62jFFMxx","eventId":"e2oBYFVEZ5GlPkKSEnkkB","note":"","beforeStory":true,"id":"5R5HVcCAAOSJv2Wg7cPcW","createdAt":1793146541383,"updatedAt":1793146541383,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","factId":"v1mIcHZInE4ZrFlaP0hJn","characterId":"K2sa5OCdLdT81u7a1yBt0","eventId":"f_ufE9D3tqhoILeyNqngm","note":"","beforeStory":true,"id":"9lH5NQWKOhK-_5JhMjJfV","createdAt":1793146652913,"updatedAt":1793146652913,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","factId":"VHM8xuN0PzEmciuV2N9c7","characterId":"B5N-ORRxzDAoRSjwt4RPw","eventId":"e2oBYFVEZ5GlPkKSEnkkB","note":"","beforeStory":true,"id":"9nNmESVvpeaNvLG_Rt3hs","createdAt":1793146595380,"updatedAt":1793146595380,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","factId":"3wVyv-0D4jJwyJJDIn8UC","characterId":"vOBEkx7GmTDi_KO4NiWCN","eventId":"yZpgnpjBHSlNECwKUqvZs","note":"","id":"ADT2lmlyg_IghqsYSQ8L8","createdAt":1793146625396,"updatedAt":1793146625396,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","factId":"kjlPwdawmNzSd_o7EtBlt","characterId":"QFvVlO7B_a1YmFMbnd84l","eventId":"ssvpvVWG-PUr4fVLVNAEq","note":"","beforeStory":true,"id":"EdbDLcC7zzOww9TT9KxdH","createdAt":1793146673111,"updatedAt":1793146673111,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","factId":"rkf2FOCQTRqek96XpCxKk","characterId":"LNs4_mqC3zge5HTKXOYFD","eventId":"zNinaqTK9rtYqND3n_Ppb","note":"","id":"HjD6cqIMjQmyh41Eo2gpc","createdAt":1793146640378,"updatedAt":1793146640378,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","factId":"IuIH_ap5aCjp_TsGZT4Xy","characterId":"xBl28xa-sqvXJ5tgbrGuh","eventId":"a8a4Mt67-JHW0f3gQMomY","note":"","id":"Ka1mAecTbLprWfcP4OwWr","createdAt":1793146582832,"updatedAt":1793146582832,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","factId":"X86sJFI6tMtII3GPAxjQB","characterId":"TRqcAjx0xfYg8Pc46bEt2","eventId":"ssvpvVWG-PUr4fVLVNAEq","note":"","beforeStory":true,"id":"LKv3hwT4wLMwUxDs9YH6p","createdAt":1793146665496,"updatedAt":1793146665496,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","factId":"julz0e6TSybH1t9QZ9cYt","characterId":"B5N-ORRxzDAoRSjwt4RPw","eventId":"e2oBYFVEZ5GlPkKSEnkkB","note":"","beforeStory":true,"id":"MO2w1svu02CXncn1ydam8","createdAt":1793146546316,"updatedAt":1793146546316,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","factId":"VHM8xuN0PzEmciuV2N9c7","characterId":"4PJK2irFlLq0lPkroCgPG","eventId":"e2oBYFVEZ5GlPkKSEnkkB","note":"","beforeStory":true,"id":"MniLjFFakIXYIbNckuCDH","createdAt":1793146592913,"updatedAt":1793146592913,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","factId":"kjlPwdawmNzSd_o7EtBlt","characterId":"EEp34P_LIwiHnQUS72F_S","eventId":"QCrXLJWL3UvmPUjnhlrKk","note":"","id":"NgDWz3LD_YodEJJGXHDe6","createdAt":1793146675559,"updatedAt":1793146675559,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","factId":"rkf2FOCQTRqek96XpCxKk","characterId":"8FkLsgdUH52stzy3qThJO","eventId":"zNinaqTK9rtYqND3n_Ppb","note":"","id":"VLlkkxVAZHn0BhjJSndBn","createdAt":1793146637911,"updatedAt":1793146637911,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","factId":"3wVyv-0D4jJwyJJDIn8UC","characterId":"K2sa5OCdLdT81u7a1yBt0","eventId":"f_ufE9D3tqhoILeyNqngm","note":"","beforeStory":true,"id":"WQOuXeKOUTdX2syI6dOrm","createdAt":1793146618014,"updatedAt":1793146618014,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","factId":"IuIH_ap5aCjp_TsGZT4Xy","characterId":"qBQ4QH8OQcPou62jFFMxx","eventId":"a8a4Mt67-JHW0f3gQMomY","note":"","id":"dPgcEZCL6BIxDs9jBFm4g","createdAt":1793146580364,"updatedAt":1793146580364,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","factId":"rkf2FOCQTRqek96XpCxKk","characterId":"vOBEkx7GmTDi_KO4NiWCN","eventId":"zNinaqTK9rtYqND3n_Ppb","note":"","id":"dTre4QnykSt1MoDDNNFrU","createdAt":1793146635445,"updatedAt":1793146635445,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","factId":"julz0e6TSybH1t9QZ9cYt","characterId":"4PJK2irFlLq0lPkroCgPG","eventId":"e2oBYFVEZ5GlPkKSEnkkB","note":"","beforeStory":true,"id":"iqjvNBA-qXsrFZIPeY8i7","createdAt":1793146543852,"updatedAt":1793146543852,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","factId":"IuIH_ap5aCjp_TsGZT4Xy","characterId":"B5N-ORRxzDAoRSjwt4RPw","eventId":"e2oBYFVEZ5GlPkKSEnkkB","note":"","beforeStory":true,"id":"iyW5Y3IP-Tcq4XMkI6PEY","createdAt":1793146575447,"updatedAt":1793146575447,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","factId":"rkf2FOCQTRqek96XpCxKk","characterId":"DdyKkgZFy6btLNqFcDspr","eventId":"Fb0lH_0HezNb7JbWS3pa9","note":"","id":"izT0oE8wqiuQHxV6sJn3j","createdAt":1793146632979,"updatedAt":1793146632979,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","factId":"v1mIcHZInE4ZrFlaP0hJn","characterId":"DdyKkgZFy6btLNqFcDspr","eventId":"zP8QlkeX6-5VlVkvPkC8A","note":"","id":"j_-IFdrBa9SZYbrAc5InK","createdAt":1793146650427,"updatedAt":1793146650427,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","factId":"Yzu619FSHx3dp2iajWWTX","characterId":"qBQ4QH8OQcPou62jFFMxx","eventId":"HndU3cXz3axOEEAjEX_H4","note":"","id":"kJhtmGz_AUOuxxfihUIlZ","createdAt":1793146607913,"updatedAt":1793146607913,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","factId":"X86sJFI6tMtII3GPAxjQB","characterId":"L41M_slYybVBTVVnpviK7","eventId":"ssvpvVWG-PUr4fVLVNAEq","note":"","beforeStory":true,"id":"lQk-BmVYllOCoH8hmTcfc","createdAt":1793146660561,"updatedAt":1793146660561,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","factId":"VHM8xuN0PzEmciuV2N9c7","characterId":"qBQ4QH8OQcPou62jFFMxx","eventId":"aV3K3iPiIvRKl7xKEj1nU","note":"","id":"mMd_bLTelPeMdR8IqAF3j","createdAt":1793146597830,"updatedAt":1793146597830,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","factId":"IuIH_ap5aCjp_TsGZT4Xy","characterId":"g_pQemPTYKrSyuBXSXecB","eventId":"a8a4Mt67-JHW0f3gQMomY","note":"","id":"nN7T1MT6zIH0803VfYD-n","createdAt":1793146585297,"updatedAt":1793146585297,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","factId":"Yzu619FSHx3dp2iajWWTX","characterId":"CaEdi_nGwv_Yo9J6gmQoC","eventId":"HndU3cXz3axOEEAjEX_H4","note":"","id":"plkSGU07qcRW4KNmSyn-p","createdAt":1793146605446,"updatedAt":1793146605446,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","factId":"X86sJFI6tMtII3GPAxjQB","characterId":"EEp34P_LIwiHnQUS72F_S","eventId":"jCCT8EN2Vj2l-O2J-Y878","note":"","id":"r_LrrudxAcxGN4KiPG51_","createdAt":1793146663010,"updatedAt":1793146663010,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","factId":"IuIH_ap5aCjp_TsGZT4Xy","characterId":"4PJK2irFlLq0lPkroCgPG","eventId":"e2oBYFVEZ5GlPkKSEnkkB","note":"","beforeStory":true,"id":"vUfq1ioeawt3Xm2A9i5Hc","createdAt":1793146577915,"updatedAt":1793146577915,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","factId":"kjlPwdawmNzSd_o7EtBlt","characterId":"TRqcAjx0xfYg8Pc46bEt2","eventId":"ssvpvVWG-PUr4fVLVNAEq","note":"","beforeStory":true,"id":"vnw0Mkjss-6abcMaSINQH","createdAt":1793146678044,"updatedAt":1793146678044,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","factId":"rkf2FOCQTRqek96XpCxKk","characterId":"K2sa5OCdLdT81u7a1yBt0","eventId":"8jiGFknV0ZrBQVyPMlNAI","note":"","id":"xXHnAkBE06Rg3lz67dyAz","createdAt":1793146642844,"updatedAt":1793146642844,"version":1},{"worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","factId":"3wVyv-0D4jJwyJJDIn8UC","characterId":"X1JRrGlyZdp8QBMZ6SYZN","eventId":"f_ufE9D3tqhoILeyNqngm","note":"","beforeStory":true,"id":"yiosdbi9xqTWcLjmqW7MM","createdAt":1793146620480,"updatedAt":1793146620480,"version":1}],"characterGoals":[],"sceneTexts":[{"id":"-qlQf96aWs4eLrFtQ5dtU","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"f_ufE9D3tqhoILeyNqngm","text":"The light ended at the Lip.\n\nIt ended the way a tide ends on a beach Tamsin Hask had seen once in a picture-book: not in a line, but in a long uncertain fringe, where the terraces were half lit and half not and the frost began to show on the north faces of things. Above the Lip the Wick still burned, a bar of white fire down the middle of the sky, and the Galleries climbed the walls of the world in their thousands, warm, stinking, lit. Below it — aft of it, the old people said, because the Founders had called it aft — the lamp simply stopped. The last two kilometres of Lantern's axis were a dark rod, and under that dark rod lay the Cold Wards, and nobody had lived in the Cold Wards for longer than anybody had been alive.\n\nTamsin clipped her tether to the Lip post, an iron spike hammered into the road by some cutter a century dead, and checked the other end on her harness. Forty metres of line. Then the reel at her hip, two hundred more. Then her own good sense, which was free.\n\n\"Going in,\" she said to nobody. Habit. Her mother's habit, from the pumps: you said the thing out loud so the air knew you were in it.\n\nShe pulled the mask down. The rig's heater hummed along her spine. She walked into the dark.\n\nIt was not quite dark. That was the first thing you learned. The Wick's light carried a long way along the curve of the rock, bouncing from terrace to terrace, so that for the first few hundred metres the Cold was a blue dusk, and only further in, past the old Pump Galleries, did it become the true black you needed the lamp on your brow for. The second thing you learned was that the Cold was not empty. People had lived here once. Their houses were still here, frost-furred, doors standing open; their washing still hung on lines between terraces, frozen into boards. There were tables with cups on them. There was a child's bicycle, which Tamsin passed every trip, leaning against a wall with its front wheel turned as if somebody had just stepped off it. She didn't touch it. Cutters didn't touch the small things. You took metal, and you left the rest to the dark.\n\nShe was working a bus-bar in the old Pump Gallery Fourteen, a fat run of copper as thick as her wrist that fed a dead scrubber bank, and she had been working it for six days. Copper was the best thing in the Cold. It was what the Count's foundries paid for, and what the Salt paid more for, and for a cutter with a family balance like hers it was the difference between a door that opened and one that didn't. The bar ran forty metres along the gallery ceiling and she had got eleven of it out. Today she thought she might get four more.\n\nThe air in the Pump Galleries was thin and cold enough to hurt through the mask. She could hear her own breath, loud, and the tick of the heater, and the scritch of the saw when she started it, and under all of that the deep slow grumble of the rock itself, which never stopped, which was the sound of Lantern turning. Frost fell from the bar where she cut it and drifted down in the light of her brow-lamp, sideways, the way things fell on Lantern, curving as they went.\n\nShe was on her third metre when the light went.\n\nNot her light. The other one. Even here, a kilometre into the Cold, there was a faint wash of it in the gallery mouth behind her, and she saw it go: blue to grey to nothing, and then, after a breath, back. And then out again. And back.\n\nTamsin turned off the saw and stood with her heart going.\n\nThe gutter, people called it. Everybody had seen it by now. Three times in the last month, five the month before. The Wick faltering for a second or two, so that all over Lantern the light stammered and every person on every terrace looked up together, mouths open, the way you'd look up at a sound in the night. Then it would steady and everybody would look down again and go on as if they hadn't.\n\nIt steadied. The dusk came back into the gallery mouth. Tamsin let her breath out through her teeth.\n\n\"Not today,\" she said. \"Not with me in here.\"\n\nShe started the saw again.","wordCount":773,"createdAt":1793145782613,"updatedAt":1793145782613},{"id":"0GGVKyxYs2iRt6TgXxZEa","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"yZpgnpjBHSlNECwKUqvZs","text":"The crier came out onto the gallery above the inner lock as the square was breaking up, and the square stopped breaking up.\n\nHe wasn't a crier, though. Tamsin saw that straight off, and felt the crowd see it too, a ripple going back through it like wind through a strip-farm. Criers were boys in grey with a slate and a loud voice. This was a man of about sixty in a long coat of a grey so dark it was nearly black, with silver at the cuffs, and behind him on the gallery were half a dozen men in the ordinary grey of the Count's bailiffs, with their docket-batons through their belts, and at their head a big pale man Tamsin knew by sight and by reputation: Brother Lusk, the Bailiff of the Count, who had walked more families into the dimming than anybody alive.\n\nThe man in the dark coat did not need a slate. When he spoke his voice was soft, and it went everywhere.\n\n\"Owing and owed,\" he said.\n\n\"Owing and owed,\" said the square, raggedly.\n\n\"My name is Imre Vosk. I am the Comptroller-General of the Count, and I keep the Account for all of you, as my predecessors have kept it since the Closing. I have come down to the Hook myself today because I have something to say that ought not to be said by a boy with a slate.\n\n\"You have seen the gutter.\" He let that sit. \"You have all seen it. I won't insult you by pretending you haven't. The Wick is failing. The fuel the Founders saved from the Closing was meant to last three hundred years, and it has, and a little more. The Wickwrights tell me it will not last a great deal longer. They tell me that at the present length of the lamp, the last cassette will be spent before the end of this year.\"\n\nThe square made no sound at all. Tamsin felt her mother's shoulder go rigid under her hand.\n\n\"The Count has known this was coming,\" Vosk said, \"and has made such provision as it can. From the first of next month the Wick will be shortened by a further third. The Galleries below Fifteen will go to half-light. The strip-farms will be consolidated in the Upper Wards, where the light is surest. That will buy us time. It will not buy us enough. We have too many mouths and too much air to warm and not enough fire to warm it with. And so the Count has taken a decision which no Count has taken since the Closing, and which I take with grief.\n\n\"The Account will be called.\"\n\nTamsin did not know what that meant. She knew, from the sound the square made, that some of the old people did.\n\n\"The ten thousand accounts on Lantern which stand deepest in debt to Caul,\" Vosk said, \"will be released from the Tally's charge. The Tally will no longer supply their air, or their water, or their heat. Those so released may go into the Cold Wards, where the Count will provide such shelter as the Cold allows, and they will owe nothing more from that day, for the Account will hold them quit. The lists will be posted at the Hook in thirty days, by family. I am told there will be those who call this cruelty.\" He looked out over them, and his voice did not change at all. \"I call it arithmetic. If we do not do this, all of us go dark together by the spring. If we do, most of us live. Caul is owed, and Caul will be paid. Owing and owed.\"\n\nHe turned and went back through the gallery door, and the bailiffs closed it after him, and Brother Lusk stood for a moment longer, looking down over the square, before he followed.\n\nNobody said \"owing and owed.\" For a long time nobody said anything.\n\nThen somebody at the back threw something at the closed door — a shoe, Tamsin thought, it was hard to see — and it struck the door and dropped onto the gallery, and the square began to move again, and the noise came up all at once, a hundred people talking and two or three screaming and one old woman somewhere singing the Hook song in a cracked, steady voice.\n\nTamsin looked down at her mother.\n\nAgathe had her chit out. She was holding it to the light, the way they all were, all over the square, a thousand people turning a thousand small metal tokens in their fingers and reading the numbers on them, as if the numbers might have changed in the last ten minutes. Agathe's balance was minus eleven thousand two hundred and six. Tamsin knew that without looking. She'd known it every day for two years.\n\nShe knew, too, without asking anybody, where minus eleven thousand stood in the ten thousand deepest accounts on Lantern.\n\n\"Mam,\" she said.\n\n\"I heard him.\" Agathe put the chit back inside her collar. Her hands were steady. She coughed, and wiped her mouth, and looked up at the empty Hook. \"Get me home, Tam. And then go and find your brother. He'll have heard, and he'll do something stupid.\"\n\nTamsin got her home. Then she stood in the Hask room in the sideways light with the copper still on the floor and the tin on the shelf, and she did not go and find her brother. She thought about a hatch in the Cold, welded shut, with H-A on it, and a dead woman holding a shoe.\n\nAFT STORE 3. The Company had run, and taken everything. That was the story.\n\nBut you didn't weld a door shut on an empty room.\n","wordCount":962,"createdAt":1793145781888,"updatedAt":1793145781888},{"id":"0WN4EPJ8TGw_5OYehRqcl","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"1Gnk6Ky4HPW4rSVah45re","text":"The throat-watch station was where the Company's charts said it would be, which was the first thing in the Sabra system that had been.\n\nIt hung forty thousand kilometres sunward of the Throat, in the slow orbit the Company's engineers had given it seven hundred years ago, a squat grey cylinder the size of a house with a ring of dishes round one end and a long boom at the other carrying instruments nobody aboard the _Good Title_ could name. It was dark. Its dishes were pointed, still, at the place where the Throat had been and was now again, a pink bruise on the sky behind it. Frost had grown on it from somewhere — outgassing, Tol said, from its own seals, over centuries — so that it looked, in the ship's floodlights, like something that had been left out overnight in a garden.\n\n\"It's an asset of the Concern,\" Haste had said, at breakfast, in the voice of a man reading from a list, \"and its records may be of value in establishing the circumstances of the Closing. Mr. Saal will go across. Miss Adaire will go with him, since she reads the formats. Captain, you'll lend them your engineer.\"\n\n\"I'll lend them my engineer,\" Ode had said, \"and I'll want him back.\"\n\nSo the three of them went across, in suits, on a line from the _Good Title_'s lock, with the dark between them and the station and the stars very still. Corvin had done vacuum work on the colony circuit, a little, the way adjusters did — boarding hulks to tag them, walking frozen domes. He did not like it. He liked it less with the bruise of the Throat over his shoulder, breathing.\n\nTol got the station's lock open with a pry-bar and a good deal of muttering. Inside there was no air. There was a short corridor, very narrow, lined with lockers whose doors had sprung open over the years, and at the end of it a round room with a ring of consoles and a single chair.\n\nThere was a man in the chair.\n\nHe had been dead a very long time. The cold and the vacuum had kept him, after a fashion: he was dry and dark and sunken, his face drawn back from his teeth, his hands shrunk to claws on the arms of the chair. He wore a Company coverall with the two leaning letters on the breast, and a name-strip that said ROACH, and he was sitting facing the main console, where a single dead screen looked back at him. On the console in front of him there was a cup, a paper logbook, and a pen.\n\nNobody said anything for a while. Their breath sounded very loud in the suits.\n\n\"He stayed,\" said Fen at last. \"At his post. To the end.\"\n\n\"Or he was left at it,\" said Corvin.\n\nShe looked at him through her faceplate. Then she looked away.\n\nTol went to the console banks and started opening panels, muttering to the machines in the way he did. Fen went to the logbook. She did not touch the dead man; she reached past him, very carefully, as if he might wake, and slid the book out from under his hand. Its pages were brittle with cold. She turned them with the tip of a gloved finger.\n\n\"Readings,\" she said. \"Every six hours. Columns of figures. I don't know what they mean.\" She turned more pages. \"Then notes. Personal. He's lonely. He writes about a woman called Hennie on Lantern. Something about her garden.\" Another page. \"Then— it changes.\" Her voice changed with it. \"The columns stop. Just notes. _Asked the Concern again for confirmation of the closing-phase figures. No reply._ And then a few days later, _No reply._ And then _No reply_ again. And then nothing for a long time, and then just a line.\"\n\n\"What does it say?\"\n\nFen held the book up so that his helmet lamp fell on the page. The hand was a scrawl, the letters crowding, as if written in the dark or in a hurry or by someone very cold.\n\n_They told me someone would come for me._\n\nThat was all. Under it the page was blank, and all the pages after.\n\n\"Right,\" said Tol, from somewhere inside a console with only his boots showing. \"I've got the core. It's intact. Cold kept it. But it's locked six ways to Sunday with Company keys, and I'm no codebreaker.\"\n\n\"I am,\" said Fen. \"Bring it.\"\n\nThey took the core and the logbook. They left the man. Corvin stood a moment longer than the others in the round room, looking at him in his chair, with his dead screen and his cup and his name-strip, ROACH, and thought about four hundred and twelve settlements and the faces on them, and a water-office on Kessane; and then, because nobody else was going to, he reached out and closed what was left of the man's eyelids, which did not close.\n\n\"Corvin,\" said Fen, from the corridor.\n\n\"Coming.\"","wordCount":843,"createdAt":1793147214202,"updatedAt":1793147214202},{"id":"2-oFICcm3rVbfqBigPnaD","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"1lGH_mKoG5xQJ7hU97kcj","text":"The _Magpie_ came in an hour later with eighty tonnes of Caul ice in her tank-frame and a crack in her forward window that had been there so long it had a name. Her pilot came through the inner lock with his helmet under his arm and the whole of the Dock Ring's gossip already on his face.\n\n\"Who?\"\n\n\"Adeyemi. Gallery Ten.\"\n\n\"Lock?\"\n\n\"Lock.\"\n\nKasimir Ostrova closed his eyes for a moment. He was nine years younger than his sister and twice as handsome and he owed money, at a conservative estimate, to a third of the Dock Ring, none of which ever seemed to cost him a night's sleep. But he had grown up in Gallery Six the same as her, and he had seen the same locks.\n\n\"Uncle Kas,\" said Pim, and was swept up and hung upside down by the ankles, which at twelve he was too old for and permitted anyway.\n\n\"How's the run?\" Dagny said.\n\n\"Cold. Long. The usual.\" Kas set Pim the right way up. \"Quiet out there, though. Quieter than it was.\"\n\n\"Quiet how?\"\n\n\"Caul Deep's still shut. You know they closed the Deep in the spring — the big shaft, the one that took nine hundred on a shift. Nobody's working it. Beacon's dark, gantries are up. Funny to fly past. Used to be you'd see the lights of it from a thousand klicks out like a city.\" He shrugged. \"They said the vein ran out. Sent all the Deep crews home through the Throat. Contracts bought out, every one. Lucky bastards.\"\n\n\"I never heard of the Company buying out a contract in my life,\" said Dagny.\n\n\"Well. You never heard of a vein running out, neither. It's a season of firsts.\" Kas grinned at Pim. \"Come on. I'll buy you a noodle. I've been paid.\"\n\n\"You've been paid and you owe Marguerite at the noodle stall forty.\"\n\n\"Then I'll buy him a noodle and her a smile, and we'll all be square.\" He put an arm round his sister's shoulders and squeezed, briefly, hard. \"Sorry about Lemuel, Dag.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" she said. \"Me too.\"\n\nBehind them, Odile's engraver whined on in the bronze.","wordCount":361,"createdAt":1793145334541,"updatedAt":1793145334541},{"id":"26-fGTDae4YWzu4MY7mjj","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"Fb0lH_0HezNb7JbWS3pa9","text":"Hask, Agathe. Gallery Twenty-Two. Born 270 AC. Pump-hand, thirty years; retired on sickness, 309. Dependants: Hask, Jory, fifteen. Adult child on separate account: Hask, Tamsin, cutter.\n\nBalance: minus eleven thousand two hundred and six.\n\nHe began, as he had been taught, at the bottom. Agathe Hask's own consumption, year by year, since she got her chit at six: air, water, heat, light, housing. Ordinary. A little heavy in the last six years, which would be the lung-rot. Her own earnings against it: thirty years of pump wages, posted monthly, regular as a heartbeat, and then nothing. The difference, by itself, came to minus three thousand and forty. That was a life. That was what it cost to be Agathe Hask.\n\nThen the inheritances. Her mother's balance, posted to her at her mother's death in 299: minus four thousand one hundred. Her father's, posted to her mother before that, and carried through. A brother's. A child's, a baby, two months old, minus nine. He paused over that one, as he always did over the small ones, and then went on.\n\nAnd then, every year, on Account Day, the inheritance surcharge.\n\nThe surcharge was older than the Count. It had been in the Tally from the Founding, a levy on all inherited balances of three parts in a hundred, posted once a year, because — the counters were taught — an inherited debt was a debt that had gone unpaid for a whole lifetime, and the Account must be compensated for its patience. Orrin had calculated it ten thousand times. You took the inherited portion of the balance on Account Day and you multiplied it by three in a hundred and you posted the result.\n\nThe inherited portion. Not the whole balance.\n\nHe looked at the figure posted on Account Day 310 for Hask, Agathe. Then he looked at the one for 309. Then 308. He took a fresh page on his slate and did the arithmetic by hand, the way he had done it as a boy, with the stylus scratching.\n\nIt was wrong. It had been wrong for eleven years. Some time around 299, when her mother's balance came to her, somebody — some counter, at some desk, in some hall — had keyed the surcharge against the whole of Agathe Hask's balance instead of the inherited part. And then, each year after, the surcharge itself had been treated as inherited, because it had been posted under the inheritance heading, and had been surcharged in turn. Three parts in a hundred, compounding, on a figure that had never been owed.\n\nHe worked it through to the present hour. It took him most of the morning. When he was done he sat back and looked at it.\n\nCorrected balance: minus nine thousand and sixty-one.\n\nTwo thousand one hundred and forty-five credits. A little over two thousand credits that Agathe Hask had never owed, that her daughter had been cutting copper in the Cold to pay down, that would have gone on her son's chit at her death and on his children's after him.\n\nOrrin turned to the list from the Comptroller-General's office and ran his stylus down it until he found the cut: the ten-thousandth account, the shallowest that would be Called. It was minus nine thousand four hundred and twelve.\n\nHe sat very still. A counter at the next desk glanced at him, and away.\n\nAt minus eleven thousand two hundred, Agathe Hask went into the Cold. At minus nine thousand and sixty-one, she did not.","wordCount":586,"createdAt":1793146319523,"updatedAt":1793146319523},{"id":"3RY4dhCUAJu9biFZn9raH","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"GEjYVGHSrB_a_j4oPocox","text":"The Company let it go for two days. On the third, Varga came up the Wick.\n\nHe came at shift-change, which was clever: the moment when there were most crews on the spine and least of them clipped on. He came up all four spoke-shafts at once with forty men in grey, in pressure armour, with stunners and with slug-guns slung across their backs, and he came fast. Dagny was at the master ring with the crews when the alarms on the spoke-hatches went, one after another, like bells, and she had time to shout once — \"_Clip on! Everybody clip on!_\" — before the first of them was out of the shaft and onto the crawl and coming hand over hand toward the collimators.\n\nThere was no fighting near the axis. Not real fighting. Nobody had weight there; you couldn't swing a punch, you couldn't plant your feet. What there was, was a long slow struggle of bodies on the rails, crews clinging to their trim wheels and Varga's men prising them off, one finger at a time, and the stunners going with their flat ugly crack, and men and women going limp on their tethers and floating out from the crawl like weed in water. It was almost quiet. That was what she remembered afterwards. How quiet it was.\n\nShe saw it happen to Joss because she was looking at him. He was on the middle ring, a hundred metres along, with two of his crew; he had his big arms wrapped round the housing of the twentieth collimator and his face turned toward her, and he was grinning, the mad grin he had when a job went bad and there was nothing to do but enjoy it. A man in grey was coming along the crawl toward him. Joss let go of the housing with one arm and swung at him — a stupid, slow, weightless swing that would never have connected — and the man in grey, startled, did what he had been trained to do with a target that moved. He fired.\n\nNot the stunner. The slug-gun.\n\nThe shot made almost no sound in the thin air at the axis. Joss jerked once. Then he let go of the housing. His tether had not been clipped — he had unclipped it to swing — and he simply drifted out, away from the crawl, away from the lamp, turning very slowly, with his arms spread and his big body loose, into the open air at the centre of the world.\n\nDagny screamed his name. She did not remember doing it. She remembered the sound coming back to her off the curve of the rock.\n\nAt the axis, a body with no weight can drift for a long time. Then the spin finds it. Joss drifted out from the Wick for nearly a minute, so slowly that she thought — she let herself think — that somebody would reach him, that a crew would get a line to him, that he would come back. Then the slow invisible hand of the rock's turning took hold of him, and he began, very gently at first, to fall. Not down. Out. Toward the curving wall of the world, toward the terraces of the Galleries, which were spinning round underneath him at four hundred kilometres an hour and which he would meet, when he met them, at that speed.\n\nShe made herself watch. Someone had to. He fell for a long time, getting smaller, turning, through the twilight. She lost him against the terraces of Gallery Nineteen. She did not see him land. She saw, a long way down, a sudden small stir among the people on the terrace there, like a stone thrown into a pond.\n\nWhen she turned round, Hale Varga was on the master ring beside her, clipped on, in his pressure armour with the faceplate up. His face was grey. He was looking where she had looked.\n\n\"I didn't order that,\" he said.\n\n\"You brought guns onto the Wick.\"\n\n\"I didn't order it.\" He turned his head and looked at her, and there was something in his face she had not expected and did not want: a sort of sickness. \"Bring the lamp up, Ostrova. Please. Before anybody else goes off it.\"\n\nShe looked past him. All along the spine his men held the collimators now, their crews stunned on their tethers or pinned against the rails. Down at the far end of the master ring a man in grey had his arm across the throat of a boy, holding him clipped to the rail, and the boy was Pim.\n\n\"Let him go,\" she said.\n\n\"Bring the lamp up.\"\n\nShe brought the lamp up.","wordCount":788,"createdAt":1793146964188,"updatedAt":1793146964188},{"id":"4SKY7CAiMFblSt4xNwxw8","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"l3mB0Z32LXliG1ks4H2N4","text":"Joss sat on the landing with the torch in his lap and wept the way big men did on Lantern, quietly and with his face turned to the wall. Dagny did not weep. She went to the girl, whose name was Abi and who was Lemuel's sister's child, and she knelt so that their faces were level, and she looked at the chit.\n\nMinus nine hundred and four, plus the girl's own, which was the ordinary minus three hundred that a Lantern child had by eight from being fed and housed and breathed. Minus twelve hundred and four. Three hundred over the line. The girl's door would be dimmed by morning. Her ration would stop at the next posting.\n\n\"You've got people on Ten?\"\n\n\"My aunt's at the farms. She's on nights.\"\n\n\"Go to her. Tell her the Wick crews will stand it. Tell her Dagny Ostrova said. Tomorrow I'll put it through a counter.\"\n\nThe girl looked at her with the flat, measuring look of every Lantern child who had already learned that adults said things. Then she went.\n\n\"You haven't got twelve hundred,\" said Joss, when she'd gone.\n\n\"The crews have.\"\n\n\"The crews have got a hole in the bottom of the bucket same as you, Dag.\"\n\n\"Then we'll stand it with the hole.\" She stood. Her knees hurt. Her balance, in the corner of her own eye, was smaller by two hundred for a door that had not saved anyone. \"Get him covered. I'll take his name to Odile.\"","wordCount":252,"createdAt":1793145334702,"updatedAt":1793145334702},{"id":"60KHzXuQh2ewF3FhCDCJa","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"e2oBYFVEZ5GlPkKSEnkkB","text":"The call came down the spine at the end of second shift, when Dagny Ostrova was forty metres up the Wick with her face in a collimator housing and her knees locked round a rung that had been warm for ninety years.\n\n\"Gallery Nine,\" said the speaker on her collar. It was Joss, below her on the crawl, and his voice had the flatness it got when something was already too late and he hadn't decided to know it yet. \"Lock Nine-Forty. Man in it. It's gone to recovery.\"\n\nShe was moving before he finished. You did not think about a lock in recovery. You thought about it afterwards, at the bell, with a drink in you.\n\nGoing down the Wick was going down only in the sense that the rock's spin pulled you, gently at first and then less gently, toward the curved floor of the world. At the axis there was no weight at all, only the lamp: three kilometres of magnetic bottle and shielding and slow white fire, with the crawls and catwalks wrapped round it like ivy round a pole. Its light fell on everything. It fell on the terraced Galleries that climbed the inner wall in every direction, on the water tanks and the strip-farms and the tin roofs, on the long seam of the Upper Wards where the Company people lived with real glass in their windows. On Lantern you could look up from anywhere and see someone's house above you, upside down, lit from the side by the same lamp that lit yours. Children were taught early not to drop things from high terraces. It was the first rule. The second was to watch your balance.\n\nDagny dropped through the spoke-shaft hand over hand, letting her weight build, and hit the Gallery Nine landing at a run with Joss three seconds behind her, wheezing. A crowd had already made a half-ring round Lock Nine-Forty. Nobody was touching it. On Lantern, nobody touched a lock in recovery, because the Tally logged who touched what.\n\nThe lock was a steel bottle three metres long, set between the Gallery Nine pressure zone and the Gallery Ten one, so that if either breached the other would hold. You walked into it, the door behind you sealed, the Tally read the chit on your wrist, and if you were in credit the door in front of you opened. If you were not, it didn't. And because the Company had built Lantern on the principle that air was the dearest thing in the Sabra system and must be recovered wherever it could be, a lock with an unauthorised occupant began, after ninety seconds, to draw its air back into the main for reclamation.\n\nLemuel Adeyemi was in the lock. Dagny knew him a little. Pump-fitter, Gallery Ten. Fifties. A cough. A wife who had died two winters back of the same cough, and whose name was on the bell. He was standing with both palms flat on the inner door's window, and his mouth was open, and his breath was making less fog on the glass than it should have.\n\n\"How long?\" Dagny said.\n\n\"Two minutes in recovery,\" somebody said. \"His shift got docked. Pump fault on Ten, they docked the whole crew a half-day. It posted on the hour. He was already in the lock when it posted.\"\n\nDagny looked at the panel by the door. The little screen showed his number and his balance, in the Company's blue, and the balance was in brackets, which was how the Tally wrote a debt it had stopped being patient with. Minus nine hundred and four. The dimming line on Lantern was minus nine hundred.\n\nFour. He was four over the line. Four was a meal. Four was an hour of water.\n\n\"Transfer,\" she said, and slapped her own chit onto the panel. \"I'll stand it. Transfer from Ostrova, nine-oh-six-two-two, to—\"\n\nThe panel chimed politely. TRANSFERS BETWEEN ACCOUNTS POST AT THE HOUR. PLEASE SEE A COUNTER.\n\n\"There's no counter on Nine at shift-end,\" Joss said behind her. \"There's no counter on Nine ever.\"\n\n\"Then get me the override.\"\n\n\"Dag. The override's in the Counting House.\"\n\nShe knew that. Everyone knew that. She said it anyway because it was the next thing to say, and because the alternative was to stand and watch, and the crowd was already standing and watching. Inside the lock Lemuel had seen her. He took one hand off the glass and made a small shape with it, a sort of apology, as if he were sorry to be making a scene.\n\n\"Torch,\" said Dagny.\n\n\"It's a pressure door. It'll take—\"\n\n\"Torch, Joss.\"\n\nHe had one on his belt, because Wick crews carried everything. She lit it and put the flame to the hinge side of the outer door and the steel went cherry-red and then white and began, very slowly, to run. The crowd made a noise behind her. The panel spoke again, in the same tone: TAMPERING WITH COMPANY PRESSURE EQUIPMENT IS CHARGED AT TWO HUNDRED CREDITS PER INCIDENT TO THE ACCOUNT OF THE TAMPERER. It read her chit through her glove. She watched her own balance drop by two hundred in the corner of the screen and kept cutting.\n\nIt took four minutes. A man can live for four minutes in a lock in recovery; Dagny had seen it. What a man cannot do is live for four minutes in a lock in recovery after he has already been in it for three. By the time the hinge gave and Joss got a pry-bar into the gap and the two of them hauled the door back on its seal, there was nothing in the lock but cold, thin air, rushing out past them into the gallery with a sound like a long breath let go, and Lemuel Adeyemi on his knees with his forehead against the inner door, as though he had decided at the end to pray to it.\n\nDagny went in and turned him over. His eyes were open. His lips were the colour of the shadow under a terrace. She put two fingers on his neck because she had been trained to and because people were watching, and then she took them away.\n\nBehind her the panel on the wall chimed a third time. Lemuel's number went grey, and under it, small and blue and patient, the Tally posted his last entry: ACCOUNT CLOSED BY DEATH. BALANCE TRANSFERRED TO ESTATE. And then, because Lemuel had no estate but a cough and a bunk and a wife's name on a bell, it posted the next one, which Dagny had seen a hundred times and which never stopped being the worst thing she had ever read: ESTATE INSOLVENT. BALANCE TRANSFERRED TO NEXT OF KIN.\n\n\"Who's his kin?\" she said.\n\nNobody answered. Then a girl at the front of the crowd, eight or nine, with a chit on a cord round her neck and her hair cut short against lice, said, \"Me.\"","wordCount":1168,"createdAt":1793145333989,"updatedAt":1793145334699},{"id":"64NSSgZJVJ3sO6V_XulGW","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"QCrXLJWL3UvmPUjnhlrKk","text":"Tol Brask found him two hours later, in the narrow corridor outside the engine spaces, where Corvin had gone to be somewhere that was not his cabin.\n\n\"You're the adjuster,\" said Tol. It was the first thing he had said directly to Corvin since Ambry.\n\n\"I am.\"\n\n\"You can read a power board?\"\n\n\"After a fashion.\"\n\nTol jerked his head at the wall panel beside him, where a grid of little lights and figures showed what each section of the ship was drawing from the engine. He put one long greasy finger on a line near the bottom. HOLD 2 — SEALED. \"Read that.\"\n\nCorvin read it. He read it twice. The figure was larger than the figure for the bridge, the galley, and all the crew quarters together.\n\n\"That's a lot of power for seed,\" he said.\n\n\"That's a lot of power for anything,\" said Tol. \"That's not a seed draw. I've hauled seed. Seed sits in a cold box and sips. That's a deep-cold draw. That's a cryo draw. That's the kind of draw you get from something you've got to keep at four degrees above nothing every second of every day, or it spoils.\" He took his finger off the board. \"Captain doesn't want to know. Captain's got a mortgage. I'm not asking you to do anything. I'm just telling you what the board says. I tell the captain what the board says, every day, and now I've told you.\"\n\n\"Why me?\"\n\nTol looked at him with his flat, absorbed, engineer's eyes.\n\n\"Because you're the one who's going to stand in front of them,\" he said, \"and explain.\"\n\nHe went back into the engine spaces and shut the door. Corvin stood in the corridor for a long time with his hand on the cold wall, feeling through it the deep steady thrum of the _Good Title_'s great engine, driving them in toward the planet and the rock behind it; and, under that, so faint he could not be certain it was there at all, a second, lower note, steady as breath, coming up from somewhere aft.\n","wordCount":349,"createdAt":1793146447057,"updatedAt":1793146447057},{"id":"7yD-i3iL0x6ny-qhuzU08","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"a8a4Mt67-JHW0f3gQMomY","text":"The announcement came at eleven the next morning, on every screen on Lantern at once.\n\nDagny was in the canteen on Gallery Six with Joss and two of the Wick crew, trying to work out how many hours the Speakers could find between them to stand the Adeyemi girl's debt, and getting a number that was not enough. The screen on the canteen wall was usually showing either the Company's safety notices or a football match from somewhere in the Concord that had been played eleven years before and brought through the Throat on a data-run. It went dark in the middle of a goal, and then it showed the Company's mark, the two letters H and A leaning on each other like tired men, and then it showed Ruth Achterberg.\n\nThe canteen went quiet in the way Lantern went quiet for the bell.\n\nThe Director was sitting at a desk with nothing on it. She was a lean, grey woman of sixty in a dark Company suit, and she wore at her throat, on a fine chain, the brass disk of the Director's seal, which every child on Lantern had been told was the key to everything. She did not look cruel. She looked like someone who had been awake all night doing arithmetic and had come out of it with an answer she disliked but would not apologise for.\n\n\"People of the Sabra Concern,\" she said. \"I am speaking to you today with news that affects every one of us.\n\n\"As you know, the Throat which connects this system to the Concord is not a fixed thing. It breathes. Its throat-watch station has measured that breathing for the whole of this colony's history. Three weeks ago the station recorded the beginning of what our physicists call a closing phase. Throats which enter a closing phase do not reopen on any timescale that is useful to us. The best estimate, from the Concord's own literature, is somewhere between six and eight hundred years.\n\n\"The Throat will remain passable for fifty-two days. After that, this system will be cut off.\"\n\nSomebody in the canteen laughed, the high bark of a person who has been hit. Nobody else made a sound.\n\n\"The Halden-Ash Company has a duty to its shareholders and to its staff,\" said the Director. \"It cannot maintain a concern which it cannot reach. It will therefore withdraw from the Sabra system before the Throat closes. The carrier _Covenant_, now at our fore dock, will carry essential staff and their families and such Company assets as can be moved. I have asked the department heads to prepare those lists.\n\n\"I want to speak directly to the indentured population of Lantern.\" She looked into the lens. Dagny would remember that afterwards: that she did not look away. \"Your contracts are held by the Company. The Company does not abandon what it holds. Your contracts will remain in force, in trust, for the full term of the closure, and will be honoured when the Throat reopens. Lantern's systems — its air, its water, its heat, its light — will continue to be administered by the Tally, which has kept you safe for ninety years and which will keep you safe after we are gone. The Tally does not need us. It needs only that you go on working, as you always have, and it will go on keeping you alive.\n\n\"I know that this is hard news. I wish that I had better. Thank you.\"\n\nThe screen went back to the Company's mark. Then, after a moment, to the football match, which had been paused at the instant before the goal and now went on as if nothing had happened, the ball crossing the line, the crowd eleven years ago and four hundred light-years away going wild.","wordCount":636,"createdAt":1793145334407,"updatedAt":1793145334407},{"id":"9-Q4kGXvWVVoJ8uFn00A9","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"OOyL9VKdWLI45rhbQCJWL","text":"They took her to the Counting House afterwards, to the long window, and the Director told her what it would cost.\n\nEvery Speaker who had voted for the strike: five hundred credits, posted to their accounts at the hour, for the loss of three days' light to the Company's farms. Every crewman who had held a collimator: two hundred. The farms' lost harvest, which was the Company's property, apportioned across every account on the rock according to its share of the light. When the Tally had finished posting, four hundred and eleven doors on Lantern had dimmed that had not been dimmed the day before. Dagny's was one of them.\n\n\"I'm sorry about Mr. Teague,\" said Ruth Achterberg. She stood at her window with her back to the room, looking up the length of the Wick, which blazed again white and hard from end to end as if nothing had happened. \"I did not want anyone hurt. Mr. Varga has disciplined the man who fired. I want you to understand that I have nothing against you, Ms. Ostrova. You were doing what your people asked. I'm doing what mine ask. In thirty-five days we'll both be relieved of it.\"\n\n\"You could take the line out,\" said Dagny. Her voice came out cracked, as if it belonged to someone else. \"Today. Go down to the root and take it out. Nobody dimmed again. What does it cost you? You'll be gone.\"\n\nThe Director turned from the window.\n\n\"It costs a debt that nobody has to pay,\" she said. \"And a debt that nobody has to pay is not a debt. It's a story. The Company does not hold stories in trust, Ms. Ostrova. It holds assets. You have a son, I understand. When he is grown, and the Throat opens, and somebody comes to ask what this place is worth, I'd like there to be an answer. That's all the line is for.\" She sat down at her bare desk. \"Mr. Varga will see you down.\"\n\nVarga saw her down. In the lift he stood with his faceplate under his arm and said nothing at all until the doors opened on the spine-road at the foot of the Wick. Then, as she stepped out, he said, low, not looking at her:\n\n\"His name's Corrie. The man who shot your friend. He's twenty-two. He's from Gallery Eleven.\"\n\n\"Is that supposed to make it better?\"\n\n\"No,\" said Varga. \"It's supposed to make you know who did it. Somebody ought to.\" And the doors closed.","wordCount":421,"createdAt":1793146964099,"updatedAt":1793146964099},{"id":"AR67Ky_IPldSnIbHUB8xH","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"oYcFtv3TJm27ejEkl1NBR","text":"Orrin Sallow had been told by the Comptroller-General himself to finish his four hundred, sign his sightings, and stop puzzling over the root. He finished his four hundred. He signed his sightings. Then he went back into the archive, because once a counter has seen a number that will not sit right he cannot stop seeing it, and because he had begun to understand, in the days since he had stood in the Hask room, that he was going to be ashamed of himself either way and would rather be ashamed of something he had done.\n\nHe did not go back into the root. He was not that brave, yet. He went into the light.\n\nEvery account on the Tally was charged for light, as for air and water and heat. Every counter knew the rate. What Orrin had never looked at, because no counter ever had any reason to look at it, was the other side of the light ledger: not what the Owing were charged for the Wick, but what the Wick was given. Fuel in, light out. The Wickwrights reported to the Count every quarter how much of the last cassette was spent, and the Count posted it, and the posting went into a ledger that nobody read.\n\nHe read it. He read three hundred years of it in two nights, sitting late in the empty third hall with the lamps turned down to save the light he was reading about.\n\nIt was very simple. At the Closing the Founders had held a stock of cassettes for the Wick. They had burned them, one at a time, through three centuries, shortening the lamp each time a cassette ran low, as the Count had always said. The stock was nearly gone. The last cassette was in the Wick now, and Ninian the Wickwright's quarterly report put it at a fifth remaining. That was the gutter. That was what the Comptroller-General had said at the Hook. All true.\n\nAnd then, at the very bottom of the ledger, under the long column of spent cassettes, there was a second line that had not changed in three hundred years. A single entry, carried forward every quarter, posted on the Founders' authority in the first year after the Closing, and then never touched again.\n\nRESERVE — HULL 7731. 14 UNITS. HELD.\n\nFourteen units.\n\nHe did not know what Hull 7731 was. He had never heard the words. They were not in the catechism, or the Account Day tours, or the rolls of the Count's property. He searched the archive for them and found them nowhere else at all.\n\nBut he knew what a unit was, in the light ledger. Every line above it said so. A unit was a cassette.\n\nHe sat in the dark hall for a long time, with the faint hum of the Tally coming up through the floor — or a pump, or the Hulk, or nothing — and thought about fourteen cassettes, held, for three hundred years, while the Wick was shortened and shortened over the Galleries and the Cold crept up from the aft end of the world. He thought about ten thousand accounts on a list. He thought about a woman on Twenty-Two asking him who Caul was.\n\nThen he wrote _Hull 7731_ on the back of his hand, in ink, with the stylus that a boy had stolen from him and given back for cheese, and went home.\n","wordCount":574,"createdAt":1793147102910,"updatedAt":1793147102910},{"id":"C_kc-bUDgOhcRPYwGjBiB","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"dI0ozeV8Sj6bMOF5593cX","text":"The counter came back three days later, which Tamsin had not expected, and he came in plain clothes, which she had expected even less.\n\nShe almost didn't know him. Without the grey coat he was only a thin young man with soft hands and a clean face and hair cut the way they cut it in the Upper Wards, short at the sides, and he stood in the doorway of the Hask room holding a cloth bag against his chest as if it might be taken off him, which on Twenty-Two it might.\n\n\"I didn't use the chit,\" he said, before anybody could speak. \"On the door. I knocked. I wanted you to know that.\"\n\n\"Door was open,\" said Tamsin.\n\n\"I knocked anyway.\"\n\nAgathe was asleep, or pretending to be; she slept a great deal now, propped on her bunk with her mouth a little open and her breath whistling. Jory was on the top bunk with his knees up, watching the counter with bright interested eyes. Tamsin was mending her rig's heater-line at the table with a soldering pen and a borrowed light.\n\n\"Well?\" she said.\n\nHe came in and shut the door behind him, and stood there.\n\n\"The correction was refused,\" he said.\n\nShe put the soldering pen down.\n\n\"Not refused. Deferred. Until after the lists are posted.\" He was speaking quickly, looking at the floor. \"The Count doesn't allow corrections during a Calling. In case— so that the line holds. I went to the Comptroller-General himself. He says he'll approve it, afterwards. He gave me his word.\"\n\n\"After the lists are posted,\" said Tamsin, \"she'll be in the Cold.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"So what's his word worth? Down there?\"\n\n\"I don't know,\" said Orrin Sallow. He looked up at her. \"That's why I came.\"\n\nShe didn't understand him for a moment. Then she did, and something tightened in her chest that she did not let reach her face.\n\n\"You came to tell us,\" she said. \"That's all. You came down twenty galleries in your second-best clothes to tell my mother she's going into the dark with two thousand credits on her that aren't hers, so you'd feel better about it.\"\n\n\"No. I came because it isn't right, and I can't make it right, and I didn't want you to hear it from a list.\" He held out the cloth bag. \"And I brought food. It's not much. It's what my mother keeps in the house. Bread. Some cheese. Real cheese. I didn't know what you— I'm sorry.\"\n\nTamsin looked at the bag and did not take it. On the top bunk, Jory sat up.\n\n\"He can stay,\" Jory said. \"If he's got cheese.\"\n\n\"Jory.\"\n\n\"I'm only saying.\" Her brother swung his legs down, dropped lightly to the floor, and held out his hand to the counter, palm up. In it was a stylus. A good one, Upper Wards make, with a silver clip. \"Here. You dropped it.\"\n\nOrrin looked at the stylus. Then at Jory. Then, unexpectedly, he laughed — a short, startled sound, as though it had been surprised out of him. \"I didn't drop it.\"\n\n\"No,\" Jory agreed. \"But you can have it back. For the cheese.\"\n\nOrrin took the stylus. Tamsin took the bag. Nobody said anything for a while. On the bunk, Agathe Hask's breath went on whistling in and out, in and out, each breath posting, somewhere far above them in the Count's vault, to an account that was two thousand credits deeper than it should have been.\n\n\"Why does it matter to you?\" Tamsin said at last. \"Really. There's ten thousand on that list. You'll have four hundred of them on your slate. Some of them must be wrong too. Why us?\"\n\nHe thought about it. She watched him think about it, and was annoyed to find she believed him when he answered.\n\n\"Because I checked yours,\" he said. \"I haven't finished the others.\"","wordCount":651,"createdAt":1793147103279,"updatedAt":1793147103279},{"id":"Cfo-zPrSNtWDC2aJnnECs","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"zP8QlkeX6-5VlVkvPkC8A","text":"The correction came back in an hour, with a line through it in red and a note in the margin in the cramped hand of the senior counter of the third hall: _Surcharges are not corrected during a Calling. Refile after the lists are posted. — A._\n\nOrrin read the note three times. After the lists were posted the Haskes would be in the Cold. After the lists were posted there would be no account to correct.\n\nHe sat at his desk while the hall emptied around him for the dinner bell. He was not hungry. He was angry, and he was not used to being angry, and he found he did not know what to do with it except work. So he did what a counter does when a number will not sit right: he went back to the beginning.\n\nThe inheritance surcharge had been in the Tally from the Founding. Every counter knew that. But nobody had ever told him where in the Tally it lived, or who had put it there, or what it had been written to do. He had never needed to know. Now he wanted to. He wanted to see, written down, the rule that said three parts in a hundred, and see whether it said _inherited portion_ or _whole_. He wanted to know whether the error was a counter's, or the Tally's own.\n\nThe Count's archive of the Tally's root entries was open to counters of the fourth rank and above. He was fourth rank. He had never opened it. Hardly anyone did; it was a long scroll of old rules, rate changes, adjustments to the dimming line, all of it three hundred years dead. He found the surcharge rule after half an hour of searching. It was where it should be, in the first year after the Closing, posted on the Founders' authority. It said _inherited portion_. It was not the Tally's error. Somebody in a ledger hall had simply keyed it wrong, eleven years ago, and nobody had looked since.\n\nHe should have stopped there. He nearly did. But the archive was open on his slate and he had never seen the very first page of it, and so — with the same small guilty pleasure as putting his hand on the Hulk at seven — he scrolled to the top.\n\nThe first entry in the root of the Tally of Lantern, as kept by the Count, was dated the forty-ninth day of the Closing, in 2387 of the old reckoning. It was very short. It established the Account.\n\nACCOUNT ESTABLISHED. CREDITOR: CAUL. ALL BALANCES ON THE TALLY ASSIGNED TO CREDITOR IN FULL. ACCOUNT HOLDER: NONE.\n\nAnd under it, where every other root entry he had seen carried the same small paired mark — the two authorities of the Founders, the twin keys of the root, side by side — there was something different. Not the paired mark. A single line of the Tally's own plain text, which he had never seen it use about anything:\n\nAUTHORISED BY A KEY NOT KNOWN TO THE TALLY.\n\nHe read it several times. He thought about what _account holder: none_ meant, in a ledger. Every account had a holder. Somebody's chit, somebody's number. An account with no holder was money owed to nobody: a debt that could be paid into for ever and drawn from by no one.\n\nHe was still looking at it when the hall door opened and a bailiff in grey said, \"Counter Sallow? The Comptroller-General asks for you. Now, please.\"","wordCount":590,"createdAt":1793146319397,"updatedAt":1793146319397},{"id":"DNTgKNQZREfnDH9I5YPDF","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"Dm2K6ci-33HjzjQtCq2bL","text":"The copper went for ninety at the Salt Docks, which was thirty more than the Count's foundry would have given and thirty less than it was worth, and Tamsin took it because she had to.\n\nThe fence was a man called Brannock who worked out of a dead hauler's cabin at the end of Spur Four, aft along the ring where the Count's bailiffs did not come without a reason. He weighed the copper on a beam scale older than the Closing and counted out the ninety in Salt tokens, which were brass washers stamped with a crude S and worth exactly what the Salt said they were worth and nothing anywhere else on the rock. Tamsin put them in the inside pocket of her rig. She would change them at a chit-shop on Nineteen that took Salt washers at a quarter off, and the quarter off would go to the Salt too, because everything on the Salt Docks ended up going to the Salt in the end.\n\n\"Somebody wants a word,\" said Brannock, not looking up from his scale.\n\n\"Who?\"\n\nHe jerked his head toward the back of the cabin, where a curtain made of an old cargo-net hung over a doorway. Tamsin looked at it for a long moment. Then she went through.\n\nThe room behind was bigger than the cabin, cut back into the dock wall, and warm, warmer than any room Tamsin had been in all year. There was a stove with real coal-cake in it. There was a rug. There was a table with a lamp on it and a pot of something that smelled of meat. And at the table, eating out of the pot with a spoon, was a woman of sixty with one eye and a patch over the other, and grey hair cropped to a burr, and arms like a hauler's.\n\nEverybody on Lantern knew who Wenna Blackwood was. Nobody below Fifteen had seen her and lived to boast of it. That was the story, anyway. Tamsin had always assumed it was the Salt's own story, put about by the Salt.\n\n\"Sit,\" said Wenna Blackwood. Her voice was a gravel chute. \"Eat, if you want. It's goat. Real goat. Off the farms.\"\n\nTamsin sat. She didn't eat.\n\n\"You're Agathe Hask's girl,\" said Wenna. \"Cutter. Good cutter, Brannock says. Clean metal, no lies about the weight.\" She chewed, swallowed. \"And you've found a door.\"\n\nTamsin went cold.\n\n\"The Cold talks,\" said Wenna, not unkindly. \"Not much. But I've got ears in it. Three cutters work the Fourteen run besides you, and one of them saw your light on the service way under the old pump bank, and saw where it stopped, and came and sold me that for a bottle.\" She put the spoon down. \"Company door. Welded. Letters on it. That right?\"\n\nThere was no point lying. \"Aft Store 3.\"\n\nWenna nodded slowly, as if Tamsin had confirmed something she already knew. \"Company stores,\" she said. \"They had a dozen, aft, in the old days. Fuel and parts and medicine. They emptied them when they ran. Everybody knows that. Every one of them's been cut open and gutted three times over in three hundred years.\" She leaned forward. \"Except that one. Never found it. Never even knew it was there. Know why?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Because somebody welded it shut and then iced over the way in, that's why. Somebody wanted it forgotten.\" Her one eye was very bright in the lamplight. \"What's behind a door like that, cutter? Eh? What would the Company not take when it ran — and not want anybody else to have?\"\n\n\"I don't know.\"\n\n\"No. Nor do I. But I'd pay to find out.\" Wenna Blackwood picked up her spoon again. \"Half of whatever's in it. You cut the door, my people carry. I'll stand your tools and your air and your mother's tin besides. Think about it.\"\n\nTamsin stood up. Her legs were not quite steady; it was the warmth, she told herself, she wasn't used to it.\n\n\"My mother's going to be Called,\" she said. She didn't know why she said it. \"Unless a counter in the Count does what he said he would.\"\n\nWenna looked up at her with no expression at all.\n\n\"Then you'd better think quick,\" she said.\n","wordCount":716,"createdAt":1793146319078,"updatedAt":1793146319078},{"id":"EA6zQDkyLJX7H7Kdb8lJ8","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"zNinaqTK9rtYqND3n_Ppb","text":"A Calling could not be posted on a balance alone. The rules said a counter must also sight the account-holder, in their place of residence, and confirm by their own eyes that the person existed and was where the Tally said. It was an old rule and a kind one, Orrin had always thought: it meant that before the Count did anything to a family, someone from the Count had stood in their room.\n\nHe had never been below Gallery Fifteen before. He was ashamed of that, a little, going down. The lift stopped at Eighteen and he walked the rest, along the terrace roads with his grey coat and his slate, and he felt the light change as he went: the clean white of the Upper Wards going yellow, then grey, then a sort of brownish dusk, sideways, as the Wick fell away behind the curve. The terraces were narrower here and the houses smaller and nearer together, and there was washing hung between them, and the smell was of damp and cooking and people. Children stopped playing and watched him go by. An old man spat, not at him, but not far from him either.\n\nTwenty-Two was the last gallery before the Lip. He found the Hask door by its number. He touched his chit to the panel, the counter's chit, which opened any door on Lantern for the purpose of a sighting, and the door sighed and clunked and let him in.\n\nThere were three people in the room. A woman of fifty or so, sitting up on the lower bunk with a blanket round her, very thin, with pale, almost colourless eyes. A boy on the top bunk, all knees, who looked at Orrin's coat and then at the door and then at Orrin's slate in a way that made Orrin hold the slate a little closer. And standing at the ring-stove with her back to him, a young woman in a cutter's rig with the upper half peeled down and tied round her waist by its arms, who did not turn round.\n\n\"Counter Sallow,\" Orrin said. \"Third hall. I'm here for a sighting. Hask, Agathe?\"\n\n\"That's me,\" said the woman on the bunk. She had a cough; he heard it under her voice, waiting.\n\n\"I'm sorry to disturb you. It won't take a moment.\" He held up the slate and read the particulars from it — name, gallery, birth year, number — and she nodded to each of them, and he marked them. His stylus was not quite steady. \"Thank you.\"\n\n\"That's it?\" said the young woman at the stove. She still had not turned. \"That's the sighting? You come down twenty galleries to look at my mother and say her name, and then she's sighted, and then she can be Called?\"\n\n\"Tam,\" said the woman on the bunk.\n\n\"No, I want to know. I want to know how it works.\" She turned round then. She had the same pale eyes, set in a hard, dirty, angular face that would have been handsome if it had not been so angry. \"Does he get paid by the head? Is there a bonus for the deep ones?\"\n\n\"I'm salaried,\" said Orrin, before he could stop himself.\n\nShe laughed. It was not a nice sound.\n\n\"Counter,\" said Agathe Hask quietly. \"My daughter's had a bad week. So has everybody on this gallery. You'll forgive her. Is there anything else you need?\"\n\nHe should have said no. He had his sighting. He had no business in this room any longer and every reason to leave it. Instead he heard himself say, \"There's an error in your account.\"\n\nThe room went quiet.\n\n\"An error,\" said Tamsin Hask.\n\n\"In the inheritance surcharge. From 299. It was keyed to the whole of your mother's balance instead of the inherited part, and then compounded. It's—\" he looked at the slate, though he knew the figure— \"it's two thousand one hundred and forty-five credits. Over eleven years. I've corrected it on my slate. I'm going to file it today.\"\n\nAgathe Hask looked at him for a long moment.\n\n\"How much does that leave?\" she said.\n\n\"Nine thousand and sixty-one.\"\n\n\"And where's the line? For the Calling?\"\n\nHe should not have told her. The cut was not posted. It would not be posted for another twenty-six days. \"Nine thousand four hundred and twelve,\" he said.\n\nNobody said anything. On the top bunk the boy had sat up.\n\n\"So she's under,\" said Tamsin. \"If you file it. She's under the line.\"\n\n\"If it's accepted. Yes.\"\n\n\"If.\"\n\n\"Corrections are— they have to be approved by a senior counter. It's a formality. The arithmetic is plain. Anyone can check it.\" He held out the slate, as if she might want to, and she looked at it, and at him, and he understood that she could not read the figures on it and that he had just shamed her, and he took it back.\n\n\"Why are you telling us?\" she said.\n\n\"Because it's true.\"\n\n\"Lots of things are true. The Count doesn't come down here and tell us them.\" She folded her arms. \"What do you want?\"\n\n\"Nothing. I— nothing.\" He found that he did not know what to do with his hands. \"It's my job. The Count is just. An account can't be Called in error. That's the rule.\"\n\n\"The Count is just,\" said Tamsin, flatly, the way you'd repeat a word in a language you didn't speak.\n\nAgathe Hask coughed, then, for a long time, bent over the blanket; and her daughter went to her and held her shoulders until it passed, and Orrin stood in the middle of the small room with his slate and looked away, at the shelf over the stove, where there was a biscuit tin with a picture of a farmhouse on its lid, very old, the paint nearly gone.\n\nWhen the coughing stopped, Agathe sat back and wiped her mouth and looked at him again with her pale eyes.\n\n\"Counter Sallow,\" she said. \"Can I ask you something?\"\n\n\"Of course.\"\n\n\"Who is Caul?\"\n\nHe opened his mouth to say the catechism. _Caul is the dark that kept us when the Company left us in it. Caul is owed for every breath, and the Owing pay._ He had said it every Account Day of his life. He found that he did not want to say it here, in this room, to this woman, and he did not know why.\n\n\"Caul is the Account,\" he said at last.\n\n\"That's what I told my girl.\" Agathe nodded slowly. \"She didn't think much of it either.\"\n\nHe left soon after. In the passage outside, as the door clunked shut behind him, he reached into his coat for his stylus to mark the sighting time on his slate, and found the clip empty. He looked back at the door. He thought about knocking. He thought about the boy on the top bunk, all knees and quick eyes.\n\nHe marked the time with his fingernail, and went back up the galleries into the light.","wordCount":1177,"createdAt":1793146319461,"updatedAt":1793146319461},{"id":"GJBOyOuonWKwxo1Jorezw","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"aV3K3iPiIvRKl7xKEj1nU","text":"He came to find her that night on the Wick.\n\nDagny was on the third crawl, two hundred metres up the spine, half inside a coolant junction with her lamp in her teeth, and she heard him long before she saw him: the clank of somebody climbing who did not climb, the scuff of shoes that were not boots, the hard short breathing of a man who has looked down. When she backed out of the junction he was clinging to the crawl rail ten metres below her, white as the lamp, with his eyes shut.\n\n\"You'll want to open them,\" she said. \"It's worse with them shut. You can't see the rungs.\"\n\n\"I— yes. Thank you.\" He opened them. Below him the whole of Lantern hung, lit, at every angle, the far terraces small as stitches. He shut them again. \"No. I— I don't think I can.\"\n\nShe climbed down to him, because the alternative was to leave a Company comptroller to fall off the Wick in the middle of a shift and spend the next week explaining it to Varga. She hooked a spare tether from her belt to his and clipped it to the rail. \"There. You can let go now, you won't go anywhere. Sit.\"\n\nHe sat on the crawl, very slowly, with his knees drawn up and his back to the rail, like a child on a high wall. The light of the lamp lay on him from above, harsh and white, and showed every line on his face. It was the face of somebody who had not slept in a long time.\n\n\"What do you want, Mr. Basu?\"\n\n\"I wanted— I wanted to speak to you where there aren't cameras.\" He gave a small breathless laugh. \"There's only one place on Lantern the Tally doesn't watch. The Wick's too bright for the lenses. Did you know that? I designed the camera net and I couldn't make it see up here. It was the first thing I ever failed at.\"\n\n\"And the second?\"\n\nHe didn't answer that. He looked at his hands.\n\n\"What the Director told you today was true,\" he said. \"The Tally can't be unlocked without stopping it. I built it so. I was twenty-six and very good at my work and very proud, and the Company wanted a system that could not be argued with, and I gave it one. No back door. No override that a frightened counter could be bribed to use. Every valve asks it. It answers. That's all.\" He breathed. \"But it's not quite all.\"\n\nDagny waited.\n\n\"Every system has a root,\" said Teodor Basu. \"The place where the rules are kept, as opposed to the place where they're applied. The Tally's root is in the vault. A console, about so big, behind three doors. From there you can change anything. The dimming line. The rates. Who owes what to whom. Whether there's a line at all.\" He was speaking faster now, the stammer gone, the way some people's went when they were on ground they knew. \"To open the root you need two keys, together, at the same moment. One is the Director's. You've seen it. She wears it round her neck. The other is the Comptroller's.\"\n\n\"Yours.\"\n\n\"Mine.\" He reached down — she watched him, puzzled — and unlaced his left shoe, and took it off, and put two fingers inside it and drew out a thin slip of something pale, like a long fingernail, which he held up to the light. It was ceramic. There were marks burned into it, too fine to read. \"It never leaves me. Not in the bath. Not in bed. It was a condition of my contract.\"\n\n\"And with both of them,\" said Dagny slowly, \"you could—\"\n\n\"With both of them, at the root, a person could do anything with the Tally that the Tally can do. They couldn't switch it off. It isn't built to be switched off. But they could rewrite its book.\" He put the key back in his shoe and laced it again, with great care, his fingers not quite steady. \"I'm not telling you that you can do this. You can't. The Director will be wearing her seal when she walks up the ramp onto the _Covenant_ and the seal will go through the Throat with her. And I will be on the _Covenant_ too. I'm essential staff. I'm on the list.\"\n\n\"Then why are you telling me?\"\n\nHe looked up at her at last. She thought for a moment that he was going to weep; then she saw that he was past that, a long way past it, in some dry country on the other side.\n\n\"Because you stood on a table,\" he said, \"and said that nobody on this rock was going to owe air to an empty office for six hundred years. And I built the office. And I have done other things, Ms. Ostrova, that I can't— that I won't— \" He stopped. He shook his head. \"I wanted one person to know there was a door. Even if it's locked. Even if there's no getting to it. I wanted someone to know it was there.\"\n\nBelow them, very far down, the bell rang once in the Dock Ring. They both stopped, and listened to it come up the spine, faint and sour, and fade.\n\n\"Who was that?\" said Basu.\n\n\"I don't know. I'll know in an hour.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" he said. \"Everybody always knows.\" He unclipped the tether from his belt and held it out to her, and then, when she didn't take it, clipped it back on. \"Would you— could you help me down? I don't think I can do it on my own.\"\n\nShe helped him down. It took forty minutes. At the bottom he thanked her, very formally, and walked away along the spine-road toward the Counting House without looking back; and Dagny stood at the foot of the Wick in the hard white light and thought about a key in a shoe, and a seal on a chain, and what it meant that a man would climb two hundred metres up a lamp he was afraid of to tell her about a door she could not open.","wordCount":1040,"createdAt":1793146166507,"updatedAt":1793146166507},{"id":"Hw2XELmphxsZY927ByVGp","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"EJWW-Mz6p40IDEc_eb7FC","text":"He asked to meet her in the chapel on Gallery Four, at the end of third shift, when the galleries were as empty as they ever were; and when she came in, he was sitting on the front bench under the tea-coloured window with a paper folder on his knees, and he did not get up.\n\n\"Sit down, please,\" said Teodor Basu. \"I'd rather— I'd rather not have to look at you while I say it. If that's all right.\"\n\nShe sat at the other end of the bench. Between them on the crate-wood was the folder. It was thick. Its edges were worn soft, as though it had been carried a long way, or handled a great deal.\n\n\"In the spring,\" said Basu, \"there was an accident at Caul Deep.\"\n\nHe said it to the window. To the ship in the resin, crossing the dark sea toward its lamp.\n\n\"The main shaft. Shaft One. Six hundred metres down through the crust into the old ice. The ice down there isn't like the ice on the surface. It's under pressure. It moves, very slowly, like— like glass in an old window. The shaft engineers had been reporting creep in the lower bracing for two years. Every quarter. Every quarter I took their figures and set them against the budget for re-bracing, and every quarter the re-bracing would have cost the Deep its season's yield, and every quarter the Director decided it could wait, and I agreed with her.\" He drew a breath. \"I agreed with her. I want to be clear about that. It isn't her decision I've come to confess. It's mine. I had the figures. I could have refused to sign the budget. I didn't.\"\n\nDagny did not move.\n\n\"On the fourth of April the lower bracing failed at four hundred and eighty metres,\" Basu said. \"The pressure ice came into the shaft. All of it, all at once. It's— there's a word the engineers used. A blowout. It fills a space the way water fills a bucket, except that it's ice, and it freezes as it comes. Shaft One was at full shift. Nine hundred and four people were below the three-hundred-metre station.\" His voice did not change. \"None of them came up.\"\n\nThe chapel was very quiet. Somewhere far below them a pump thudded, and stopped.\n\n\"Varga's men went down the next day. There were— there were some at the lift station at three hundred metres. Who had got that far. Behind them the shaft was solid. Varga reported that recovery was impossible: it would take a year to cut the ice, and the shaft might go again while they cut. The Director asked me what a year's recovery would cost. I told her. She asked me what it would cost if it were known. In the Concord. The Labour Inspectorate had already fined the Company twice for Caul. A third finding, with nine hundred dead, would end the Concern. Possibly the Company.\" He swallowed. \"So the Director ordered the shaft sealed at the three-hundred-metre station. And she asked me to make the nine hundred go away.\"\n\n\"Go away,\" said Dagny.\n\n\"From the Tally. From the books.\" He put his hand on the folder, flat, as if holding it shut. \"Every person on Lantern is an account. When an account closes, the Tally posts why. Death, or completion of contract. If nine hundred accounts had closed by death in one hour, it would be in every hourly posting that went out to the Concord on the data-runs. So I closed them another way. I posted them as contracts bought out by the Company. Terms completed. Repatriated, through the Throat, on the spring transport.\" He was speaking very precisely now, every word placed. \"I wrote letters, for the ones who had family here. From the Concord. _Arrived safe. Doing well. Will send for you._ I wrote four hundred and thirty-one letters, Ms. Ostrova. I sent them out on one data-run and had them come back in on the next. I copied their handwriting from their payroll signatures.\"\n\nDagny stood up.\n\nShe did not decide to. She was on her feet and then she was in front of him and she had hit him, open-handed, so hard across the face that his head turned and his glasses went skittering off along the bench and onto the floor. He did not put up his hands. He sat there with his face turned away and the mark of her hand coming up red on his cheek, and waited, and when she did not hit him again, he turned his head back and looked at her for the first time, with his naked short-sighted eyes.\n\n\"Yes,\" he said. \"Thank you.\"\n\n\"Don't you _thank_ me—\"\n\n\"I'm sorry. I mean it. Nobody's— in eight months, nobody has done that.\" He bent, slowly, and felt along the floor for his glasses, and found them, and put them on. One lens was cracked. \"There's more. I'm afraid there's more.\"\n\nShe found she was shaking. She sat down again, not on the bench, on the step in front of it, with her back to him, looking at the dark empty chapel.\n\n\"The Inspectorate noticed,\" Basu said behind her. \"Not the deaths. The arrivals. Nine hundred people repatriated through the Throat on the spring transport, and not one of them ever landed anywhere in the Concord. Not one ever registered for work, or drew a pension, or wrote home. It took them a few months to notice. Then it took them a few months more to be sure. The notice came in on the data-run three weeks before the Director's announcement. A Labour Inspectorate fleet, four ships, with a commission. Due through the Throat in a hundred days.\" He paused. \"Three weeks later, the throat-watch recorded the onset of a closing phase.\"\n\nDagny turned round.\n\n\"That's—\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Teodor Basu. \"I thought so too.\"\n\nHe held out the folder. She did not take it. He set it on the step beside her.\n\n\"Those are their names,\" he said. \"All nine hundred and four. From payroll. I've— I've been carrying them. I don't know what else to do with them.\"","wordCount":1032,"createdAt":1793147342225,"updatedAt":1793147342225},{"id":"J8PMU_ckJ9he4h1dPQicc","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"x_sZl2bTW0F7It6IP7uZm","text":"There were eleven ships in the queue at the mouth of the Throat, and the _Good Title_ was seventh.\n\nThe order had been settled at Port Ambry by the harbourmaster's lottery, which every captain had sworn to honour and which every captain, Ode said, would honour exactly as long as it suited them. The queue lay in a loose line along the approach, thirty thousand kilometres out from the mouth, each ship holding station on its neighbour with little flickers of manoeuvring thrust. From the _Good Title_'s bridge they looked like beads on a string. Beyond them was the Throat.\n\nClose to, it was not a patch of wrong sky any longer. It was a wound. Corvin could find no other word for it. A great ragged lens of distortion, thousands of kilometres across, through which the stars on the far side swam and doubled and smeared, and at its heart a pulsing bruise of light, pink going to violet going to a deep hot red, like something seen through closed eyelids. The edges of the lens were never still. They rippled. Ode called them the lips, and the word had made him faintly sick the first time she said it and now seemed simply accurate. They opened and closed. Not much — a few hundred kilometres, the throat-men said, on a period of a few hours — but enough. A ship in the lips when they closed would not be a ship afterwards.\n\n\"First goes at the top of the next open,\" Ode said. She was in the master's chair with her boots on the console, which Corvin had learned was how she sat when she was worried. \"That's the _Pellucid_. Then the _Harrow_, then the _Lady Jane_, then the _Saint Ishmael_. We go on the third open after. Nine hours, give or take.\"\n\n\"And until then?\"\n\n\"Until then we sit here and watch better men than us go first.\" She did not take her eyes off the main screen. \"Tol.\"\n\n\"Captain.\"\n\n\"How's she feel?\"\n\nTol Brask's voice came from somewhere below, flat and absorbed. \"Engine's fine. Engine's always fine. Hull's grumbling on the old Pellan scar. I'll watch it.\"\n\n\"Watch it.\"\n\nHaste had come up from his cabin and was standing at the back of the bridge in a dark coat, quite still, watching the screen with the polite interest of a man at a concert. Corvin found that he disliked that more than anything else about him: that he could look at the Throat as if it were a performance being put on for him, and might be asked for an encore.\n\nThe _Pellucid_ went at the top of the open. Corvin watched her go: a long grey freighter, Concord-built, much newer than the _Good Title_, sliding forward on a steady burn into the swimming dark at the centre of the lens. For a while she was a clear shape. Then she was a smear. Then she was a red point that wavered and stretched and was gone.\n\nNobody on the bridge said anything for a minute.\n\n\"She's through,\" said Ode at last. \"Or she's dead. Either way she's out of the queue.\" She took her boots off the console. \"Next.\"","wordCount":534,"createdAt":1793146447526,"updatedAt":1793146447526},{"id":"KM62JgVzaVWBLWMxX3GGc","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"E7gP4pT5_i7yTLiAsRdJE","text":"The Hask room was on Gallery Twenty-Two, which was a low gallery, near the Lip, where the light came in sideways and never quite warmed anything. It was three metres by four. It had a bunk for her mother and a bunk for Jory and a mat for Tamsin, a ring-stove, a water tap with a meter on it, a door that opened when the family was in credit and didn't when it wasn't, and on a shelf over the stove, a tin.\n\nThe tin had held biscuits once, a long time ago, in somebody else's family. Now it held chits. Every chit the Hasks had ever owned was in that tin: Tamsin's grandmother's, her grandfather's, her great-grandmother's, a great-uncle who had died at nineteen in a pump accident, a baby who hadn't got a name. When you died on Lantern your chit came home. That was the law. The Count didn't want it; the Count kept the numbers. The family kept the metal, so that it would remember what it owed.\n\nAgathe Hask was sitting up on her bunk with the tin in her lap when Tamsin came in. She did that sometimes, took the chits out and laid them on the blanket in rows, like a game of patience. Her lips moved while she did it. Tamsin had asked her once what she said, and her mother had said, \"Their names, what do you think,\" and gone on.\n\n\"You're late,\" said Agathe, and coughed.\n\nThe cough was the lung-rot. Everybody below Gallery Fifteen got it in the end, from the damp and the mould and the scrubbers that were older than the Count; it came on slow and stayed for years, and it took you by inches, and the Tally charged you for the extra air. Agathe had had it for six years. The last two she'd been off the pumps, which meant she wasn't earning, which meant every breath she drew went on the balance.\n\n\"Got four metres,\" Tamsin said. She swung the copper down onto the floor and stretched her back till it cracked. \"Good four. Thick.\"\n\n\"Count price or Salt price?\"\n\n\"Salt.\"\n\nHer mother's mouth went thin. Agathe had been a pump-hand for thirty years and a churchgoer for all of them, and she didn't like the Salt, and she didn't like that the Salt was where the money was. But she didn't say anything. She'd stopped saying things about money about the time she'd stopped earning any.\n\n\"Where's Jory?\"\n\n\"Out. Running for somebody. Said he'd be back for the Hook.\"\n\n\"Who died?\"\n\n\"Old Pask. Wim Pask, Twenty-Three.\" Agathe coughed again, longer this time, and when she was done she sat with her eyes closed and her hand flat on her chest as though she were holding something down. \"In his sleep. His door dimmed last week. He'd been going up to sleep on the Hook steps. They found him there this morning.\"\n\nTamsin sat down on her mat. She thought about the woman by the hatch, with the shoe. She didn't say anything about the hatch. Not yet.\n\n\"How much was he over?\"\n\n\"Does it matter?\"\n\n\"It matters to whoever gets it.\"\n\n\"His granddaughter. On Nineteen.\" Agathe opened her eyes. They were very pale, her mother's eyes, almost colourless in the bad light; Tamsin had them, and so did Jory. The Hask eyes, people said, as if it were a family heirloom, which in a way it was. \"She's got two of her own. She'll be under by the summer.\"\n\nThey sat for a bit. The water meter on the tap ticked over, once, as somebody somewhere on the family's account drew a cup. Probably Jory, wherever he was.\n\n\"Mam.\"\n\n\"What.\"\n\n\"Who's Caul?\"\n\nHer mother looked at her as if she had asked what air was.\n\n\"You know who Caul is.\"\n\n\"I know what they say in church. I know it's the Account. Caul is owed and the Owing pay. I know the moon's called Caul and the Salt run ice off it. I mean who.\" She heard her own voice, and it was too loud for the room, and she didn't care. \"Who's the money going _to_, Mam? We've owed Caul for three hundred years. Everybody on this rock owes Caul. Every hour of air, every cup out of that tap. Where does it go? Who's sitting on the other side of the book, getting fat on it?\"\n\n\"Nobody's getting fat.\" Agathe's voice was quiet. \"Caul is owed. That's all. Caul is the dark that kept us. When the Company ran and left us in it, the dark could have taken us, and it didn't. It let us breathe. So we owe it. That's what the Account is. That's what the Count keeps. You know all this.\"\n\n\"That's church.\"\n\n\"That's the truth. Same thing, down here.\" Agathe began to put the chits back in the tin, one at a time, in their order. \"You want to know who's on the other side of the book, you go up to the Count and ask them. They've got the book.\"\n\nThe door opened. It made the noise it always made, a hydraulic sigh and a clunk, and the little light by the frame went green, and Jory came in sideways, the way he came in everywhere, as if he were expecting to have to leave in a hurry. He was fifteen and all elbows. He had a cut on his lip that hadn't been there this morning.\n\n\"They're ringing for Pask,\" he said. \"Everyone's going. You coming?\"\n\n\"What happened to your face?\"\n\n\"Ran into something.\" He grinned, and the cut opened, and he licked it. \"There's going to be a crier after. From the Count. Whole of the Hook's full of grey coats. Something's happening.\"\n\nAgathe put the lid on the tin.\n\n\"Help me up,\" she said.","wordCount":972,"createdAt":1793145782271,"updatedAt":1793145782271},{"id":"KVUIs_F21czkRMPLSFd4L","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"5w1fwLh1tZoBJXJuXo19t","text":"There were forty collimators on the Wick, ring after ring of them down the three-kilometre length of the lamp, each a squat grey housing the size of a cupboard that shaped the magnetic bottle at that point and so decided how much light the lamp gave out there. Every Wick crew on Lantern knew how to trim one. You did it every shift, a little this way, a little that, to keep the light even as the fire inside the bottle wandered. Nobody had ever trimmed all forty at once, together, all the way down.\n\nThey did it at the start of second shift. Dagny took the master ring at the root of the lamp, by the Counting House, where she could see the whole length of the spine. Joss had the middle. Every collimator had a crew on it, two or three to a housing, clipped to the rails in the hard white light, waiting with their hands on the trim wheels and their eyes on her.\n\nShe raised her arm. She lowered it.\n\nThe light went down.\n\nNot fast. The fire inside the bottle did not like to be squeezed, and fought it, and they had to coax it, a quarter-turn at a time, watching the gauges, easing it down by degrees. But it went. Dagny felt it before she saw it — a coolness on the backs of her hands, on her face, as if a cloud had passed over a sun she had never seen. Then the shadows began to lengthen. Then, all along the curving walls of the world, the terraces went from white to gold, and from gold to amber, and from amber to a deep slow blue, like the dusk in the picture-books Pim had read when he was small, from planets, where the sun went down.\n\nIt took twenty minutes. When it was done Lantern lay in twilight, and the Wick hung down the middle of it not as a sword any longer but as a dim, smouldering bar, like a coal blown on and left.\n\nDagny clipped off at the master ring and looked down.\n\nShe had expected noise. Shouting, sirens, the Company screens all lighting up at once. There was nothing. All over the rock, on every terrace, people had come out of their houses and were standing looking up. She could see them. Thousands of them, small and still, faces turned toward the lamp. Nobody spoke. Nobody moved. On Lantern you were taught at five not to look straight at the Wick, because it would burn your eyes; and here were nine thousand people looking straight at it, and it was not burning, and they could see it, for the first time in their lives, as a thing that someone had made.\n\nOn the Gallery Six terrace somebody began to sing. Dagny could not hear the words from so high. It was one of the old crossing songs, she thought, from the tune — one of the ones the _Mercy_'s people had brought, about a ship and a dark sea and a light on the far shore. Another voice took it up. Then a gallery's worth. It went round the curve of the world, terrace to terrace, under the dim red lamp, the way the bell's voice went round the Dock Ring, until it seemed to Dagny that the whole rock was singing, very quietly, to itself.\n\n\"Mum.\"\n\nPim was beside her on the master ring. He was not supposed to be on the Wick at all, let alone at the root of it, but he had come up the spoke-shaft behind the crews and nobody had had the heart to send him down. He was looking at the twilight with his mouth open.\n\n\"It's beautiful,\" he said. \"Why's it beautiful?\"\n\n\"Because it's ours,\" said Dagny, and was surprised to find her throat had closed on it.\n\nBelow them, across the gulf of the dim air, in the Counting House, the long window of the Director's office was a strip of hard white light: the only full-lit thing left on Lantern. While she watched, a figure came and stood in it, small and dark against the light, and stood there, looking up the length of the dimmed Wick toward her, for a long time.","wordCount":715,"createdAt":1793146964256,"updatedAt":1793146964256},{"id":"Kj8mqJBStAIIIn6SYsyDm","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"I271o33fPP2iqfWekZfue","text":"They went in on the third open, and it took eleven hours.\n\nCorvin had read about throat transit. Everybody had. It was in children's books: the long fall, the strange lights, the time that went wrong. He had read, as a boy, that people in transit saw their own lives, or the faces of the dead, or nothing at all; that some came out singing and some came out mad. What none of the books had said was how boring most of it was. The _Good Title_ went into the red and the red closed round her and for hours afterwards there was nothing on the screens but red, a deep dull red that moved slowly, like blood in water, and every so often a shudder through the hull as she rode some invisible swell, and Tol's voice from below saying _fine, fine, she's fine_, as if to himself.\n\nAfter the third hour Ode sent everybody who was not on watch to their bunks. Corvin did not go to his bunk. He went looking for somewhere with a window, and found the observation blister on the ship's upper spine — a dome of old scratched glass with two worn couches under it, built in some optimistic decade when throat-runners had expected to carry tourists — and found that it was already occupied by Fen Adaire and her bell.\n\nTol had stowed the crate there, it turned out, because it was the only space aboard that was not cargo, quarters, or Brightwater's. Fen had opened it. She had the bell out of its foam and sitting upright on the deck, wedged with cargo straps to the couch legs, and she was lying on her back on the deck beside it with a hand-lamp and her loupe, reading it.\n\n\"Sorry,\" said Corvin. \"I'll find somewhere else.\"\n\n\"There isn't anywhere else.\" She didn't look up. \"Sit, if you want. It's a big window.\"\n\nHe sat on one of the couches. Above them, through the glass, the red moved. Every so often something moved in it that might have been a shape and might have been his eyes.\n\n\"Does it bother you?\" he said. \"That?\"\n\n\"The Throat? No. I've been through four.\" She turned the loupe a fraction. \"What bothers me is that I can read six hundred and twelve names on this bell and I don't know who any of them are.\"\n\n\"I thought you said nearly three thousand.\"\n\n\"I said I'd counted nearly three thousand. Counting isn't reading. Most of them are too worn. Or too small. Or cut in a hand I can't make out.\" She sat up, pushing her hair back with the hand that held the lamp. \"There's an order to them, though. That much I've worked out. The oldest are round the lip. They're the crossing — thirty-one names, cut very carefully, all in one hand. Then the colony's dead, year by year, going up the waist. You can see the hand change every twenty or thirty years. A new dockmaster, maybe. Somebody whose job it was. I can date some of them roughly by the style.\" She shifted round, on her knees now, and put her lamp on the shoulder of the bell. \"And up here, at the top, the last hand. Very good. Very steady. Old, I think — there's a little shake on the long strokes. These are the newest. That one—\" she touched a bright line with her fingertip— \"is the newest of all. Or one of them.\"\n\nHe leaned forward. He knew which name it was before he read it.\n\n\"Lemuel Adeyemi,\" he said.\n\nShe looked at him in surprise. \"You saw it at Ambry.\"\n\n\"I saw it at Ambry.\" He did not know how to explain that he had thought about it since. That a name on a bell, cut clean and bright among thousands of worn ones, had followed him onto the ship and into his bunk at night. \"Who was he?\"\n\n\"I've no idea. A person who died on Lantern, in the last years before the Closing. That's all the bell can tell me.\" She sat back on her heels. \"That's the thing about provenance, Mr. Saal. Objects remember things. They just don't remember them in sentences.\"\n\n\"Corvin.\"\n\n\"Corvin.\" She considered him. \"And there's something else. Here.\" She moved the lamp down, to a band low on the waist, below the old weathered names and above the lip, where he had taken the bronze for blank. In the light it was not blank. It was cut, very small, very close, with hundreds of names: a dense grey band of letters, cramped together, so close that they almost touched. \"These aren't in order with the others. They're cut over a blank band that the old dockmasters had left — the bell was cast with a plain belt there, for decoration — and they're in hands I don't see anywhere else on the bell. Three or four of them, I think, taking turns. Fast. Not as careful. Like people in a hurry, or people very tired.\" She looked up at him. \"Hundreds of them. All at once, at the end.\"\n\n\"An epidemic,\" said Corvin. \"A disaster. A fire.\"\n\n\"Maybe.\" She didn't sound convinced. \"If there'd been a disaster, the Company would have filed it. There'd be a record. I've read every page they sent out before the Closing, Corvin. There's no fire. There's no plague. There's nothing.\"\n\nHe said nothing. He was thinking, for no reason he could name, of a line near the end of the Director's statement, about a mine.\n\n\"Why do you want to take it home?\" he said instead. \"Really.\"\n\nFen was quiet for a while. Above them the red moved, and the hull gave one of its long low shudders, and Tol's voice came faint through the deck: _fine, she's fine._\n\n\"Because they're names,\" she said at last. \"Somebody cut every one of these by hand, for three hundred years, so that the people who died on that rock wouldn't just be numbers in a Company's books. And then somebody took the bell away from them. Took it out through the Throat, before the end, and left them with nothing to cut their names on. I don't know who did that, or why. But I've had it all my life, and I've never once felt it was mine.\" She ran her thumb along the dense grey band. \"It's theirs. Whoever's left.\"\n\n\"There may be nobody left.\"\n\n\"Then it can go back to the rock. Back on whatever hook it came off. It'll still be home.\" She looked at him, with the lamp in her lap throwing its light up under her face. \"Your turn. Why are you here?\"\n\n\"I'm paid to be.\"\n\n\"Everybody's paid to be. That isn't what I asked.\"\n\nHe was quiet for a long time. Then, because they were inside a throat, in the long red dark between one universe and the next, where it seemed — the books were right about this much — that ordinary rules had been left on the other side, he told her. The water-office on Kessane. His father's hand shaking on the stylus. Thirty-five years. The discharge in Haste's pocket, wanting only a seal.\n\nShe listened without interrupting. When he was done she said, \"So you're bonded.\"\n\n\"Everybody's bonded.\"\n\n\"I'm not.\"\n\n\"Then you're the first person I've met in ten years who isn't.\"\n\n\"My great-grandmother paid ours off. She sold a painting. It was the only thing we owned worth anything except this.\" She touched the bell. \"She could have sold this instead. A bell off a lost colony ship — there are collectors who'd have paid ten times what the painting fetched. She didn't. She sold the painting and kept the bell, and paid the family out of bond, and died in a rented room.\" Fen smiled a little, not happily. \"We don't really know why. She just said it wasn't hers to sell.\"\n\nThe hull shuddered again, harder. Somewhere below them something metal rang, once, like a struck pipe; and very faintly, as if in answer, the bell under Fen's hand hummed.\n\nThey both looked at it.\n\n\"Sympathetic resonance,\" said Fen, after a moment. \"It does that. It's a bell.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Corvin.\n\nNeither of them looked away from it for some time.","wordCount":1387,"createdAt":1793146447386,"updatedAt":1793146447386},{"id":"Ko7KjHL_zag9-jafdXgpt","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"HUiWV3Gkl-86q7dmQqpTN","text":"They came out of the Throat into a system that did not answer.\n\nThe red thinned and tore and fell away behind them, and there were stars — different stars, a different sky, the shapes of the constellations all broken and rearranged — and a sun. It was not much of a sun. It was a dim orange disc, much smaller than Kessane's, so faint you could look straight at it through the screen filters. And off to one side of it, a little way along the ecliptic, a great banded ball the colour of ochre and old bruises, with a thin hard line of ring drawn across its face.\n\n\"Sabra,\" said Ode. \"And that's Nhai. The gas giant. Your rock's in orbit round it, if it's anywhere.\" She was grey with tiredness; she had not left the chair in eleven hours. \"Comms. What have we got?\"\n\nThe young comm officer had been listening since the red began to thin. He took his headset off and looked at her and then at Haste and then at Corvin, as though he would rather not say it to any of them.\n\n\"Nothing, Captain.\"\n\n\"Nothing on what?\"\n\n\"On anything. I've been through every band in the Company's 2387 tables. Traffic control, haulers, emergency, the Concern's own channels. The throat-watch station's frequency. Then everything else. Nothing.\" He hesitated. \"It's very quiet, Captain. Quieter than empty space usually is. Nobody's even leaking.\"\n\n\"The _Pellucid_? The _Harrow_?\"\n\n\"Both through. I've got their transponders, both heading in-system. But they're not talking either. Not to us.\"\n\n\"Course they're not. They're racing.\" Ode rubbed her eyes. \"And the rock?\"\n\n\"Can't see it.\" The navigator answered this time, an older woman bent over a plot. \"Lantern's orbit puts it on the far side of Nhai from us right now. It'll be behind the planet for days yet. We'll need to come round.\"\n\n\"How long?\"\n\n\"At our best burn? Nine days to clear the limb.\"\n\nNine days. Corvin found himself looking at the banded face of the gas giant, at the hard bright line of the ring across it, and thinking that somewhere behind that, in its shadow, a rock eleven kilometres long had been turning for seven hundred and two years; and that nobody on this ship had the faintest idea whether there was a single living thing inside it.\n\n\"Silence is an answer of a kind,\" said Haste, behind him. He had come up onto the bridge without anyone hearing; he did that. \"It narrows the possibilities. Captain, you'll lay a course for the rock, please, at your best burn. Mr. Saal, I'd like you to begin preparing for both contingencies. A populated habitat and an empty one. I expect you'll need a different speech for each.\"\n\n\"I'll need a different speech for each,\" Corvin agreed.\n\nWhen Haste had gone, Ode looked at Corvin for a long moment, from under her grey brows.\n\n\"Different speech for each,\" she said. \"Mine's the same either way. God help them.\"","wordCount":498,"createdAt":1793146447305,"updatedAt":1793146447305},{"id":"Ltr7YG9UMMt2oeRB9g1tq","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"SJlDN8saqZuYtSVXnhcn1","text":"Kas took her to Caul the next day in the _Magpie_, off the books, on a run he logged as a tank inspection, and for the six hours of the flight he did not make a single joke.\n\nCaul came up out of the dark as a white ball streaked with grey, very bright against the bruise-coloured bulk of Nhai behind it. Close to, it was not white at all. It was a hundred shades of blue and grey and dirty yellow, cracked and ridged and pitted, with long straight scars across it where the haulers' landing-tracks ran and, at the bottom of a vast frozen basin on its leading face, a cluster of gantries and domes and towers that had been, until the spring, the biggest working mine in the Sabra Concern.\n\nIt was dark. Kas had said so, the day Lemuel died. _Used to be you'd see the lights of it from a thousand klicks out like a city._ It looked now like what it was: a town where nobody lived. The landing beacon was dead. The dome lights were dead. Frost had grown on the gantries, long feathers of it, the way frost grew on anything metal that was left alone on Caul. Kas brought the _Magpie_ down on the old pad by hand, slowly, with the floodlights on, and their light went out over a field of abandoned equipment — ice-cutters, haul-sleds, a crawler on its side — and threw long black shadows away across the ice.\n\n\"Shaft One's under the big tower,\" Kas said. \"The lift head. I used to see the men going down in the morning. Shift-change, you'd get a queue of a hundred, two hundred, on the gantry. Waving at the haulers.\" He was looking straight ahead through the cracked window. \"I waved back. Every week.\"\n\nThey suited up. They went across the pad to the tower, and in at the lift-head door, which was not locked — who would come? — and down.\n\nThe lift was dead, so they used the emergency ladder, a cage of steel rungs bolted down the side of the shaft beside the cable runs. It was three hundred metres to the station. Dagny had climbed the Wick every day of her life and she had never climbed anything like it: down and down in the narrow dark with her lamp on the rungs and the walls of the shaft going past, smooth grey ice-crete at first and then, lower, the old ice itself, blue-black and glassy, so clear in places that her light went into it a metre and showed her things frozen there, stones and bubbles and once a dead tool, hanging in the ice as if in air. Kas came down above her. She could hear his breathing on the suit channel, harsh and quick.\n\nAt three hundred metres the ladder ended on a steel platform, and the platform ended in a wall.\n\nIt was ice-crete. Poured, new, grey, filling the whole width of the shaft from side to side like a cork in a bottle, with a heavy steel inspection hatch set in the middle of it and the hatch welded shut. Somebody had stencilled on it, in Company blue, SHAFT CLOSED — NO ENTRY — BY ORDER. Under the stencil, very small, the leaning letters.\n\nBeside the hatch there was a port. A thick round window of armoured glass, the kind they put in pressure walls, at head height, with its own little lamp behind it, dead.\n\nDagny put her light to the glass.\n\nAt first she saw only blue. A deep cold glassy blue, like looking into a lake. Then her eyes understood what they were looking at, and she did not move, and did not breathe, and heard on the channel behind her Kas say a word that was not a word.\n\nThe lift station was full of ice. It had come up the shaft from below and filled the station to the roof and frozen there. And it was full of people.\n\nThey were standing. Most of them were standing. Caught in the act of running toward the lift — toward her — in their mine-suits and helmets, frozen in mid-stride, arms out, heads turned, some of them holding on to each other. The ice was so clear she could see their faces. Some had their eyes open. A woman near the front had her hand up, flat, the palm toward the port, as if she had been reaching for it. Behind her, deeper in the blue, there were more. And more behind those. As far as the light would go, there were people in the ice, crowded, standing, reaching, going back and back into the dark of the shaft until the blue swallowed them.\n\nAnd on the inner face of the hatch — on the inside, where the ice had not quite reached, in a pocket of clear dark space between the frozen people and the steel — there were marks. Long pale gouges in the paint. Five of them. Then five more. Like fingers dragged down a door.\n\nDagny looked at them for a long time.\n\n\"They poured the seal,\" she said. Her voice on the channel sounded very far away. \"With some of them still on this side of the ice. Still alive. They poured it, and welded the hatch, and went up.\"\n\nKas said nothing. She heard him breathing.\n\n\"Count them,\" she said.\n\n\"Dag—\"\n\n\"Count them. The ones you can see. I'll count too. Then we'll put them together.\"\n\nThey counted. It took a long time. The faces in the ice went back further than either of them could see, and they lost count, and began again, and lost it again. In the end Dagny said \"That's enough,\" and Kas said \"I got a hundred and twelve,\" and she said \"A hundred and nine,\" and they stood together on the steel platform in the dark with their lamps on the port and did not say anything else for a while.\n\nThen Dagny took the folder out of her suit's thigh pocket, where she had carried it all the way down — Teodor's paper folder, with nine hundred and four names in it — and held it up flat against the armoured glass, so that the woman with her palm raised could see it, if she could see anything. She did not know why. It was a stupid thing to do. She held it there anyway, until her arm ached.\n\n\"I'll bring them up,\" she said to the glass. \"Your names. I promise you. I'll bring your names up.\"\n\nOn the way back, in the _Magpie_'s cabin, with the white ball of Caul shrinking behind them against the planet, Kas said the only thing he said on the whole flight home.\n\n\"Every week,\" he said. \"I flew past them every week, and waved.\"","wordCount":1147,"createdAt":1793147342154,"updatedAt":1793147342154},{"id":"OeBzEZ_3vXNpt_vM9hAbb","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"A84nmiWcv6-wINQJ3D7BS","text":"The bell of the _Ardent Mercy_ hung at the inner lock of the Dock Ring, on a hook of black iron that had been drilled into the rock the year Lantern was first pressurised. It was not large. It came up to Dagny's hip. It had been cast in the shipyards at Tannet Reach for a colony carrier that had crossed the Throat ninety-one years before, and the carrier had been cut up for her hull plate within a decade of arrival, as such ships always were; but the bell had been kept, because it was bad luck to melt a ship's bell, and because somebody on that first crossing had started the habit of ringing it for the dead.\n\nThere had been thirty-one dead on the crossing. Their names were cut round the lip in a neat, old-fashioned hand. After them came Lantern's dead, in a hand that changed every generation as the dockmasters did, round the lip and up the waist and onto the shoulder and in among the decoration on the crown, so that the bronze was now more letter than metal. From the length of the lock you could not see the names. You could only see that the bell was a strange colour, a dull brown furred all over with scratches, like an old animal.\n\nOdile Marchbank was waiting for her. She had heard; everyone on Lantern heard everything inside an hour, it was the one thing the rock did faster than the Tally. She was seventy-four and three-quarters deaf and she had kept the bell for thirty years, and her hands, which shook when she held a cup, went still as stone when they held the engraver.\n\n\"Adeyemi,\" Dagny said. \"Lemuel. Ten.\"\n\nOdile nodded. She didn't need the spelling; she had cut his wife. She took the bell-rope, which was a length of braided hauler cable, and she rang the bell once.\n\nIt was not a loud sound. Lantern swallowed noise; the rock was too big and too full of air and machinery. But the bell had a voice that went a long way anyway, low and a little sour, and all along the Dock Ring people stopped what they were doing at the sound of it. Hauler crews in the gantries. Customs men at their desks. The women who ran the noodle-stalls along the ring road. They stopped and stood, a breath's worth, and then went on.\n\nDagny felt a small hand slide into hers. She didn't look down.\n\n\"You're supposed to be at school,\" she said.\n\n\"School finished,\" said Pim. \"Everyone came out when they heard the lock. Mister Okonkwo said it was a teaching moment.\" He was twelve, and thin, and he collected bolts, and he had her husband's eyebrows, which she had never got used to. \"Was it Mister Adeyemi from Ten?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Did you try?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe thought about that. \"How much was he over?\"\n\n\"Four.\"\n\nPim did not say anything else. He held her hand while Odile fitted the engraver's burr and bent to the bronze, and the thin whine of it went up into the ring, and the first letters of Lemuel's name came up bright on the shoulder of the bell, among nine decades of brown.\n\n\"I'm running out of bell,\" Odile said, without looking up. She said it every few months. It was a sort of joke. \"One day I'll have to start on the hook.\"","wordCount":571,"createdAt":1793145334628,"updatedAt":1793145334628},{"id":"P0AKUbdYVw210MOCdIvNz","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"A8Kl-HQYWOi3_j0Cw9n3V","text":"He went down to Twenty-Two in the morning without changing his clothes, with grey paint dust still in the creases of his trousers, and when Tamsin Hask opened the door to him he said, before she could speak: \"There's fuel.\"\n\nShe looked at him. Then she stood aside and let him in and shut the door.\n\nAgathe was asleep. Jory was not there. Tamsin pulled out the room's one chair for him and sat on her mat on the floor, cross-legged, in her undersuit with her hair tied back, and listened while he told her. He told it badly, in the wrong order, the ledger and then the floor and then the ledger again and then the Wickwrights' order, and she did not interrupt him once. When he had finished she sat for a while looking at her hands.\n\nThen she reached inside her undersuit and took out a folded square of yellowed plastic and held it out to him.\n\nHe unfolded it. A Company docket, its paper brown with age. He read it, and read it again. _Cassettes 1–212 to Wick store, Spine Road. For the lamp. Store to be sealed on completion._ The stamp. The neat small signature.\n\n\"Where did you get this?\"\n\n\"Aft Store 3. In the Cold. Behind a welded door.\" She told him about the door, and the empty racks, and — after a moment, not looking at him — about the corner behind the last rack, and the wall. He did not know what to say to that. Nobody would have. She did not seem to need him to say anything.\n\n\"Two hundred and twelve,\" she said. \"That's where the Wick's fuel came from. Those two hundred and twelve, and whatever was in the other stores. The Company put them in the Wick store for the lamp before it ran. Didn't it?\"\n\n\"I think so. Yes.\"\n\n\"And your Count's been burning them down for three hundred years, and shortening the lamp, and shortening it, and telling us it was all there was.\" She took the docket back and folded it, carefully, along its old creases. \"And it's got fourteen more under its floor.\"\n\n\"In reserve,\" said Orrin. \"Yes.\"\n\n\"For the Upper Wards.\"\n\n\"For an auxiliary lamp. In the Upper Wards. When the Wick goes out.\" He made himself say the rest of it. \"For about four thousand people. The Count. The counting families. The farms they've moved up there. It's— I think it's always been the plan. Since long before Vosk. The reserve was set aside at the Founding. Somebody's always known it was there. Every Comptroller-General. They just never needed it until now.\"\n\n\"And the Calling?\"\n\nHe had thought about that all night, on his knees, and then at his desk, and then walking down the galleries in the grey morning.\n\n\"Thirty thousand people can't live under a lamp built for four,\" he said. \"But ten thousand fewer is a start. And then, when the Wick goes out, the Galleries go dark, and the Cold comes up from the aft end, and—\" He stopped. \"And it doesn't matter, then, how many there are. Because they'll be in the dark, and the Count will be in the light, and the Account will go on being kept, by the ones who are left.\"\n\nTamsin Hask said nothing for a long time. On the bunk Agathe stirred, and coughed in her sleep, and settled.\n\n\"It's not arithmetic,\" Tamsin said at last. \"It's a lifeboat.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"With fourteen seats in it, and a door, and a man with a stick at the door.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nShe got up off the floor. She went to the shelf over the stove and took down the biscuit tin with the farmhouse on the lid and opened it, and stood looking into it — at the chits, all the family's chits, three hundred years of them — and then she closed it again and put it back.\n\n\"I'm going to take them,\" she said.\n\nHe stared at her.\n\n\"The fourteen. Out of the Hulk, up the spine, into the Wick. All of them. And light it. All the way to the aft end.\" She turned round, and her face was quite calm. \"You're going to tell me how to get into the Hulk.\"\n\n\"I can't—\" he began, and stopped. He had been going to say _I can't, it's the Count's_, and found that the words did not mean anything any longer. He said instead: \"I don't know how. I found a stencil on a floor. That's all. _Reactor access, frame forty._ I don't know where frame forty is. I don't know if there's a way in from the halls. The Sanctum's down there. The Tally's root. Nobody goes into the Hulk but the Comptroller-General.\"\n\n\"Then find out.\"\n\n\"And if I do? You'll walk in with a cutter's lance and fourteen cassettes the size of coffins will just— come out with you? Up through the Count, up the spine, past the bailiffs, past Lusk—\"\n\n\"No,\" said Tamsin. \"Not on my own.\" And she looked past him, at the door, and he turned and saw that it had opened while they were talking, without the sigh and clunk it usually made, and Jory was standing in it, with his finger on his lips, grinning; and behind him, filling the doorway from side to side, there was a slab of a man in a cutter's rig whom Orrin had never seen before, and who looked at him with no expression at all.\n\n\"This is Dobbs,\" said Tamsin. \"He works for the Salt. Dobbs, this is my counter.\"","wordCount":932,"createdAt":1793147487505,"updatedAt":1793147487505},{"id":"WhE620txS9qz0I6sRTT49","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"y2ttHnDgt8Jg1CDQBa4ir","text":"Dagny had thought about how to begin. In the end she didn't begin; Hesketh did, which he had told her he would, because he was the eldest.\n\n\"Director,\" he said. \"There are nine thousand one hundred and forty indentured people on this rock. You've told us you're leaving and they're staying. We've come to ask what happens to them.\"\n\n\"I told you that, too,\" said Ruth Achterberg. She sat very straight at the bare desk. The brass disk of the seal lay against her collarbone, on its chain. \"Their contracts are held in trust. Their systems are run by the Tally. Nothing changes.\"\n\n\"Everything changes,\" said Mariam Oyelowo.\n\n\"Nothing that matters to the air.\"\n\n\"It matters to the air that there'll be nobody on this rock who can hear a complaint,\" said Dagny. \"Five days ago a man died in a lock on Gallery Nine because his balance was four over. There was no counter on Nine. The override was here.\" She put her hand flat on the desk. \"In this building. With you. In fifty days there won't be anybody in this building at all. What happens then, Director? Who does the next one ask?\"\n\nAchterberg looked at her for a moment. \"I'm sorry about Mr. Adeyemi,\" she said, and it was hard to tell whether she meant it; her voice was the same voice she used for everything. \"Mr. Basu has prepared a proposal which I think addresses that. Teodor?\"\n\nThe Comptroller cleared his throat. His hand went to the folder in front of him and then away again.\n\n\"The— the Tally's dimming line,\" he said, \"is currently set at minus nine hundred credits. For the duration of the closure the Company proposes to reset it to minus fifteen hundred. It also proposes to suspend interest on all accounts. So that no balance grows except by— by actual consumption. Nobody's debt will grow merely by— by being there.\"\n\n\"It's a substantial concession,\" said the Director. \"It costs the Company a great deal.\"\n\n\"It costs the Company nothing,\" said Dagny. \"The Company won't be here.\"\n\nThere was a small silence.\n\n\"And what happens at minus fifteen hundred?\" said Hesketh, mildly. \"Same as at nine. Door shuts. Ration stops. Lock goes to recovery. You've given us six hundred credits of rope, Director. That's a year for a child. Then what?\"\n\n\"Then they'll have to work,\" said Achterberg. \"As people do everywhere.\"\n\n\"With what? You're taking the refinery staff. You're taking the ice contracts. You're taking the—\"\n\n\"We are taking,\" said the Director, \"what is ours, Mr. Hesketh. As is our right.\" She did not raise her voice. \"I want to be absolutely clear with all three of you, because I think you have been told things by people who wanted you to believe them. The _Covenant_ can carry fourteen hundred people. She will carry the Company's essential staff, who number four hundred and six with their families, and she will carry their effects and such Company property as can be moved. She cannot carry nine thousand. Nothing in this system can carry nine thousand. If I could take you all, I would not have to, because you would be a cost and not an asset; but I can't, so the question does not arise.\"\n\n\"Then leave us the Tally,\" said Dagny. \"Unlocked. Take the books with you if you want them, take every number in it, but take the locks off the air before you go. Let the scrubbers run for everybody, debt or no debt. That's all we're asking. Not passage. Not money. Air.\"\n\nAchterberg turned and looked at Teodor Basu. Basu looked at the desk.\n\n\"Tell them,\" said the Director.\n\n\"It— the Tally isn't a lock on the air plant, Ms. Ostrova,\" Basu said. \"It is the air plant. That's— that's how it was designed. There is no separate control system. Every valve on Lantern asks the Tally whether to open, and the Tally answers by— by checking whose air it is. If you took the Tally out, the valves would have nobody to ask. They'd fail closed. Every one.\" He spread his hands, helplessly. \"There's no way to unlock it without— without stopping it. And if you stop it—\"\n\n\"Everyone dies,\" said Achterberg. \"Yes. I'm sorry. It was not built to be convenient for this moment. It was built, ninety years ago, to make sure no one on this rock ever took air that was not paid for. It has done that very well. It will go on doing it.\" She folded her hands. \"I would ask you to look at it a different way. The Tally is the only reason this colony can survive our withdrawal at all. It runs itself. It needs no engineers, no clerks, no Director. As long as you work, it will keep you alive. That is not nothing. In most closing systems in the Concord's history the colonists starved within a decade. You will not.\"\n\n\"We'll just owe,\" said Mariam.\n\n\"You'll owe what you have always owed. To the people who built this place and paid for your passage to it.\" Achterberg stood. \"And one day the Throat will open again, and the Company — or its successors — will come back, and the debt will be honoured. That is what _in trust_ means. It means we haven't forgotten you.\"\n\nBehind her, Hale Varga, who had not said a word, shifted his weight very slightly, the way a big man does when he wants a room to remember he's in it.\n\nHesketh got up on his stick. \"Six hundred years,\" he said. \"That's a long time to be remembered.\"","wordCount":938,"createdAt":1793146166579,"updatedAt":1793146166579},{"id":"X8bb5p9Y-YIeBoq3jGqGM","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"LvXM0bEI0nlmqY1Qsun4c","text":"The answer had been under his feet all his life.\n\nOrrin found it on the sixth night, on his knees, in the fifth ledger hall, which was the lowest and oldest of the five, and the only one whose floor was not rock. He had not gone there looking for it. He had gone there because he had run out of places to look, and because when a counter has run out of places to look he goes back to the beginning, and the beginning of the Count, everybody knew, was the Hulk.\n\nThe fifth hall was the Account Day hall. It was where the tours came. It was kept a little cleaner than the others and lit a little brighter, and at its centre, where the desks stopped, there was a square of floor fenced off by a low brass rail, with a plaque on the rail that every child on Lantern had read aloud on their first visit, slowly, sounding out the long words. _HERE THE FOUNDERS RAISED THE COUNT UPON THE BONES OF THE SHIP THAT BROUGHT US. OWING AND OWED._ Inside the rail the floor was the Hulk's own plating, unpainted for the tours: a great curved sheet of riveted metal, dark blue-grey, worn smooth and shiny in the middle where three hundred years of small hands had touched it.\n\nOutside the rail the same plating ran on under the desks, under the walls, under the whole of the hall, painted over in Count grey. And under the grey, in places — where chairs had been dragged, where feet had scuffed — the old blue showed through.\n\nHe had noticed it, absently, since he was a boy. He noticed it properly on the sixth night because he had been staring at the floor for an hour, sitting at a desk in the empty hall with his head in his hands, trying to think where else in the Count a hull might be numbered; and his eye had fallen on a place by the wall where a filing chest had stood for a century and been moved, and the paint under it had peeled in a long curling strip, and under the peeled grey there was blue, and on the blue there was white.\n\nA stencil. Very old. The edge of a letter, or a number.\n\nHe got down on his knees. He had nothing to scrape with but the stylus Jory had given back to him. He used that. The Count grey came away in flakes and dust and then, as he worked down the strip, in long satisfying curls, and under it, in a stencil a hand's-breadth high, in the square white Company type that he had seen only on the oldest ledgers in the archive, letters came up out of the floor.\n\nHA 7731.\n\nAnd beside it, smaller: REACTOR ACCESS — FRAME 40 — AUTHORISED PERSONNEL.\n\nHe knelt there for a long time with paint dust on his knees and his hands. Then he got up, and went to the brass rail, and looked at the bare shining metal inside it that he had touched at seven and felt nothing.\n\n_The bones of the ship that brought us._\n\nThe Hulk was Hull 7731. And the Hulk had a reactor. And in the light ledger, carried forward every quarter for three hundred years on the Founders' own authority, the Hulk's reactor held fourteen units in reserve.\n\nHe went back to the third hall and opened the light ledger again and did the arithmetic, because that was what he was for. A cassette, at the Wick's present length — a third of what it had been at the Founding — lasted a little under three years. At full length, a little under one. Fourteen units would light the Wick as it burned now for forty years. Would light it from end to end, the whole length of the world, the Cold Wards and all, for fourteen.\n\nHe sat back. He thought about the Comptroller-General standing on the gallery above the Hook, saying in his soft voice that the last cassette would be spent before the end of the year. That the Count had made such provision as it could. That it was arithmetic.\n\nIt was arithmetic. Orrin had simply not been given all of the numbers.\n\nThere was one more thing to look for, and he found it in the Wickwrights' own orders, which the Count filed and nobody read: a work order, issued two years ago, signed by the Comptroller-General, for an _auxiliary lamp, Upper Wards, to be commissioned on the failure of the Wick_. Specifications attached. It was a small lamp. A lamp for three terraces and the Counting House. A lamp for perhaps four thousand people.\n\nIts fuel source, in the specifications, in Vosk's own neat hand, was given as _Reserve_.","wordCount":808,"createdAt":1793147487593,"updatedAt":1793147487593},{"id":"ZYo0mAUopIIrhMoyp61KP","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"h2gC_4R7WoP77fprmKMMo","text":"They met in the old chapel on Gallery Four, because it was the only room on Lantern with no Company camera in it. The Company had never thought a chapel worth watching.\n\nIt was a long room cut into the rock with a low ceiling and benches made of hauler crate-wood, and at the far end, where in a chapel on a planet there might have been an altar, there was a window of coloured resin somebody had poured in the colony's first decade: a crude picture of a ship crossing a dark sea toward a lamp. The _Ardent Mercy_, coming to Lantern. The resin had yellowed. The lamp in the picture had gone the colour of tea. Under it, on the night of the fourteenth day of the Closing, sixty-one Speakers from every crew on the rock sat shoulder to shoulder on the crate-wood benches and argued for four hours.\n\nDagny let them. She had learned that from Hesketh, years ago, when she was a new Speaker and wanted every meeting to end in the first ten minutes. _People need to say it_, the old man had told her. _Even if it's been said. Especially if it's been said. You can't ask a man to risk his door for a thing he hasn't heard himself say out loud._ So she let them say it. That the Company was leaving. That the debt was staying. That the dimming line was a rope with a noose on the end, and that six hundred extra credits only made the rope longer. That there were children on Gallery Nine already sleeping in the corridors because their doors had dimmed. That the farms couldn't feed the rock without the Company's refinery-tech. That the stills couldn't run without the Company's spare parts. That the Company would take the parts. That the Company would take the fuel. That the Company would take everything except the debt and the dead.\n\nAt the end of the fourth hour, when people had stopped saying new things and begun saying old ones again more loudly, Mariam Oyelowo stood up in the front row, slow and broad, and said:\n\n\"The _Covenant_ eats.\"\n\nThe room quietened. Mariam did not raise her voice; she never did.\n\n\"Fourteen hundred people, eight months to the nearest Concord port, that's what my sister in the Company kitchens says. Fourteen hundred mouths for eight months. Where's that coming from? Not the Concord. There's no ship from the Concord. It's coming off the strip-farms. Off my farms. The Company's been taking our harvest into the _Covenant_'s cold holds for a week. They need two more harvests to provision her. Maybe three.\" She looked round at them, her face heavy and calm. \"Farms don't grow in the dark.\"\n\nNobody said anything for a moment. Then everybody turned and looked at Dagny.\n\n\"The Wick,\" said somebody at the back.\n\n\"The Wick,\" said Dagny.\n\nShe stood up. Her knees hurt; they always did, now, after a long sitting. She went to the front, under the yellowed window, and turned and faced them.\n\n\"I can bring the lamp down,\" she said. \"Not out. Never out — if it goes out, it may not light again, and then we're all dead in a week and the Company's won. But down. To a twilight. Enough to keep the scrubbers' bacteria alive and the water from freezing in the mains. Not enough to grow a crop.\" She looked at Mariam. \"How long before the farms suffer?\"\n\n\"Three days, the first beds start to turn. Ten and the harvest's gone.\"\n\n\"Then we tell them we'll bring it back up,\" Dagny said, \"the day the Director goes into the root of the Tally with her seal and takes the dimming line out of it. Not lower it. Take it out. Nobody on this rock dimmed again, for anything, ever. Let them have their trust. Let them have their debt on paper and their books full of numbers. But nobody goes into a lock again and doesn't come out because the number's wrong.\"\n\n\"She'll say she can't,\" said Hesketh, from his bench, leaning on his stick.\n\n\"She can. Basu told me so.\" It was the first time she had said it aloud, and she felt the room take it in. \"With the seal, at the root, she can do anything the Tally can do. She can take the line out. She won't, because a debt nobody's made to pay is worth nothing to anybody who might buy it. That's the only reason. Not the air. Not the safety. The price.\" She held up her wrist, the chit on it. \"So we make the price higher.\"\n\nThere was a long silence. Then Joss Teague, sitting on the end of the second bench, with his bad knees stuck out into the aisle, said in his big cracked voice: \"Wick crews are in.\"\n\n\"Pumps are in,\" said Hesketh.\n\n\"Farms,\" said Mariam.\n\n\"Stills.\"\n\n\"Docks.\"\n\nIt went round the room. Not everybody. The hauler crews held back, and the noodle-sellers' association, and a few of the old Gallery Two families who had lived nearest the Upper Wards for three generations and were not going to start being brave now. But enough. Dagny counted the hands, and counted them again, and then looked up at the tea-coloured lamp in the window over her head and thought, absurdly, that she ought to say a prayer, and did not know one.\n\n\"Tomorrow,\" she said. \"Second shift. We bring her down.\"","wordCount":914,"createdAt":1793146964324,"updatedAt":1793146964324},{"id":"_IkYbB-i8RZByIfokDnFN","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"pHplzGMP6B9jNZUrFoF11","text":"The _Good Title_ was a Throat-runner, which was to say she was old, strong, ugly, and built around an engine much too big for her. She had been built to carry cargo through healed throats in the years when throats were still healing, in the last great age of opening, three centuries back. She had run the Ashcombe Throat when it was a raw wound full of debris and the Pellan Throat the week it opened, and she had the scars of both down her flanks, sealed over in four different colours of hull compound. Her master had been running her for thirty years.\n\nCaptain Mabry Ode was sixty-three, gaunt as a gantry, and she did not shake hands. She met them at the ship's lock in a coverall that had been washed so often it had no colour left, and looked at Corvin, and at his case, and at Haste behind him, and at the crate on its trolley with Fen beside it, and said, \"What's that?\"\n\n\"A bell,\" said Fen.\n\n\"I can see it's a bell. What's it for?\"\n\n\"It's going home.\"\n\nOde looked at her for a moment longer, as if deciding whether this was a joke, and then turned and spat, accurately, into the gap between the ship's lip and the dock. \"Tol,\" she said. \"Find a place for the lady's bell. Not the hold. The hold's spoken for.\"\n\nA thin man with grease to his elbows unfolded himself from a hatch and came and looked at the crate with more interest than he had shown in any of the people. \"What's she weigh?\"\n\n\"Three hundred and twelve kilos.\"\n\n\"Bronze?\"\n\n\"Mostly tin bronze. Some lead.\"\n\n\"Huh,\" said Tol Brask, and took the trolley without another word, and went off down the corridor talking to it under his breath.\n\n\"The hold's spoken for?\" said Corvin.\n\nOde jerked her head at Haste. \"Ask him.\"\n\nHaste only smiled. \"Agricultural stock. For the colony, if there is one. Seed, embryos, a soil culture library. If there's no one there, we'll need to start something. It's all sealed and certified. The captain's been very patient about it.\"\n\n\"The captain's had her hold sealed by a lien-house for the first time in thirty years of running throats,\" said Ode, \"and the captain's got a ship that's mortgaged to that lien-house to the last rivet. So the captain's been very patient about it. Yes.\"\n\nBehind them, on the dock, there was the sound of boots. Corvin turned. Ten men and women were coming up the dock in a loose column, in the dark grey field uniform of Brightwater's bailiffs, with kit bags on their shoulders and long cases slung on their backs that were not tools. At their head was a heavy, unhurried man of fifty with a sergeant's flash on his sleeve and the bored eyes of someone who has seen every kind of trouble and found none of it interesting.\n\n\"Sergeant Kell,\" said Haste. \"And his people. You'll find them very quiet.\"\n\n\"I wasn't told about bailiffs,\" said Ode.\n\n\"You were told about a recovery team, Captain.\"\n\n\"I was told about clerks.\"\n\n\"Sergeant Kell is a very good clerk,\" said Haste, and stepped aside to let the column file past him into the lock. Kell nodded to the captain as he passed, civilly enough. Ode watched him go with an expression Corvin had seen before, on four hundred and twelve settlements, on the faces of people watching the bailiffs come off the boat.","wordCount":582,"createdAt":1793146023698,"updatedAt":1793146023698},{"id":"axrX2eyARmPFTF9HMRNmW","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"f2hytYDgklrX8qR_7pRBE","text":"Haste wanted to talk about the instrument. He wanted to talk about it most evenings, in the _Good Title_'s cramped wardroom, over a glass of the good Kessane brandy he had brought aboard in a case of his own, and Corvin had come to understand that this was not because Haste needed to explain it to him. It was because Haste liked to hear it.\n\n\"Persons and issue,\" Haste said, turning the glass. \"Do you know, I think it's the most beautiful phrase in commercial law? Persons. And issue. A debt attaches to the person who incurred it, and to every person who comes out of them, until it's paid. It sounds monstrous when you put it like that. People do put it like that. But think what it means.\" He held the glass to the light. \"It means a promise outlives the man who made it. It means that what you owe doesn't vanish because you've had the good fortune to die. That's not monstrous, Mr. Saal. That's civilisation. That's the whole of it. A world where promises die with the promisor is a world where nobody will lend you anything at all.\"\n\n\"The people on Lantern didn't make the promise,\" said Corvin. \"Their ancestors did. Seven hundred years ago. Most of them under contract to people who owned the air they were breathing.\"\n\n\"Their ancestors signed. Freely, under the law of the day. In return for passage to a new world, which they received.\" Haste sipped. \"Your father signed for you, too. You've never once in thirty-five years suggested to me that his signature was void.\"\n\nCorvin said nothing.\n\nAt the end of the wardroom table Sergeant Kell had a slug-gun in pieces on an oilcloth and was cleaning it, part by part, with the slow absorbed care of a man shelling peas. He did not look up. He had not said a word all evening. He seemed to Corvin like part of the furniture: something solid and practical bolted to the deck, waiting.\n\n\"I've been meaning to ask,\" said Corvin. \"About the hold.\"\n\n\"Agricultural stock,\" said Haste, without a pause.\n\n\"Your agricultural stock draws more power than the rest of the ship together. The engineer showed me the board.\"\n\n\"Does it?\" Haste raised his eyebrows, mildly. \"I'm no engineer. I imagine it's the embryo library. Embryos are delicate. They have to be kept very cold.\" He set his glass down. \"Is there a reason you're interested, Mr. Saal? Beyond the general curiosity of a careful man?\"\n\nThere it was. The pleasant voice, and under it the other thing. Corvin had heard that voice in his own mouth on four hundred and twelve settlements: _is there a reason you're interested?_ It was the voice that told a man where the edge was, and invited him to step back from it.\n\nHe stepped back from it. He was aware of himself doing so. He felt it the way he had felt, once, on a colony on Malory's Reach, the moment when the floor of a dome had started to give under his boot and he had shifted his weight without thinking.\n\n\"No,\" he said. \"No reason.\"\n\n\"Good,\" said Haste, and smiled, and poured him another brandy he did not want.\n\nAt the end of the table Kell slid the bolt back into the slug-gun with a small oiled click, and looked up for the first time, straight at Corvin, with his bored, patient, uninterested eyes; and then looked down again and went on cleaning.","wordCount":585,"createdAt":1793147214125,"updatedAt":1793147214125},{"id":"d7jHHKzQPikNWTLYvVcsM","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"gL8RzmdmYsU_VJsOTmErj","text":"She found Pim on the way home, asleep on the bench by the Bell Hook with his school bag for a pillow and his collection of bolts spilling out of his pocket. Odile was sitting beside him with her engraver in her lap, not working, just sitting, the way old people did.\n\n\"I let him stay,\" Odile said. \"He wanted to see the new name. It's a woman from the stills. Bronwen Price. Her heart.\"\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\n\"He asked me why we cut the names.\"\n\n\"What did you tell him?\"\n\nOdile looked up at the bell, brown and furred and silent on its iron hook above them. \"I told him what my master told me. A ship's bell remembers its crew. Everybody that sailed on her. Everybody that died aboard. When the ship's gone, the bell's what's left, and the names are what it remembers.\" She sniffed. \"He said Lantern isn't a ship. I said it was the only ship any of us has got.\"\n\nDagny sat down on the other side of her sleeping son, and for a while the three of them stayed there, under the bell, in the dim light of the ring at night.\n\n\"They're leaving the Tally to count what we owe them,\" Dagny said at last. \"Every hour of air, for six hundred years.\"\n\n\"So I hear.\"\n\n\"Somebody ought to count what we're owed.\"\n\nOdile turned her head and looked at her, with her old faded eyes, for a long moment. Then she looked up at the bell.\n\n\"I'm running out of bell,\" she said.\n","wordCount":261,"createdAt":1793146166203,"updatedAt":1793146166203},{"id":"duM0s0eBIdnfxMhM7Syx1","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"Zc5kIuvenmtRkGl-4jtFs","text":"It was on the way out, with four metres of copper strapped to her back and the reel winding at her hip, that she found the hatch.\n\nShe'd taken a different way. That was the third thing you learned about the Cold: never quite the same way twice, because the rock moved, a little, over the years, and ice built where it built, and a road you knew could be a slide of frost between one trip and the next. Her usual route through the back of Fourteen had iced over, so she went down a level and along a service way she didn't know, keeping the tether in mind, counting metres.\n\nThe service way ended in a door. Not a house door. A big one, set into the raw rock of the outer wall, with a frame of riveted steel and a wheel at its centre the size of a cart-wheel, rimed white. Tamsin put her brow-lamp on it and brushed at the frost with her glove, and the letters came up under the ice in the old square type nobody used any more.\n\nAFT STORE 3. And under that, two letters leaning on each other like tired men, which she had seen before only in the oldest bits of the Cold, on pipes and plates and the backs of machines: H and A.\n\nThe Company.\n\nShe stood looking at it for a long time. Everybody on Lantern knew the story, the way everybody knew the Account. Once upon a time there had been a Company, and the Company had owned the rock and everything in it, and the Company had built the Wick and the Tally and the locks, and then the Throat had begun to close and the Company had run. Out through the Throat, in their ships, with everything they could carry, and the door shut behind them and they never came back. They took the good fuel and the medicines and the bell from the Dock Ring. They left the Tally, and the Account, and the people. That was the story. You heard it in school and in church and in the songs they sang at the Hook.\n\nAnd here was one of their doors, still shut.\n\nThe seam round the frame had been welded. Not with a cutter's torch, either; it was a proper bead, wide and even, the work of somebody who'd meant it to stay shut for ever. On the floor in front of it, half under the frost, was a body.\n\nThat wasn't unusual. There were bodies all over the Cold. People had died in it when it was first let go, and people had died in it since — cutters, runaways, the drunk, the dimmed who had nowhere else to sleep. The cold kept them. Tamsin knelt and put her light on this one because it was the decent thing and because sometimes they had a chit that could be turned in to the Count, which paid a finder's tenth on any balance closed by death.\n\nIt was a woman, she thought, though it was hard to say. Dried to leather by three centuries of cold dry air. She was wearing old coveralls with the H-A letters on the shoulder, and she was sitting with her back to the hatch and her knees drawn up, and in her two hands, held against her chest, was a child's shoe. Just the one. Small. Red once.\n\nThere was a chit on a cord round her neck. Tamsin turned it to the light. The number was rubbed away, and the old-style printing on the face of it was mostly gone, but the balance was still there, in brackets, as balances always were when they were bad. Two thousand and something.\n\nShe didn't take it. She didn't know why. She sat back on her heels and looked at the woman and the shoe and the welded door, and the frost fell sideways through the light, and from very far away, through a kilometre of cold and dark and rock, she heard the Hook ring.\n\nSomebody was dead.","wordCount":683,"createdAt":1793145782468,"updatedAt":1793145782468},{"id":"f9gV6hH2J_U200xlsrj0C","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"i8ypbu-01B-kOeLt2dcsJ","text":"It was during the second open, with the _Harrow_ and the _Lady Jane_ both gone into the red, that the _Saint Ishmael_ broke the queue.\n\nShe was the ship with the priests: a converted pilgrim-hauler, broad and slow, with a cargo of the faithful in orange who had sold everything they owned, the noodle-woman had told Corvin, for a berth on the first ship through to the Return. She was fourth in the lottery. She should have waited for the third open, as the _Good Title_ was waiting. Instead, as the lips began to close at the end of the second, Corvin saw her drive light flare, very bright, and saw her begin to move.\n\n\"What's she doing?\" he said.\n\n\"Cutting,\" said Ode. She sat up very straight. \"Stupid. Stupid bloody—\" She hit the comm. \"_Saint Ishmael_, _Saint Ishmael_, this is _Good Title_, you are out of turn, the lips are closing, abort, abort your run—\"\n\nThe comm hissed. Then a calm, pleasant voice — the priest's voice, Corvin thought, the one from the gallery rail — said: \"_Good Title_, thank you for your concern. We have been shown the way. We go in faith. Peace be with you all.\"\n\n\"Faith's got nothing to do with the bloody lips, you lunatic—\"\n\nBut the _Saint Ishmael_ was already going, faster than she had any business going, her drive a long white stroke against the swimming stars, straight at the heart of the lens. For a moment Corvin thought she would make it. She was so nearly through. The red of the throat's centre had begun to swallow her bow; he could see the light of it on her hull. Then the lips closed.\n\nHe had expected it to be sudden, and it was not. It was slow, and it was quite silent, and that was worse. The edge of the lens slid across the screen like a curtain drawn across a window, and where it crossed the _Saint Ishmael_ the ship simply came apart: not in an explosion, but as if she had been made of paper and somebody had folded her. Her stern went one way and her bow another and her middle went into a long bright streak of debris that was pulled out and out along the edge of the lips like taffy, glittering, and then the curtain was closed and there was only a thin bright line drawn along the rim of the Throat, slowly spreading, slowly dimming, where four hundred people had been.\n\nThe bridge was very quiet.\n\n\"Throats don't forgive,\" said Mabry Ode at last. She said it softly, without anger, the way you would say the name of someone who had died. \"Every throat-runner learns that the first year, or doesn't get a second.\" She turned to the comm officer, a young man with his hands over his mouth. \"Log it. _Saint Ishmael_, out of turn, lost in the lips, all hands. Time and position. Send it back to Ambry on the next relay.\"\n\n\"All hands,\" the young man said, through his fingers.\n\n\"All hands.\" Ode swung her chair round and looked at Haste, who had not moved. \"That's the Rush, Mr. Haste. That's what your claim's worth, to some people. That's what it's worth to the rest of us too, if we don't keep our heads.\"\n\n\"I'm sure we'll keep them, Captain,\" said Haste.","wordCount":561,"createdAt":1793146447458,"updatedAt":1793146447458},{"id":"gATg08Lbd7eU3yzW7E0lZ","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"4FxCZy2Qs_KaAt_JBQN7T","text":"What happened in the canteen in the next ten minutes Dagny remembered afterwards only in pieces. A man on Gallery Six put his chair through the screen and the screen went on showing football in a web of cracks. Two women began to fight over nothing, over a bench, and had to be pulled apart. Old Mister Okonkwo the schoolteacher sat with his hands flat on the table, saying, \"Six hundred years, six hundred years,\" as if it were a sum that would come out differently if he said it often enough. Somebody shouted that the Company would take the fuel. Somebody shouted that the Company would take the water. Somebody shouted that they should go up to the Upper Wards now, all of them, and see how many lists the department heads had made when they had their own windows broken.\n\nAnd Pim was beside her, she didn't know when he had come in, saying, \"Mum. Mum. In trust. What's in trust mean?\"\n\nShe understood it, she realised, before she understood anything else. It was Lantern; you learned the Company's language before you learned your prayers. _Held in trust_ meant that the Company was leaving, and the debt was staying. It meant that every chit on every wrist on Lantern would go on counting down, hour by hour, against the air, for six hundred years, and that if the Throat ever opened again somebody in a Company suit would come through it with the books in their hand to see how much was owed. It meant that the Adeyemi girl's twelve hundred would be eighteen hundred by the time she was grown and nine thousand by the time she died, and would go to whoever came after her.\n\nIt meant that the lock on Gallery Nine would go on doing what it had done to Lemuel, with nobody even in the Counting House to blame for it.\n\nShe found she was standing on a table. She did not remember getting up there. The canteen was looking at her, the way the Wick crews looked at her when the lamp went wrong and someone had to say a number out loud.\n\n\"All right,\" Dagny said. Her voice came out level, which surprised her. \"All right. Shut up and listen. I'm a Speaker for the Wick crews, and from tonight the Wick crews are going to want to talk to the other crews. Pumps, farms, docks, stills. Everyone who wears a chit. Fifty-two days is a long time. It's long enough for the Company to pack. It's long enough for us to make it listen first.\"\n\n\"Listen to what?\" somebody shouted.\n\n\"To the one thing it hasn't said.\" She held up her wrist, with the chit on it, so that the canteen could see the blue numbers. \"She said the Tally keeps us alive. It doesn't. We keep it alive. We fed it ninety years. If the Company's going, the Company can take its ledger with it, or it can switch the bloody thing off before it goes. Either way, nobody on this rock is going to owe air to an empty office for six hundred years.\"\n\nThere was a silence. Then the man who had broken the screen began to clap, slowly, and others took it up; and Joss, below her, looked up with his wet red face and said, very quietly, so that only she could hear: \"Dag. You know what you just did.\"\n\nShe did. She had just volunteered.","wordCount":581,"createdAt":1793145334287,"updatedAt":1793145334287},{"id":"gmliWU61jApADCLUg8GnJ","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"jCCT8EN2Vj2l-O2J-Y878","text":"Her name was Fen Adaire and she was twenty-nine and she had brought a bell.\n\nIt was in a crate on a station trolley, packed in grey foam, and she would not let it out of her sight. She had come straight from the packet dock with it, she said, because she had been told her berth was on the _Good Title_, and she wanted to see the hold before she agreed to put anything in it, and then the porter had brought her up to the third ring by mistake and here she was. She said all this rather fast, standing in the doorway with one hand on the crate, a slight woman in a travelling coat too warm for the station, dark hair cut short, a provenance archivist's loupe on a cord round her neck. Haste introduced her with the air of a man producing a clever purchase.\n\n\"Miss Adaire reads dead companies,\" he said. \"Ledger formats, charter law, corporate scripts. She did the Tannet salvage for us four years ago. When we found out what else she had, we thought she ought to come.\"\n\n\"What else she had?\" said Corvin.\n\nFen looked at Haste, and then at Corvin, and then she knelt and undid the clasps on the crate and folded back the foam.\n\nThe bell lay on its side in a nest of grey. It was not large — he could have got his arms round it — and it was a strange dull colour, a brown furred all over with fine pale scratches, so that at first he took it for something badly corroded. Then she turned it a little toward the light and he saw that the scratches were letters. Every surface of it was cut with names. Round the lip, up the waist, onto the shoulder and across the crown, in among the casting's raised decoration, in a dozen different hands: names, thousands of them, so close and so many that the bronze between them was only a sort of mortar.\n\nAcross the waist, in raised capitals that had been part of the original cast, larger than everything else and nearly worn away, were two words. ARDENT MERCY.\n\n\"She was a colony carrier,\" said Fen. \"Built at Tannet Reach in 2290. She crossed into the Sabra system in 2296 with the first Halden-Ash settlers. She never came back out. Carriers didn't; they were broken up for plate on arrival. This was her bell.\"\n\n\"Then how is it here?\"\n\n\"That,\" said Fen, \"is the question.\" She sat back on her heels. \"My family's had it six generations. My great-great-great-grandmother bought it at a wreck auction at Tannet. The lot was listed as _bell, bronze, inscribed, recovered from unidentified hulk, condition poor_. She paid eleven crowns for it. Nobody knew what it was. My grandmother was the one who read the waist and looked up the name. We've been trying to find out how it got out of Sabra ever since.\"\n\n\"And?\"\n\n\"And nothing. There's no record of it on any manifest. No record of any Sabra ship being wrecked on the Concord side. The Throat closed in 2387 and the bell turned up at Tannet thirty years later on a hulk nobody could identify, and that's all anybody knows.\" She ran her thumb along the lip, very lightly. \"Somebody brought it out. Somebody carried it through, before the end. I'd like to know who. And I'd like to take it home.\"\n\n\"Home,\" said Corvin.\n\n\"Back through. To wherever it came from.\" She looked up at him and, for the first time, seemed to see him properly: the case, the suit, the face. Her mouth tightened a little. \"Mr. Haste tells me you're the man who'll be explaining to them what they owe.\"\n\n\"If there's anyone to explain it to.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Fen. \"If.\"\n\nHe crouched beside the crate, because it seemed rude not to. Close to, the names became legible. They were tiny, most of them, cut with a fine burr, and the oldest were so worn that he could only make out a letter here and there. The newer ones were sharper. He tilted his head and read the nearest bright line, high on the shoulder, where the light caught it.\n\n_Lemuel Adeyemi._\n\nJust a name. One of thousands. He did not know why it stopped him. Perhaps only that it was so clear, so cleanly cut, as if someone had made it the day before.\n\n\"How many names?\" he said.\n\n\"I've counted two thousand nine hundred and eighty, give or take the ones I can't read.\" Fen closed the foam over the bell, gently, as if putting a child to bed. \"A carrier's crew was forty. Her colonists were six hundred. I don't know who the rest of them are, Mr. Saal. I'd very much like to.\"","wordCount":805,"createdAt":1793146023773,"updatedAt":1793146023773},{"id":"iU4hjKfjk2U6WPUn7Ge-f","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"DZy-AU7Be22nac5ny3Y4T","text":"On the security floor of the Counting House, two levels below them, in an office with a desk and a cot and a wall of camera screens, Hale Varga sat alone in the dark and watched a recording.\n\nIt was from the Dock Ring traffic log. An unscheduled departure, nineteen hours ago: hauler _Magpie_, pilot Ostrova K., logged as tank inspection, flight plan filed for the Caul run. And a return, an hour ago, from the same heading. Six hours out, six hours back. Long enough for a landing. Long enough for a climb.\n\nHe sat with it for a while, his big scarred hands flat on the desk. On another screen, from a camera on the comptroller's floor that the Comptroller did not know was there, Dagny Ostrova sat against a door with her face in her hands.\n\nThen Varga reached out, and deleted the _Magpie_'s flight from the traffic log, and deleted the camera's last hour, and sat on in the dark with nothing on his screens at all.\n","wordCount":171,"createdAt":1793147341838,"updatedAt":1793147341838},{"id":"jxTzAfMqPWshB2cgSg8Ba","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"eSa0yO-8rukj37whOMaQd","text":"The Count posted the lists thirteen days early.\n\nIt came without warning on the morning of the seventeenth day after the Hook. The gutter had come four times in one night — four times the Wick had stammered and nearly died, and four times the whole of Lantern had woken in the dark and lain listening to the silence — and when the light steadied the last time and the third shift began, the boards at the Hook had been papered over in the night with long grey sheets, and the criers were out on every gallery with their slates, and the first thousand names were being read.\n\nThe Count called it mercy. The lamp was failing faster than the Wickwrights had said; every day's delay cost air. Better the first of the Called go now, in good order, while there was still light enough to see them into the Cold, than in a panic later in the dark. The rest would follow in their thousands, every few days. The criers read it out in the Comptroller-General's words, over and over, all morning, and then they began on the names.\n\nAgathe Hask was not in the first thousand. She was in the third.\n\nTamsin heard it from a crier on Twenty-Two and stood very still on the terrace road while he read the rest of the third list, family by family, and the people round her stood still too, and then one of them began to weep, and then a door down the terrace opened and a man ran out of it shouting, and the crier stepped back against the wall with his slate held up in front of him like a shield.\n\nThe first thousand went at second shift.\n\nThe bailiffs brought them down from all over the Galleries in columns, to the ring road at the aft end of the Dock Ring, where it ran past the Salt Docks and on into the dark. There they were counted. There was a table set up in the middle of the road with a lamp on it and a counter behind it, and each family as it came was stopped, and its names read, and its chits touched to a counter's slate, and the slate chimed, and the chits went grey. Released from the Tally's charge. Owing nothing more. And then they were waved on, along the ring road, to the Lip.\n\nTamsin watched from the steps of a dead noodle stall at the corner of Spur Two. Orrin was beside her. He had come down without his coat. He had not said a word since the columns began.\n\nThey came past for an hour. Old people, mostly — the deepest accounts were the longest lives — and the sick, and whole families together where the head of the family was Called and the children under sixteen went with them by law, carrying what they could carry. Bedding. Pans. A man with a goat on a string. A woman with a birdcage, with no bird in it. Nobody fought. That was what Tamsin could not get over afterwards. A thousand people, and the bailiffs were perhaps forty, with their grey coats and their docket-batons, and nobody fought. They walked. They had been walking toward this all their lives, one hour's air at a time, and when it came they knew the way.\n\nBrother Lusk stood at the Lip.\n\nHe was where the light ended, at the iron post where cutters clipped their tethers, a big pale man in grey with his baton through his belt and his hands folded in front of him, and as each family reached him he nodded to them, gravely, the way a priest nods at a door, and they walked past him and on into the blue dusk of the Cold, and their lamps went down the dark road and grew small.\n\n\"He's counting them,\" said Orrin. It was the first thing he had said. \"Look. His lips. He's counting them in.\"\n\nThe counter at the table was a heavy-set grey-haired man with ink on his cuffs. Tamsin did not know him. Orrin did.\n\n\"Abney,\" Orrin said. \"Senior Counter Abney. Third hall. He taught me long division.\" His voice did not change. \"He refused my correction. _Surcharges are not corrected during a Calling._ He's very careful. He's the most careful man I know.\"\n\nThe column was ending. Along the Galleries, faint, a sound had begun — a sound Tamsin had heard once before in her life, at her grandmother's funeral, when the whole of Twenty-Two had done it. Somebody on a terrace high above had taken an iron bar and struck a pipe. A water main, a rail, anything that would ring. A flat hard clang, like the Hook. Then another, on another terrace. Then another. It went round the curve of the world, gallery to gallery, the way the songs went round, until all of Lantern was ringing, thousands of iron bars on thousands of pipes, not in time, not together, a great ragged clanging din that went on and on, under the stammering Wick, while the last of the first thousand walked past Brother Lusk into the dark.\n\nLusk turned his head, slowly, and looked up at the terraces, at the ringing.\n\nHe did not seem angry. He seemed — Tamsin thought, afterwards — interested. As if it were a sum that had come out in an unexpected way, and he would want to check it later.\n\nBeside her, Orrin Sallow was crying. He was doing it without any noise, standing quite straight, with the tears running down his face and his hands at his sides.\n\n\"I believed it,\" he said. \"All of it. _The Count is just._ I said it every morning. I said it to her.\" He did not wipe his face. \"I want to see the floor of the Hulk, Tamsin. Frame forty. I want to see the fourteen. I want to see them with my own eyes.\"\n\n\"You will,\" said Tamsin.\n\nShe found that her hand was in his. She did not remember taking it. She did not let go.\n","wordCount":1021,"createdAt":1793147487294,"updatedAt":1793147487294},{"id":"k7Mo-_aTIYC603THsCkhu","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"uTK9LSRUiQd0vJvwfbVuL","text":"Odile rang the bell for Joss Teague at nine that night, and the whole Dock Ring stopped for him, and then went on.\n\nDagny watched her cut the name. She had brought Pim, because there was nowhere to leave him — their door on Six was dimmed, and would stay dimmed until she found five hundred credits she did not have — and he sat on the bench under the Hook with his knees drawn up and his face closed, and did not look at the bell at all. He had not spoken since the Wick. When she had gone to him at the end of the master ring, after Varga's man let him go, he had let her hold him and said nothing, and he had said nothing since.\n\nThe engraver whined. _Joss Teague._ It came up bright on the bronze, high on the shoulder, just under Bronwen Price, just under Lemuel Adeyemi.\n\n\"I'm nearly out,\" said Odile, when she had finished. She said it differently this time. Not as a joke. \"Look. There's the shoulder and the crown, and then there's nothing but the hook.\"\n\n\"There's the band on the waist,\" said Dagny. \"The plain one.\"\n\n\"That's the founders' band. You don't cut the founders' band.\" Odile put the engraver down. Her hands, which had been still as stone on the bronze, had begun to shake again. \"Thirty years I've kept her. Ninety-one years she's hung there. And now there's three hundred Company men going to walk past her up the ramp to their ship and not one of them's ever going to have his name on her, and she's full.\" She looked at the bell for a long moment. \"Joss used to buy me a drink every Christmas. He said it was for keeping his mother. I cut his mother twelve years ago.\"\n\nDagny did not know what to say. So she said nothing, and sat down on the bench beside her son, and the three of them stayed there under the bell, as they had a week before, while the Dock Ring went about its night business round them.\n\nThat was where Teodor Basu found them.\n\nHe came along the ring road in a dark coat, without his Company suit, and without any of the two or three security men who usually walked a little behind Company officers on the lower rings. He looked as if he had not slept since she last saw him. He stopped in front of the bench and looked at Pim, and at Odile, and then at Dagny.\n\n\"I'm— I'm sorry,\" he said. \"About Mr. Teague.\"\n\n\"Everyone's sorry.\"\n\n\"Yes.\" He sat down, uninvited, on the end of the bench, very upright, his hands on his knees. \"I've come to tell you two things. I don't know if either of them is any use. I've decided that isn't for me to judge any longer.\"\n\nDagny waited.\n\n\"The first is about the keys.\" He spoke low and fast, without the stammer, the way he had on the Wick. \"I told you the Director's seal never leaves her. That's true. And mine never leaves me. That's true too. But there's one more time they must be used together, before the _Covenant_ goes. On the morning of departure the Director and I go down into the root, together, and close the Company's own accounts on the Tally — the staff accounts, the Concern's operating accounts — and transfer the trust. It's in the charter. It has to be done by both keys, at the root, in person. It will take about twenty minutes.\" He looked at her. \"That's the only time, between now and the Throat, that both keys will be in the same room as the root. With the door open.\"\n\nDagny was very still.\n\n\"And the second thing?\" she said.\n\n\"The second thing is something I don't understand.\" Basu looked down at his hands. \"I keep the _Covenant_'s manifest. Every crate. Every cassette. I've been through it a dozen times, because it's my job, and because— because I wanted to see what was being taken. There's one item on it I didn't put there. It came aboard under Mr. Varga's seal, from a Concord military shipment, three months ago, before the throat-watch said anything at all. It's listed as _device, Concord ordnance, class T_. It's in a shielded bay behind the drive. I asked the Director what it was. She told me it was a precaution.\" He raised his eyes. \"I've worked for her for twenty years, Ms. Ostrova. I know when she's telling me the truth. I know when she isn't. She wasn't.\"\n\n\"What's class T?\"\n\n\"I don't know.\" He stood up. \"I'm going to find out. I think— I think I may not like it.\"\n\nHe went away along the ring road, very upright, into the night. Dagny sat and looked after him. Beside her, Odile had picked up the engraver again and was turning it in her shaking fingers.\n\n\"That man,\" Odile said, \"has done something he can't carry.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"They always come to the bell in the end. The ones who can't carry it.\" She looked up at the bronze, at the bright new name on the shoulder. \"Dagny.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"If you're going to make them pay,\" said the old woman, \"I'll need more bell.\"\n","wordCount":886,"createdAt":1793146963886,"updatedAt":1793146963886},{"id":"mPHjkR_fNAS2Yz60q3FWS","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"kUV91bMJyH1WgOtlk72uB","text":"It took a day to get the lance in.\n\nWenna sent four cutters: Dobbs, a slab of a man who said nothing all day, and three others whose names Tamsin did not bother to learn, with sledges and a thermal lance and two tanks of oxygen and enough tether to rope off half the Cold. They went in at the Lip in the dead of third shift, when the Count's bailiffs were thinnest on the lower roads, and Tamsin led them by her old way through the back of Pump Gallery Fourteen and down the service way that ended at the door.\n\nThe woman was where Tamsin had left her, sitting with her back to the hatch and the shoe held against her chest. Dobbs looked at her and then at Tamsin and stood back. Tamsin knelt and put her arms round the dried, light, terrible body, and lifted her — she weighed less than a child — and carried her ten metres back along the service way and set her down against the wall in the same position, knees up, the shoe in her hands, facing the door. Jory watched. He did not say anything.\n\nThen they lit the lance.\n\nThe weld was good. Tamsin had known it would be. Whoever had laid it had laid it as if they meant it to outlast them, a wide even bead all the way round the frame, and the steel under it was thick, old Company plate. The lance ate into it slowly, white and roaring, with the steel running red down the face of the door and freezing as it ran into long black icicles of slag. They took it in turns. It was hot, hard, choking work, in masks, in the cold, with the sweat freezing in their rigs, and it took them eleven hours. At the end of it Dobbs put a pry-bar the length of a man into the gap and leaned on it, and two of the others leaned with him, and the wheel at the centre of the door, which none of them had dared touch, turned on its own a quarter-circle with a groan like an animal, and the hatch of Aft Store 3 swung open into the dark.\n\nThe air that came out was colder than the Cold.\n\nThey stood back from it and let it settle. Then Tamsin put her brow-lamp up and went in.\n\nIt was a long room. Longer than she had imagined. A vault cut into the raw rock of the outer wall, forty metres deep, with a rounded roof, and down the length of it on both sides, in rows of three, racks: tall steel cradles with clamps and cables and coffin-shaped hollows in them, each hollow the size of a man. Tamsin had never seen a fuel cassette. Nobody below the Wickwrights had. But she knew what had stood in those hollows as soon as she saw them. There were a hundred of them. Two hundred. More.\n\nThey were all empty.\n\nDobbs came in behind her with a bigger lamp and swung it along the racks, slowly, all the way to the end. Empty. Every one. The clamps hung open. The cables lay coiled on the floor where somebody had unplugged them and dropped them. At the far end, on a pallet, there was a heap of what looked like spare clamps and a sealed medical locker with a red cross on it, frosted white.\n\n\"Well,\" said Dobbs, the only word he said all day. \"There's your fuel.\"\n\nTamsin did not answer. She was looking at the wall beside the door.\n\nThere was a paper on it. An actual paper, a Company docket in a plastic sleeve, pinned to a board at eye height where somebody signing things out would see it. The plastic had gone yellow and brittle and the paper inside was brown, but the cold had kept it, and the print was clear. She did not read well. She read slowly, moving her lips, putting her light on each line.\n\nTRANSFER DOCKET — AFT STORE 3.\nCASSETTES 1–212 TO WICK STORE, SPINE ROAD. FOR THE LAMP.\nSTORE TO BE SEALED ON COMPLETION.\n\nAnd a date she could not make out, and a stamp, the two tired letters, H and A, and under the stamp a signature. She could read that, just. It was small and neat and careful, the hand of somebody who signed a great many things and wanted each of them to be legible. _T. Basu._\n\n\"Two hundred and twelve,\" she said aloud. \"For the lamp.\"\n\n\"So that's where the Wick's fuel came from,\" said one of the cutters. \"Out of here. Three hundred years back. Huh.\"\n\n\"So that's where it went,\" said Tamsin. She did not know why she said it like that. She took the docket off the board, sleeve and all, and folded it carefully, and put it inside her rig.\n\nIt was Jory who found the other thing. He had gone along the back of the racks with his own light, the way he went everywhere, sideways and quick, and he had stopped at the end nearest the door, in a corner behind the last rack where somebody had heaped up a nest of old grey insulation blankets. He was standing very still.\n\n\"Tam,\" he said, in a voice she had not heard him use since he was small.\n\nShe went. She looked. Then she put her hand on his shoulder and turned him round, gently, and walked him back toward the door, and went back on her own.\n\nThey were very small bones. They were mostly hidden in the blankets, which was something. And on the rock wall above the nest, scratched in with something sharp — a buckle, maybe, or a spoon — at the height a child could reach, there was a column of short lines. Tally marks. Tamsin counted them because she could not help it. Eleven.\n\nUnder the eleven marks, in big uneven letters, the kind a child would make who had only lately learned them, there were five words.\n\nSHE SAID SHE WOULD COME BACK.\n\nTamsin stood there for a long time. Then she went out of the store and along the service way to where she had set the woman down against the wall with the red shoe in her hands, facing the door that had been welded shut ten metres from her for three hundred years; and she knelt down in front of her and could not, for a while, see anything at all.\n\nWhen she came back, Dobbs and the others had already begun to unbolt the empty racks for the steel. Nobody had touched the corner. Nobody looked at her.\n\n\"Leave the blankets,\" she said. \"And the wall.\"\n\n\"We know,\" said Dobbs.","wordCount":1134,"createdAt":1793147103119,"updatedAt":1793147103119},{"id":"majQFPRjFla7c8HjpP8e7","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"YoW2-AGKrhEXdDCLO-hxU","text":"Basu was waiting for her at the foot of the Wick when the _Magpie_ docked, and he did not need to ask. He looked at her face and nodded, and then he said, \"Come up. Please. There's something I can show you now. I couldn't before.\"\n\nHe took her up into the Counting House by the service stair, past the offices, to a narrow room at the back of the comptroller's floor where there was nothing but a wall of old screens and a chair. There were no windows. On every screen there were figures.\n\n\"The throat-watch station,\" he said. \"Its raw feed. Not the summaries that come to the Director's office. The raw readings, every six hours, from the instruments. They come to my floor first, for the accounts — the Company charges the Concern for the station's running, so I log its output. Nobody's ever looked at them but me and the man who sends them. A man called Roach. He's been out there eleven years.\" He sat down in the chair and began touching screens, bringing up columns. \"I'm not a physicist. But I can read a trend. I've read nothing but trends for thirty years. I went back through every reading for the last six months, last night, after I left you.\"\n\nHe turned the chair round to face her.\n\n\"There is no closing phase,\" said Teodor Basu. \"There never was. The Throat's breathing is exactly what it's been for ninety-one years. Regular as a clock. The numbers in the Director's announcement — the ones she said came from the throat-watch — they aren't in the throat-watch's feed. They aren't anywhere. Somebody in the Director's office made them up.\" He took his glasses off and rubbed his eyes, carefully, round the cracked lens. \"Roach has been asking for confirmation. Three times. Nobody's answered him.\"\n\nDagny sat down on the floor with her back against the door, because there was nowhere else to sit and her legs had stopped holding her.\n\n\"Then it's not closing,\" she said. \"It's not going to close. We could just— wait. Let them go. And in fifty days the inspectors come through, and—\"\n\n\"It's going to close,\" said Basu.\n\nHe turned back to the screens and touched one, and the columns of throat-watch figures gave way to a single page of text. A manifest entry. She knew the format; she had seen him look at it before, on the bench by the bell.\n\n_Device, Concord ordnance, class T. Shielded bay 4, aft of drive. Received under Security seal._\n\n\"I found out what class T means,\" he said. \"It took me a while. It's not in any Company catalogue. It's in the Concord's own military register, the part that's public, under _throat ordnance_. They were made during the Succession Wars, two hundred years ago, when the Concord was fighting itself through every throat it had. A fleet that was losing would go back through a throat and then—\" he made a small movement with his hand, a closing— \"behind them. So the enemy couldn't follow.\"\n\n\"Close it,\" said Dagny.\n\n\"Collapse it. The register calls it a shear. A throat-shear. It's fired into the mouth of a throat by a ship in transit, and it— the physics is beyond me. It cuts the throat's lips. They fold in. The throat collapses. And it stays collapsed, Ms. Ostrova, for six or seven or eight hundred years, exactly as the Director said in her announcement, because that much of what she said is true: a collapsed throat heals on its own time and no one else's.\" He looked at her. \"The Company bought a shear from a Concord arms broker three months ago. Before the throat-watch said anything at all. Before the Director announced anything at all. The _Covenant_ will carry it into the Throat on the day she leaves, with four hundred and six essential staff aboard, and fire it behind her as she comes out the other side. And the Throat will close on the day the Director said it would, and the inspectors will be on the other side of it, and the nine hundred and four will be under the ice of Caul for ever, and nobody in the Concord will ever know. Except as a line in a Company report. _Repatriated._\"\n\n\"And us.\"\n\n\"And you. Nine thousand people who know what Caul is, sealed in a box with a meter on the air, with your contracts held in trust.\" He spread his hands. \"The Company doesn't need to silence you. The Throat will do it.\"\n\nFor a long time neither of them said anything. Dagny sat against the door with her knees up and her hands over her face and saw, on the inside of her eyelids, the blue of the ice and the woman with her palm on the glass.\n\nThen she took her hands away.\n\n\"Then we stop the shear,\" she said. \"We stop it, and we let the inspectors come.\"\n\nTeodor Basu looked at her for a long moment through his cracked glasses.\n\n\"Yes,\" he said. \"I hoped you would say that.\"","wordCount":854,"createdAt":1793147342066,"updatedAt":1793147392567},{"id":"nPilhENJD3dA2coTDksnv","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"DOH-FR-aq2rXZFqUbTdkx","text":"The signal came on the fourth day out from the Throat, at nine minutes past the hour.\n\nThe young comm officer heard it first. His name was Dacey, and he was nineteen, and he had been listening to the silence of the Sabra system for four days with the dogged attention of somebody who has been told by his captain that silence is never really silent if you listen hard enough. He had been working through the Company's old frequency tables band by band, the dull ones, the housekeeping channels, the ones nobody would ever have used to call for help. On the eleventh of them, a narrow band labelled in the 2387 tables simply _HOURLY — INTERNAL_, he heard a click.\n\nHe sat up. He waited. He was a patient boy. At nine minutes past the next hour, it came again: not a click this time, but a burst. A short, dense squeal of data, two seconds long, and then nothing.\n\nHe called the captain. The captain called Tol, and Tol called Fen, and by the time the next hour came round there were six people crowded into the comm space behind the bridge, Corvin among them, in a silence so complete that he could hear Ode's breathing.\n\nNine minutes past. The burst came.\n\n\"Again,\" said Fen. \"Run it back. Slower.\"\n\nDacey ran it back. On his screen the two-second squeal unfolded into a shape: a long ragged comb of pulses, dense at the front, thinning toward the end. Fen leaned over his shoulder, her face very close to the screen, her lips moving.\n\n\"It's Company format,\" she said. \"Their old internal packet. I've seen it in the archives. There's a header—\" she put her finger on the dense front of the comb— \"and then a long body, and then a checksum. The header's encrypted. The body's encrypted. But the structure—\" She stopped.\n\n\"What?\" said Ode.\n\n\"The structure's a posting.\" Fen straightened up. She looked, Corvin thought, as if she had seen something in the room behind them. \"That's what the Company called it. An hourly posting. It's the Tally.\"\n\nNobody spoke.\n\n\"The ledger,\" said Fen. \"The thing that ran the habitat. The Company's documents are full of it. Every hour, on the hour, it posted every account — every debit and credit, air and water and heat, every person on the rock. And it broadcast a summary on this band, to the Company's receivers. Here. The throat-watch. The haulers. So the Company would always know what it was owed.\" She looked at the screen. \"It's still doing it. Seven hundred years. On the hour. Plus nine minutes, because its clock has drifted. It's still posting the accounts.\"\n\n\"So they're alive,\" said Dacey. His voice cracked on it, and he flushed.\n\n\"No,\" said Fen. \"Not necessarily. That's the point. The Tally doesn't need anyone. The Director's statement said so: it's fully autonomous, it requires no Company supervision. If everyone on Lantern died six hundred years ago it would still post their accounts every hour, for as long as it had power. It'd post the dead. It'd post them as closed, or as estate, or—\" She stopped again. \"I don't know what it would post them as. I can't read it.\"\n\n\"The core,\" said Corvin. \"From the throat-watch. It'll have the Company's keys.\"\n\n\"It might. If I can get into it. If the keys are the same ones. If the Tally hasn't changed its own in seven hundred years.\" She pushed her hair back with both hands. \"If I could read the body of that packet, I could tell you how many accounts it's posting, and whether the balances are moving. If they're moving, someone's breathing. If they're not—\"\n\n\"How long?\" said Haste.\n\nHe was in the doorway. Nobody had heard him come. Fen turned round and looked at him, and Corvin saw her face close like a door.\n\n\"I don't know,\" she said. \"Days. A week. It's seven-hundred-year-old cryptography on a core that's been in vacuum since before my family had a name.\"\n\n\"Take as long as you need,\" said Haste pleasantly. \"It doesn't change anything, of course. Either way, we're going to the rock.\" He looked at the screen, at the ragged comb of the burst, as though it were an interesting curio in a dealer's window. \"But I'd like to know which speech Mr. Saal will be giving.\"\n\nHe went away again. After a while, so did everybody else, back to their posts, back to their bunks, until there was only Dacey at his console and Fen at his shoulder and Corvin in the doorway.\n\nTen minutes before the hour, Fen said, without turning round: \"Do you want to hear it again?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Corvin.\n\nSo they waited. Nine minutes past the hour, from somewhere on the far side of the great banded planet ahead of them, behind which a rock eleven kilometres long was turning in the dark, the burst came: two seconds of dense, patient, meaningless noise, exactly as it had come every hour for seven hundred and two years, to receivers that had been dead for most of them.\n\nSomething was still counting.\n\nCorvin stood in the doorway of the comm space and listened to it, and thought — he could not help thinking it, and despised himself a little for it — that it was the sound of a debt being kept. And that he had been sent all this way, at the end of thirty-five years of his own, to collect it.\n","wordCount":920,"createdAt":1793147213908,"updatedAt":1793147213908},{"id":"oecBo2F4hgrbFFbV_79Px","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"AEagkJ64mjXguslMU4DWt","text":"That night, in the cabin he had been given — a steel box two metres by three, with a fold-down desk and a bunk and a porthole looking out on the dock lights — Corvin opened the case and began, again, on the Company's records.\n\nThere were not many. When a throat closes, the last news through it is whatever happened to be on the last ship. Halden-Ash's final filings from the Sabra Concern had come out on a data-run forty days before the Closing: a few thousand pages of accounts, inventories, staff lists, a throat-watch summary, and a statement from the Director to the Company's board. After that there was nothing at all. Seven hundred years of nothing.\n\nHe read the Director's statement again. It was short and dry and he could find nothing wrong with it. _The throat-watch has confirmed the onset of a closing phase. The Concern will withdraw in good order. Essential staff and Company assets will be transported. The indentured population will remain, its contracts held in trust in accordance with the Company's charter, its life-support administered by the Tally, which is fully autonomous and requires no Company supervision. All Concern operations will be wound down prior to withdrawal._\n\nAnd then a list of the operations, and how each had been wound down. The refinery. The greenhouses. The water contracts with the ice-haulers. And, near the end, one line he had noticed on the packet and noticed again now, because it was the only line in the whole document that was about people rather than things:\n\n_Caul Deep: mine closed, spring 2387; workforce of 900 repatriated through the Throat per contract, completed prior to this report._\n\nHe read it twice. Nine hundred people, sent home. Their contracts bought out, presumably, or completed. A decent thing for a company to do, before it left the rest behind. It was the only decent thing in the file.\n\nHe made a note to ask the archivist where the nine hundred had been landed, and turned out the light.\n\nOutside the porthole, very faint, beyond the dock lights and the station's sprawl, the patch of wrong sky was breathing pink.\n","wordCount":361,"createdAt":1793146023502,"updatedAt":1793146023502},{"id":"qEm7CyLw80kthTjhvR9Pp","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"iDqV4BoCHvaJlhJWRsP1w","text":"She went to the Salt that night.\n\nWenna Blackwood was where she had been before, behind the cargo-net curtain at the back of Brannock's cabin, with the stove and the rug and a pot of something on the table. She looked up when Tamsin came in and did not seem surprised.\n\n\"Your counter came back,\" she said.\n\n\"How do you know that?\"\n\n\"Because you're here.\" Wenna pushed out a chair with her boot. \"Sit.\"\n\nTamsin sat. \"Half,\" she said. \"Of what's in the store. You carry. You stand my tools and my air.\"\n\n\"And your mother's tin.\"\n\n\"And my mother's tin. And something else.\"\n\nThe one eye considered her.\n\n\"If it's fuel,\" said Tamsin. \"If there's cassettes in there. I want one. Not to sell. To give to the Wickwrights.\"\n\nThere was a pause. Then Wenna Blackwood laughed, a big rough sound like gravel going down a chute.\n\n\"To give to the Wickwrights,\" she said. \"You hear that, Brannock? The girl wants to light the lamp.\" She wiped her eye. \"Child. If there's a single live cassette behind that door — which there won't be, because the Company took every one it could carry and burnt the rest getting out — it's worth more than every soul on Twenty-Two put together. I'm not giving it to a guild of blind old men so the Count can burn it on the Upper Wards' tomato beds.\"\n\n\"Then I'm not cutting your door.\"\n\n\"It's not my door. It's not your door either.\" Wenna leaned back. \"But all right. All right. If there's fuel, one's yours, to do as you like with. Burn it in your stove for all I care. Two's yours. And if there's not — and there won't be — you've still got half, and your tools, and a winter's air.\" She held out her hand across the table. It was huge and scarred and missing the top of one finger. \"Done?\"\n\nTamsin looked at the hand.\n\n\"There's a body,\" she said. \"By the door. A woman. She's holding a shoe. Your people don't touch her. I move her. And they don't touch anything small.\"\n\nSomething changed in Wenna Blackwood's face. It was there and gone, too quick to name.\n\n\"Done,\" she said.\n\nTamsin took the hand.\n\nBehind her, from the doorway, Jory said, \"I'm coming.\"\n\nShe had not heard him follow her. She turned round, and he was there in the gap of the cargo-net curtain, all elbows, with his chin up.\n\n\"No,\" she said.\n\n\"I'm coming. I can carry. I'm good on a tether. You know I am. And if Mam goes into the Cold—\" his voice cracked, and he pushed through it— \"if Mam goes into the Cold I'm going to need to know the way.\"","wordCount":458,"createdAt":1793147103211,"updatedAt":1793147103211},{"id":"qUVL7gXBE7dSskpz1KYxZ","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"HndU3cXz3axOEEAjEX_H4","text":"The Slake was the worst bar on the Dock Ring and therefore the one the hauler crews used, a low cave cut into the ring wall with a counter made of a hauler's old heat-shield and a smell of spilt still-spirit that had soaked into the rock itself. Kas was at the back table playing banker for a game of shove with four crewmen off the _Agnes_ and the _Dorrit_, and losing. He lost cheerfully. Kas always lost cheerfully; it was why people kept letting him play.\n\nHe saw her face in the doorway and folded his hand.\n\n\"Gentlemen,\" he said, \"my sister has come to save me from myself. Hold my seat. Don't look at my cards. I'll know.\" And he came over and steered her to the end of the counter, where the noise was worst and nobody could hear.\n\n\"Well?\" he said.\n\n\"Nothing. She gave us six hundred credits of rope.\"\n\nKas whistled softly. \"Generous.\"\n\n\"Basu says the Tally can't be turned off without killing everyone.\"\n\n\"Basu would know.\" He signalled for two cups of the thin brown beer the Slake brewed in a tank out the back, and when they came he pushed one to her and drank half of his own in one go. \"So what now? Strike?\"\n\n\"Maybe. Hesketh thinks it's too early. Mariam thinks it's too late.\" She turned the cup in her hands. \"Kas. When's your next run?\"\n\n\"Two days. Why?\"\n\n\"Do any of the haulers go through the Throat? Even now? I was thinking — if somebody could get a message through. To the Concord. The papers, the courts, the Labour Office, I don't know. Somebody who'd care that nine thousand people were being left behind in a box with a meter on the air.\"\n\nHe laughed, not unkindly. \"Dag. Nothing goes through the Throat that the Company doesn't send. Haulers haven't got the drive for it, and the throat-watch would see you coming a week out, and Varga's men would have you before you got halfway. The only things going through now are the _Covenant_ and the last two data-runs. And the data-runs are the Company's.\" He drank again. \"Besides, who in the Concord would care? You think we're the first? There's a hundred company towns past a hundred throats. Nobody cares about any of them.\"\n\nShe didn't answer. After a while he put his hand on her arm.\n\n\"Listen,\" he said, in a different voice. \"I've had an offer.\"\n\n\"What kind of offer?\"\n\n\"Company work. Hauling.\" He glanced round; nobody was near. \"They want every tug that can push a load moving cargo from the aft stores up to the fore dock. Fuel cassettes. Out of the stores and into the _Covenant_'s hold. Hundreds of them. They're paying in hard credit, Dag. Concord credit. Not chits. Real money, that spends on the other side.\"\n\nShe stared at him. \"They're taking the fuel.\"\n\n\"Some of it. They say they're leaving enough for the Wick. Three hundred years, at the length she burns now.\" He shrugged. \"They're taking the rest. It's theirs. They bought it.\"\n\n\"And you're going to haul it for them.\"\n\n\"I'm going to get paid for hauling it for them.\" His hand tightened on her arm. \"Think, Dag. Think about what hard credit's good for. On this side of the Throat it's worth nothing. On the other side it's worth everything. If a man had enough of it—\"\n\n\"There's no other side, Kas. Not for us. You just said so.\"\n\n\"Not for us,\" he agreed, and let go of her arm, and smiled, and the smile was the one he had used on her since he was five years old and had broken something. \"Course not. Drink your beer.\"","wordCount":621,"createdAt":1793146166430,"updatedAt":1793146166430},{"id":"rGUYP0XHLPgUFTnve3V9l","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"8jiGFknV0ZrBQVyPMlNAI","text":"He had never been to the top of the Counting House. The Comptroller-General's gallery was a single long room with a single long window, curving, that looked straight down the length of Lantern along the Wick, and when Orrin came through the door the light hit him so hard he had to stop and put his hand up. Beyond the glass the whole world hung, lit and upside down: the Upper Wards bright, the Galleries dimming, the Wick blazing down the middle of the sky to where, two-thirds of the way along, it simply stopped, and beyond it the dark of the Cold lay like a bruise.\n\n\"Few people are prepared for it,\" said Imre Vosk. \"Sit, Counter. Please.\"\n\nHe was at a desk at the far end of the room, with the window behind him so that it was hard to see his face. Orrin sat. There was a cup of tea in front of him, already poured. He did not touch it.\n\n\"You've been in the root,\" said Vosk.\n\n\"Yes, Comptroller-General. I'm fourth rank. It's open to—\"\n\n\"It is open to you. Yes. I'm not reproaching you.\" Vosk's voice was as soft as it had been at the Hook; it was a voice you leaned toward without meaning to. \"I'm interested in what took you there. Counters of your age don't usually go reading three hundred years of rate tables for pleasure.\"\n\n\"There's an error in an account on the Calling list. A surcharge keyed to the whole balance. I wanted to see whether the rule was at fault, or the keying. It was the keying. The rule's sound.\"\n\n\"Of course it is. The Founders wrote it.\" Vosk nodded. \"And the account?\"\n\n\"Hask. Agathe. Twenty-Two. Corrected, she's above the cut. I filed it. Senior Counter Abney refused it until after the lists.\"\n\n\"Abney was correct. We can't have corrections in flight during a Calling, Counter. You understand that. If every counter went into every account looking for reasons to lift it over the line, the line would mean nothing, and the Calling would fail, and then all of us would go into the dark together.\" He spread his hands. \"It's a hard rule. It's the rule.\"\n\n\"She doesn't owe it,\" said Orrin. \"Two thousand credits of it. It's not hers.\"\n\n\"Then it will be corrected afterwards, as Abney said.\"\n\n\"Afterwards she'll be in the Cold.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Vosk gently. \"That's a pity.\" He let a moment pass. \"You saw the first entry. In the root.\"\n\nIt was not a question. Orrin said, \"Yes.\"\n\n\"What did you make of it?\"\n\n\"I didn't— I don't know. _Account holder, none._ And the key. I've never seen the Tally say that.\"\n\n\"The Tally says what it was told to say, at the Founding, by people who had a great deal more to think about than how it would read to a counter three hundred years later.\" Vosk leaned forward a little, and the light from the window lay along his shoulder and the side of his face. \"Let me tell you what the doctrine says, Counter Sallow, since you know it already. The Account is held by Caul. Caul holds itself. The Founders gave the Account to the dark that kept us, so that no living man could ever own it, and no Company could ever come back and claim it. That is what _holder, none_ means. That is why the key is not known. Because it was not a man's key. Do you understand?\"\n\n\"Yes, Comptroller-General.\"\n\n\"The Count administers the Account. It does not own it. I hold the Count Key, which lets me keep the books, and I hold it as my predecessors did, in trust, for Caul. That's all. There's nothing hidden in the root, Counter. There's only the Founding, which is a holy thing, and which is not improved by being puzzled over by clever young men at dinner time.\" He sat back. \"You've done good work on the Calling lists. Abney tells me you're the most careful counter in the third hall. I'd like you to go on being careful. Finish your four hundred. Sign your sightings. And then, when the Calling is done, refile your correction, and I will approve it myself.\"\n\nOrrin looked at the tea he had not drunk. He thought of the room on Twenty-Two, and the tin with the farmhouse on the lid.\n\n\"Thank you, Comptroller-General,\" he said.\n\n\"Owing and owed, Counter.\"\n\n\"Owing and owed.\"","wordCount":743,"createdAt":1793146319319,"updatedAt":1793146319319},{"id":"rVXGdPMNuQjkpHT5DrMJk","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"yOks9hrcFzL4xJijiOILK","text":"Orrin Sallow had been taught, at six, that every number in the Account was a person, and that a counter who made an error had wronged somebody he would never meet.\n\nHe had believed it then and he believed it now, at twenty-six, sitting at his slate in the third ledger hall of the Counting House with the lamps turned up as far as the Count allowed and a cup of cooling barley-water at his elbow. He believed it in the plain unexamined way he believed that the Wick was above him and the rock below. It was why he had become a counter rather than a doctor or a Wickwright, which had been his mother's two hopes for him. A doctor saved the one in front of him. A counter, if he was careful, kept faith with all of them.\n\nThe ledger halls lay in the belly of the Counting House, under the old Company offices and over the Sanctum, where the Tally itself ran and where nobody went but the Comptroller-General. There were five halls, each a long low vault with rows of desks down it, and at every desk a counter in grey with a slate, and on every slate a family. The halls were quiet. Counting was quiet work. There was only the soft tapping of styluses and, very faintly, through the floor, a hum that some of the older counters said was the Tally thinking and that Orrin privately thought was a pump.\n\nThe Hulk was under all of it. You could not forget that in the halls, because the floor of the lowest one was not rock but metal: a great curved plate, riveted, cold to the touch, painted over many times in Count grey and showing through the paint in places as a dull blue. Children who came on the Account Day tours were allowed to kneel and put their hands on it. _That's her_, the guides said. _That's the_ Ardent Mercy, _that brought the first families. The Founders built the Count on her bones, so that the Account would stand on the ship that brought us._ Orrin had put his hand on it at seven and felt nothing at all, and had been ashamed of feeling nothing, and had never told anyone.\n\nThe Calling lists had come down from the Comptroller-General's office four days ago, ten thousand accounts in order of depth, and had been divided among the halls. Each counter had been given four hundred. The instruction on each slate was the same, in the Comptroller-General's own neat hand: _Verify. Every balance, every entry, from the last birth to the present hour. No account may be Called in error. The Count is just._\n\nOrrin had three hundred and twelve still to do. He was on Hask.","wordCount":465,"createdAt":1793146319668,"updatedAt":1793146319668},{"id":"tEVgek3t6kDFDbcdzt4cT","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"nMF2Eceln_s4_fNZVUL2s","text":"Brightwater Recovery had rented an office on the station's third ring, behind a door with the firm's name freshly lettered on frosted glass. Quennell Haste was waiting for him inside it.\n\nCorvin had met Haste twice before, both times in the firm's tower on Kessane, both times across a table with a great deal of polished wood on it. Haste was in his forties, slim and pale and beautifully kept, with the kind of patience that cost money to acquire. He was a junior partner. At Brightwater that meant he owned a slice of the firm and the firm owned the rest of him; Corvin had never been sure which part was talking.\n\n\"Mr. Saal. Good of you to come so far.\"\n\n\"I wasn't given the choice, Mr. Haste.\"\n\n\"No,\" Haste agreed pleasantly. \"But it was good of you all the same. Sit. You'll have read the file.\"\n\nCorvin sat. He had read the file. He had read it on the packet from Kessane, six weeks of it, and then read it again, and the more he read it the less he liked it, which was usually a sign that it was a good one.\n\nIn 2296 the Halden-Ash Company had opened the Sabra Concern: an ice-mining and fuel-refining colony beyond a newly charted throat, worked by indentured labour. Ninety-one years later, in 2387, the throat had entered a closing phase and Halden-Ash had withdrawn its staff. Its indentured workers, nine thousand of them, had been left behind with their contracts held in trust. Two years after that, Halden-Ash had failed. Its assets had been broken up and sold. Most of them were worthless, because most of them were on the far side of a dead throat. One of the worthless assets — the Sabra Concern's receivables, which was to say the debt of nine thousand people who could not be reached and might not be alive — had passed through the hands of nineteen lien-houses in seven centuries, at prices that fell to almost nothing and then, as the throat-watchers began to publish their numbers, rose again. Brightwater had bought it eleven years ago.\n\n\"You've seen the figure,\" said Haste.\n\n\"I have.\"\n\n\"Say it, would you? I like to hear it said.\"\n\nCorvin said it. It was a number with more digits than the population of the Concord. It was what nine thousand people's air and water and heat and housing came to, at the Company's 2387 rates, plus seven hundred and two years of compound interest at the rate written into the contracts.\n\n\"Of course nobody expects to collect it,\" said Haste. \"That number is a legal fiction. What matters is the instrument. Under the Perpetual Instruments Act, a debt held in trust by a Concord company, over persons under that company's contract, attaches to those persons and their issue for the life of the instrument. The instrument has no term. Halden-Ash wrote it so. Which means, Mr. Saal, that if there is anyone alive on the far side of that throat — anyone at all who is descended from a Halden-Ash indenture — then the moment we come through, Brightwater holds their contracts.\"\n\n\"And if there's nobody alive?\"\n\n\"Then we have a rock, a fusion lamp, a refinery, and first title to a system nobody else has a claim on.\" Haste smiled. \"Brightwater wins either way. That's why we bought it.\"\n\n\"And what do you need an adjuster for?\"\n\n\"Because if there are people,\" Haste said, \"someone has to go and stand in front of them and explain.\" He leaned back. \"You're very good at that. I've read your file too. Eleven years on the colony circuit. Four hundred and twelve settlements. A very low rate of incident.\"\n\nCorvin said nothing. A low rate of incident meant that when he stood in front of people and explained, they mostly didn't kill him. It meant he was good at the voice. The calm, sorry, unmovable voice of the man who has come for what is owed and will not leave without it.\n\n\"There's one more thing,\" said Haste, \"and then I'll let you go and find your berth. Your bond.\"\n\n\"I know what my bond is.\"\n\n\"I know you do. I'd like you to know what it could be.\" Haste took a slip from his pocket and laid it on the desk between them and turned it round with one finger so that Corvin could read it. It was a discharge. Fully drafted. It wanted only a partner's seal. \"Your father's water debt was sold to Brightwater when you were nine. You have been working it off for thirty-five years. At your current rate you'll be clear of it at seventy-one. If you bring us the Sabra Concern — the instrument, enforced, on a living population, or clean title to the system if there isn't one — this is yours. That's not a promise. It's a term. It's in your assignment letter.\"\n\nCorvin looked at the slip for a long moment.\n\nHe thought of his father, who had signed him over in a water-office on Kessane with his hand shaking, and had died four years later, and whose face Corvin could no longer quite remember. He thought of the colony circuit, the four hundred and twelve settlements, the faces he could remember.\n\n\"I'll need to see the Company's records,\" he said. \"Everything that came through before the Closing. And someone who can read them.\"\n\n\"You'll have both,\" said Haste. \"The records are aboard. And the someone—\" he glanced at the frosted door, where a shape had just stopped, hesitating, with something large on a trolley— \"is, I think, here.\"","wordCount":941,"createdAt":1793146023842,"updatedAt":1793146023842},{"id":"u623PrU0xW8CayMT3A_we","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"CimEAn-7R7WEXiipp9rpn","text":"The Hook was an iron bracket the length of a man's arm, black with age, drilled into the rock wall of the Dock Ring above the inner lock. There was nothing on it. There had been nothing on it for three hundred and eleven years. It was the only empty thing on Lantern that nobody had ever tried to fill.\n\nUnder it there was a square, as much of a square as the curve of the rock allowed, and the square was full. Gallery Twenty-Three had come for Wim Pask, and so had a good part of Twenty-Two and Twenty-Four, and a crowd of dockhands from the Salt Docks aft along the ring, and the noodle-sellers, and children who had nothing better to do. Tamsin got her mother onto the steps at the side where she could lean on the wall, and Jory slid off into the crowd at once, and Tamsin stood with her hand on her mother's shoulder and watched Pask's granddaughter go up to the Hook.\n\nShe was a young woman, younger than Tamsin, with a baby on her hip and a toddler holding her trouser leg. One of the old men handed her the bar. It was a length of iron pipe, worn smooth and dark at the gripping end by three hundred years of hands. The woman stood under the Hook and looked up at the empty bracket and, after a moment, with the baby on her hip, she swung the bar up and struck it.\n\nThere was no bell. There had not been a bell since the Closing. What there was, was the iron Hook ringing against iron, a hard flat clang that went out across the square and along the ring and was swallowed by the rock. It was a poor sound. It was not meant to be anything else. Everybody in the square stood still for it, and touched their chits, and said, under their breath, \"Owing and owed.\"\n\n\"Owing and owed,\" said Agathe, and coughed.\n\nThe young woman handed back the bar. Nobody would cut Wim Pask's name into anything. There was nothing to cut it into. His chit would go into the woman's own tin, on Nineteen, beside her mother's and her father's, and his balance would go onto her account, and the baby on her hip would carry a part of it from the day she got her own chit at six. That was all. That was what a death was, on the Owing side of Lantern.\n\nTamsin had always thought the Hook was the saddest thing on the rock. She'd said so once, when she was small, and her grandmother had told her not to be soft. _The Company took the bell when they ran_, her grandmother had said. _They took the bell and every name on it. We ring the Hook so we remember there was a bell, and there were names, and somebody took them. That's not sad. That's angry. Get it right._","wordCount":497,"createdAt":1793145782169,"updatedAt":1793145782169},{"id":"umZFgYsU6gOfR70JmzpvH","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"ssvpvVWG-PUr4fVLVNAEq","text":"From the observation gallery of Port Ambry you could see the Throat begin to heal, if you knew where to look and were prepared to look for a long time.\n\nCorvin Saal had been told this by four different people since he came off the packet that morning, and he had been told it the way people on frontier stations told you things, as if they had personally arranged it. Port Ambry was a station that had waited three hundred years for one event. It had been built, at enormous expense, by a consortium of lien-houses and throat-speculators who had calculated that the Sabra Throat must heal some time in the thirtieth or thirty-first century and wanted to be first through it when it did. The consortium had gone bankrupt twice. The station had been sold, sublet, mortgaged, half abandoned, reoccupied. Generations of people had been born on it, grown old on it, and died on it, staring at a patch of black sky where nothing happened. And now something was happening, and the whole of Port Ambry was drunk on it.\n\nHe found a space at the gallery rail between a woman selling hot noodles from a tray and a priest of some throat-cult in orange, and looked.\n\nAt first there was nothing. Stars. The faint brown smear of a dust lane. Then, as his eyes learned the sky, a place in the middle of it where the stars were wrong: a little smeared, a little doubled, as if seen through old glass. And in the centre of the wrongness, so faint it might have been his own pulse in his eyes, a colour. Not a light. A colour, a sort of bruised pink, coming and going.\n\n\"She's breathing,\" said the noodle woman, with satisfaction. \"Four months ago you couldn't see a thing. Now look at her.\"\n\n\"When will it open?\"\n\n\"Open? It's open. You could put a probe through it today and it'd come out the other side in a hundred bits.\" She handed a carton to someone over his shoulder and took a coin. \"A ship? Three weeks. The throat-men say three weeks. That's when the lips settle. And then—\" she spread her hands, taking in the gallery, the station, the long docks below them where Corvin had counted eleven ships under refit when he came in— \"then it's the Rush.\"\n\n\"Don't call it the Rush,\" said the priest in orange, mildly. \"It's the Return.\"\n\n\"It's the Rush, Father, and you've got a berth on the _Saint Ishmael_ same as everybody.\"\n\nThe priest smiled and looked at the bruised place in the sky and said nothing. Corvin looked at it too. He tried to feel something about it. Seven hundred and two years. On the far side of that patch of wrongness there was a star called Sabra, and a gas giant, and a rock, and once there had been nine thousand people on the rock, and after the Throat shut nobody in the Concord had heard a word from them since.\n\nHe felt, mostly, the weight of the case in his hand, and in it the instrument he had been sent here to enforce.","wordCount":528,"createdAt":1793146023896,"updatedAt":1793146023896},{"id":"x1EWCQH8QOkSMQaQOyvx9","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"_Y2sOqdha0PcXlBvivyWT","text":"There were three of them in the end, because three was what the Director's office would admit. Dagny for the Wick crews. Mariam Oyelowo for the strip-farms, a broad, slow-spoken woman with soil in the creases of her knuckles that no scrubbing would shift. And Ezra Hesketh for the pumps and stills, who was seventy and had been a Speaker longer than Dagny had been alive, and who walked with a stick and had told her on the way up, in the lift, that he expected nothing and would be disappointed anyway.\n\nThe Counting House sat at the fore end of Lantern, at the root of the Wick, where the spin was least and the light was best. It had been built first, before the Galleries, before the farms; there was a story that the Company's surveyors had lived in it for two years while the rest of the rock was hollowed out round them. Its lower floors were the vault, where the Tally ran, and nobody Dagny knew had ever seen them. Its upper floors were offices. The top one was the Director's, and it had a window.\n\nDagny had seen the window from below all her life. It was a single long pane, curving, set into the face of the Counting House so that it looked straight down the length of Lantern, along the Wick, to the dark aft end of the rock eleven kilometres away. From the Galleries it looked like a strip of mirror. From inside it was the whole world, spread out and lit and upside down above you, every terrace and roof and tank and farm, and the Wick blazing down the middle of it like a sword.\n\nShe stood at it for a moment while they waited, because she couldn't help it. Gallery Six was a speck. She found it by the line of the water mains. Somewhere in that speck Pim was at school.\n\n\"It's a good view,\" said a voice behind her. \"I've never got used to it.\"\n\nIt was the Comptroller. Basu. A small, neat, brown man of fifty or so, in a Company suit that had been well cut once and was now a little big for him, as though he had lost weight in a hurry. He stood a careful distance from the glass.\n\n\"You don't like heights,\" Dagny said.\n\n\"No.\" He smiled, briefly. \"An unfortunate thing on Lantern. Everything is a height.\" He looked at her, and then away, and she had the odd impression that he wanted to say something else and had decided against it.\n\nThen the inner door opened and the Director came in, with Hale Varga behind her, and they all sat down.","wordCount":450,"createdAt":1793146166636,"updatedAt":1793146166636},{"id":"xrySoxJIxKbbPxC0x8-P2","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"a1u58gr6AFx6j7nnj_pXE","text":"On the top floor of the Counting House, behind the only clean window on Lantern, Teodor Basu, Comptroller of the Sabra Concern, watched the feed from the Gallery Six canteen camera with the sound off. He watched the woman on the table. He watched her hold up her wrist.\n\nHe ran her number. Ostrova, Dagny. Wick crews. Indentured nineteen years. Balance minus six hundred and eighty-one, including a tampering fee of two hundred posted last night at Lock Nine-Forty.\n\nHis hand, on the desk, was shaking. He put the other one over it until it stopped.\n\nThen he opened a file that was not on any list the Director had asked for, and looked for a long time at a number that was not a balance at all, and was nine hundred, and closed it again.","wordCount":136,"createdAt":1793145334007,"updatedAt":1793145334007}],"plotThreads":[],"motifs":[],"continuitySuppressions":[],"writingLogs":[{"id":"obGvoCuNg2SvFddh616qo","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","date":"2026-10-28","words":32874,"createdAt":1793145781888,"updatedAt":1793147487593},{"id":"wg0crDNUkJBKFqJx83ah3","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","date":"2026-10-27","words":3803,"createdAt":1793145333989,"updatedAt":1793145334688}],"sceneRevisions":[{"id":"5NuGPt1mApNNHutJ4ZF_M","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"e2oBYFVEZ5GlPkKSEnkkB","text":"The call came down the spine at the end of second shift, when Dagny Ostrova was forty metres up the Wick with her face in a collimator housing and her knees locked round a rung that had been warm for ninety years.\n\n\"Gallery Nine,\" said the speaker on her collar. It was Joss, below her on the crawl, and his voice had the flatness it got when something was already too late and he hadn't decided to know it yet. \"Lock Nine-Forty. Man in it. It's gone to recovery.\"\n\nShe was moving before he finished. You did not think about a lock in recovery. You thought about it afterwards, at the bell, with a drink in you.\n\nGoing down the Wick was going down only in the sense that the rock's spin pulled you, gently at first and then less gently, toward the curved floor of the world. At the axis there was no weight at all, only the lamp: three kilometres of magnetic bottle and shielding and slow white fire, with the crawls and catwalks wrapped round it like ivy round a pole. Its light fell on everything. It fell on the terraced Galleries that climbed the inner wall in every direction, on the water tanks and the strip-farms and the tin roofs, on the long seam of the Upper Wards where the Company people lived with real glass in their windows. On Lantern you could look up from anywhere and see someone's house above you, upside down, lit from the side by the same lamp that lit yours. Children were taught early not to drop things from high terraces. It was the first rule. The second was to watch your balance.\n\nDagny dropped through the spoke-shaft hand over hand, letting her weight build, and hit the Gallery Nine landing at a run with Joss three seconds behind her, wheezing. A crowd had already made a half-ring round Lock Nine-Forty. Nobody was touching it. On Lantern, nobody touched a lock in recovery, because the Tally logged who touched what.\n\nThe lock was a steel bottle three metres long, set between the Gallery Nine pressure zone and the Gallery Ten one, so that if either breached the other would hold. You walked into it, the door behind you sealed, the Tally read the chit on your wrist, and if you were in credit the door in front of you opened. If you were not, it didn't. And because the Company had built Lantern on the principle that air was the dearest thing in the Sabra system and must be recovered wherever it could be, a lock with an unauthorised occupant began, after ninety seconds, to draw its air back into the main for reclamation.\n\nLemuel Adeyemi was in the lock. Dagny knew him a little. Pump-fitter, Gallery Ten. Fifties. A cough. A wife who had died two winters back of the same cough, and whose name was on the bell. He was standing with both palms flat on the inner door's window, and his mouth was open, and his breath was making less fog on the glass than it should have.\n\n\"How long?\" Dagny said.\n\n\"Two minutes in recovery,\" somebody said. \"His shift got docked. Pump fault on Ten, they docked the whole crew a half-day. It posted on the hour. He was already in the lock when it posted.\"\n\nDagny looked at the panel by the door. The little screen showed his number and his balance, in the Company's blue, and the balance was in brackets, which was how the Tally wrote a debt it had stopped being patient with. Minus nine hundred and four. The dimming line on Lantern was minus nine hundred.\n\nFour. He was four over the line. Four was a meal. Four was an hour of water.\n\n\"Transfer,\" she said, and slapped her own chit onto the panel. \"I'll stand it. Transfer from Ostrova, nine-oh-six-two-two, to—\"\n\nThe panel chimed politely. TRANSFERS BETWEEN ACCOUNTS POST AT THE HOUR. PLEASE SEE A COUNTER.\n\n\"There's no counter on Nine at shift-end,\" Joss said behind her. \"There's no counter on Nine ever.\"\n\n\"Then get me the override.\"\n\n\"Dag. The override's in the Counting House.\"\n\nShe knew that. Everyone knew that. She said it anyway because it was the next thing to say, and because the alternative was to stand and watch, and the crowd was already standing and watching. Inside the lock Lemuel had seen her. He took one hand off the glass and made a small shape with it, a sort of apology, as if he were sorry to be making a scene.\n\n\"Torch,\" said Dagny.\n\n\"It's a pressure door. It'll take—\"\n\n\"Torch, Joss.\"\n\nHe had one on his belt, because Wick crews carried everything. She lit it and put the flame to the hinge side of the outer door and the steel went cherry-red and then white and began, very slowly, to run. The crowd made a noise behind her. The panel spoke again, in the same tone: TAMPERING WITH COMPANY PRESSURE EQUIPMENT IS CHARGED AT TWO HUNDRED CREDITS PER INCIDENT TO THE ACCOUNT OF THE TAMPERER. It read her chit through her glove. She watched her own balance drop by two hundred in the corner of the screen and kept cutting.\n\nIt took four minutes. A man can live for four minutes in a lock in recovery; Dagny had seen it. What a man cannot do is live for four minutes in a lock in recovery after he has already been in it for three. By the time the hinge gave and Joss got a pry-bar into the gap and the two of them hauled the door back on its seal, there was nothing in the lock but cold, thin air, rushing out past them into the gallery with a sound like a long breath let go, and Lemuel Adeyemi on his knees with his forehead against the inner door, as though he had decided at the end to pray to it.\n\nDagny went in and turned him over. His eyes were open. His lips were the colour of the shadow under a terrace. She put two fingers on his neck because she had been trained to and because people were watching, and then she took them away.\n\nBehind her the panel on the wall chimed a third time. Lemuel's number went grey, and under it, small and blue and patient, the Tally posted his last entry: ACCOUNT CLOSED BY DEATH. BALANCE TRANSFERRED TO ESTATE. And then, because Lemuel had no estate but a cough and a bunk and a wife's name on a bell, it posted the next one, which Dagny had seen a hundred times and which never stopped being the worst thing she had ever read: ESTATE INSOLVENT. BALANCE TRANSFERRED TO NEXT OF KIN.\n\n\"Who's his kin?\" she said.\n\nNobody answered. Then a girl at the front of the crowd, eight or nine, with a chit on a cord round her neck and her hair cut short against lice, said, \"Me.\"\n\n## Next of Kin\n[\nPlace: #The Galleries\nCharacters: @@Dagny Ostrova @@Joss Teague\nWhen: same day\n]\n\nJoss sat on the landing with the torch in his lap and wept the way big men did on Lantern, quietly and with his face turned to the wall. Dagny did not weep. She went to the girl, whose name was Abi and who was Lemuel's sister's child, and she knelt so that their faces were level, and she looked at the chit.\n\nMinus nine hundred and four, plus the girl's own, which was the ordinary minus three hundred that a Lantern child had by eight from being fed and housed and breathed. Minus twelve hundred and four. Three hundred over the line. The girl's door would be dimmed by morning. Her ration would stop at the next posting.\n\n\"You've got people on Ten?\"\n\n\"My aunt's at the farms. She's on nights.\"\n\n\"Go to her. Tell her the Wick crews will stand it. Tell her Dagny Ostrova said. Tomorrow I'll put it through a counter.\"\n\nThe girl looked at her with the flat, measuring look of every Lantern child who had already learned that adults said things. Then she went.\n\n\"You haven't got twelve hundred,\" said Joss, when she'd gone.\n\n\"The crews have.\"\n\n\"The crews have got a hole in the bottom of the bucket same as you, Dag.\"\n\n\"Then we'll stand it with the hole.\" She stood. Her knees hurt. Her balance, in the corner of her own eye, was smaller by two hundred for a door that had not saved anyone. \"Get him covered. I'll take his name to Odile.\"\n\n[\nPlace: #The Dock Ring\nCharacters: @@Dagny Ostrova @@Odile Marchbank @@Pim Ostrova @@Kas Ostrova\nItems: ^The Mercy Bell\nWhen: same day\n]\n\nThe bell of the _Ardent Mercy_ hung at the inner lock of the Dock Ring, on a hook of black iron that had been drilled into the rock the year Lantern was first pressurised. It was not large. It came up to Dagny's hip. It had been cast in the shipyards at Tannet Reach for a colony carrier that had crossed the Throat ninety-one years before, and the carrier had been cut up for her hull plate within a decade of arrival, as such ships always were; but the bell had been kept, because it was bad luck to melt a ship's bell, and because somebody on that first crossing had started the habit of ringing it for the dead.\n\nThere had been thirty-one dead on the crossing. Their names were cut round the lip in a neat, old-fashioned hand. After them came Lantern's dead, in a hand that changed every generation as the dockmasters did, round the lip and up the waist and onto the shoulder and in among the decoration on the crown, so that the bronze was now more letter than metal. From the length of the lock you could not see the names. You could only see that the bell was a strange colour, a dull brown furred all over with scratches, like an old animal.\n\nOdile Marchbank was waiting for her. She had heard; everyone on Lantern heard everything inside an hour, it was the one thing the rock did faster than the Tally. She was seventy-four and three-quarters deaf and she had kept the bell for thirty years, and her hands, which shook when she held a cup, went still as stone when they held the engraver.\n\n\"Adeyemi,\" Dagny said. \"Lemuel. Ten.\"\n\nOdile nodded. She didn't need the spelling; she had cut his wife. She took the bell-rope, which was a length of braided hauler cable, and she rang the bell once.\n\nIt was not a loud sound. Lantern swallowed noise; the rock was too big and too full of air and machinery. But the bell had a voice that went a long way anyway, low and a little sour, and all along the Dock Ring people stopped what they were doing at the sound of it. Hauler crews in the gantries. Customs men at their desks. The women who ran the noodle-stalls along the ring road. They stopped and stood, a breath's worth, and then went on.\n\nDagny felt a small hand slide into hers. She didn't look down.\n\n\"You're supposed to be at school,\" she said.\n\n\"School finished,\" said Pim. \"Everyone came out when they heard the lock. Mister Okonkwo said it was a teaching moment.\" He was twelve, and thin, and he collected bolts, and he had her husband's eyebrows, which she had never got used to. \"Was it Mister Adeyemi from Ten?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Did you try?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe thought about that. \"How much was he over?\"\n\n\"Four.\"\n\nPim did not say anything else. He held her hand while Odile fitted the engraver's burr and bent to the bronze, and the thin whine of it went up into the ring, and the first letters of Lemuel's name came up bright on the shoulder of the bell, among nine decades of brown.\n\n\"I'm running out of bell,\" Odile said, without looking up. She said it every few months. It was a sort of joke. \"One day I'll have to start on the hook.\"","wordCount":2030,"createdAt":1793145334622},{"id":"93IylJn-MVYi5wYRqfsA7","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"e2oBYFVEZ5GlPkKSEnkkB","text":"The call came down the spine at the end of second shift, when Dagny Ostrova was forty metres up the Wick with her face in a collimator housing and her knees locked round a rung that had been warm for ninety years.\n\n\"Gallery Nine,\" said the speaker on her collar. It was Joss, below her on the crawl, and his voice had the flatness it got when something was already too late and he hadn't decided to know it yet. \"Lock Nine-Forty. Man in it. It's gone to recovery.\"\n\nShe was moving before he finished. You did not think about a lock in recovery. You thought about it afterwards, at the bell, with a drink in you.\n\nGoing down the Wick was going down only in the sense that the rock's spin pulled you, gently at first and then less gently, toward the curved floor of the world. At the axis there was no weight at all, only the lamp: three kilometres of magnetic bottle and shielding and slow white fire, with the crawls and catwalks wrapped round it like ivy round a pole. Its light fell on everything. It fell on the terraced Galleries that climbed the inner wall in every direction, on the water tanks and the strip-farms and the tin roofs, on the long seam of the Upper Wards where the Company people lived with real glass in their windows. On Lantern you could look up from anywhere and see someone's house above you, upside down, lit from the side by the same lamp that lit yours. Children were taught early not to drop things from high terraces. It was the first rule. The second was to watch your balance.\n\nDagny dropped through the spoke-shaft hand over hand, letting her weight build, and hit the Gallery Nine landing at a run with Joss three seconds behind her, wheezing. A crowd had already made a half-ring round Lock Nine-Forty. Nobody was touching it. On Lantern, nobody touched a lock in recovery, because the Tally logged who touched what.\n\nThe lock was a steel bottle three metres long, set between the Gallery Nine pressure zone and the Gallery Ten one, so that if either breached the other would hold. You walked into it, the door behind you sealed, the Tally read the chit on your wrist, and if you were in credit the door in front of you opened. If you were not, it didn't. And because the Company had built Lantern on the principle that air was the dearest thing in the Sabra system and must be recovered wherever it could be, a lock with an unauthorised occupant began, after ninety seconds, to draw its air back into the main for reclamation.\n\nLemuel Adeyemi was in the lock. Dagny knew him a little. Pump-fitter, Gallery Ten. Fifties. A cough. A wife who had died two winters back of the same cough, and whose name was on the bell. He was standing with both palms flat on the inner door's window, and his mouth was open, and his breath was making less fog on the glass than it should have.\n\n\"How long?\" Dagny said.\n\n\"Two minutes in recovery,\" somebody said. \"His shift got docked. Pump fault on Ten, they docked the whole crew a half-day. It posted on the hour. He was already in the lock when it posted.\"\n\nDagny looked at the panel by the door. The little screen showed his number and his balance, in the Company's blue, and the balance was in brackets, which was how the Tally wrote a debt it had stopped being patient with. Minus nine hundred and four. The dimming line on Lantern was minus nine hundred.\n\nFour. He was four over the line. Four was a meal. Four was an hour of water.\n\n\"Transfer,\" she said, and slapped her own chit onto the panel. \"I'll stand it. Transfer from Ostrova, nine-oh-six-two-two, to—\"\n\nThe panel chimed politely. TRANSFERS BETWEEN ACCOUNTS POST AT THE HOUR. PLEASE SEE A COUNTER.\n\n\"There's no counter on Nine at shift-end,\" Joss said behind her. \"There's no counter on Nine ever.\"\n\n\"Then get me the override.\"\n\n\"Dag. The override's in the Counting House.\"\n\nShe knew that. Everyone knew that. She said it anyway because it was the next thing to say, and because the alternative was to stand and watch, and the crowd was already standing and watching. Inside the lock Lemuel had seen her. He took one hand off the glass and made a small shape with it, a sort of apology, as if he were sorry to be making a scene.\n\n\"Torch,\" said Dagny.\n\n\"It's a pressure door. It'll take—\"\n\n\"Torch, Joss.\"\n\nHe had one on his belt, because Wick crews carried everything. She lit it and put the flame to the hinge side of the outer door and the steel went cherry-red and then white and began, very slowly, to run. The crowd made a noise behind her. The panel spoke again, in the same tone: TAMPERING WITH COMPANY PRESSURE EQUIPMENT IS CHARGED AT TWO HUNDRED CREDITS PER INCIDENT TO THE ACCOUNT OF THE TAMPERER. It read her chit through her glove. She watched her own balance drop by two hundred in the corner of the screen and kept cutting.\n\nIt took four minutes. A man can live for four minutes in a lock in recovery; Dagny had seen it. What a man cannot do is live for four minutes in a lock in recovery after he has already been in it for three. By the time the hinge gave and Joss got a pry-bar into the gap and the two of them hauled the door back on its seal, there was nothing in the lock but cold, thin air, rushing out past them into the gallery with a sound like a long breath let go, and Lemuel Adeyemi on his knees with his forehead against the inner door, as though he had decided at the end to pray to it.\n\nDagny went in and turned him over. His eyes were open. His lips were the colour of the shadow under a terrace. She put two fingers on his neck because she had been trained to and because people were watching, and then she took them away.\n\nBehind her the panel on the wall chimed a third time. Lemuel's number went grey, and under it, small and blue and patient, the Tally posted his last entry: ACCOUNT CLOSED BY DEATH. BALANCE TRANSFERRED TO ESTATE. And then, because Lemuel had no estate but a cough and a bunk and a wife's name on a bell, it posted the next one, which Dagny had seen a hundred times and which never stopped being the worst thing she had ever read: ESTATE INSOLVENT. BALANCE TRANSFERRED TO NEXT OF KIN.\n\n\"Who's his kin?\" she said.\n\nNobody answered. Then a girl at the front of the crowd, eight or nine, with a chit on a cord round her neck and her hair cut short against lice, said, \"Me.\"\n\n## Next of Kin\n[\nPlace: #The Galleries\nCharacters: @@Dagny Ostrova @@Joss Teague\nWhen: same day\n]\n\nJoss sat on the landing with the torch in his lap and wept the way big men did on Lantern, quietly and with his face turned to the wall. Dagny did not weep. She went to the girl, whose name was Abi and who was Lemuel's sister's child, and she knelt so that their faces were level, and she looked at the chit.\n\nMinus nine hundred and four, plus the girl's own, which was the ordinary minus three hundred that a Lantern child had by eight from being fed and housed and breathed. Minus twelve hundred and four. Three hundred over the line. The girl's door would be dimmed by morning. Her ration would stop at the next posting.\n\n\"You've got people on Ten?\"\n\n\"My aunt's at the farms. She's on nights.\"\n\n\"Go to her. Tell her the Wick crews will stand it. Tell her Dagny Ostrova said. Tomorrow I'll put it through a counter.\"\n\nThe girl looked at her with the flat, measuring look of every Lantern child who had already learned that adults said things. Then she went.\n\n\"You haven't got twelve hundred,\" said Joss, when she'd gone.\n\n\"The crews have.\"\n\n\"The crews have got a hole in the bottom of the bucket same as you, Dag.\"\n\n\"Then we'll stand it with the hole.\" She stood. Her knees hurt. Her balance, in the corner of her own eye, was smaller by two hundred for a door that had not saved anyone. \"Get him covered. I'll take his name to Odile.\"\n\n## The Bell\n[\nPlace: #The Dock Ring\nCharacters: @@Dagny Ostrova @@Odile Marchbank @@Pim Ostrova @@Kas Ostrova\nItems: ^The Mercy Bell\nWhen: same day\n]\n\nThe bell of the _Ardent Mercy_ hung at the inner lock of the Dock Ring, on a hook of black iron that had been drilled into the rock the year Lantern was first pressurised. It was not large. It came up to Dagny's hip. It had been cast in the shipyards at Tannet Reach for a colony carrier that had crossed the Throat ninety-one years before, and the carrier had been cut up for her hull plate within a decade of arrival, as such ships always were; but the bell had been kept, because it was bad luck to melt a ship's bell, and because somebody on that first crossing had started the habit of ringing it for the dead.\n\nThere had been thirty-one dead on the crossing. Their names were cut round the lip in a neat, old-fashioned hand. After them came Lantern's dead, in a hand that changed every generation as the dockmasters did, round the lip and up the waist and onto the shoulder and in among the decoration on the crown, so that the bronze was now more letter than metal. From the length of the lock you could not see the names. You could only see that the bell was a strange colour, a dull brown furred all over with scratches, like an old animal.\n\nOdile Marchbank was waiting for her. She had heard; everyone on Lantern heard everything inside an hour, it was the one thing the rock did faster than the Tally. She was seventy-four and three-quarters deaf and she had kept the bell for thirty years, and her hands, which shook when she held a cup, went still as stone when they held the engraver.\n\n\"Adeyemi,\" Dagny said. \"Lemuel. Ten.\"\n\nOdile nodded. She didn't need the spelling; she had cut his wife. She took the bell-rope, which was a length of braided hauler cable, and she rang the bell once.\n\nIt was not a loud sound. Lantern swallowed noise; the rock was too big and too full of air and machinery. But the bell had a voice that went a long way anyway, low and a little sour, and all along the Dock Ring people stopped what they were doing at the sound of it. Hauler crews in the gantries. Customs men at their desks. The women who ran the noodle-stalls along the ring road. They stopped and stood, a breath's worth, and then went on.\n\nDagny felt a small hand slide into hers. She didn't look down.\n\n\"You're supposed to be at school,\" she said.\n\n\"School finished,\" said Pim. \"Everyone came out when they heard the lock. Mister Okonkwo said it was a teaching moment.\" He was twelve, and thin, and he collected bolts, and he had her husband's eyebrows, which she had never got used to. \"Was it Mister Adeyemi from Ten?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Did you try?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe thought about that. \"How much was he over?\"\n\n\"Four.\"\n\nPim did not say anything else. He held her hand while Odile fitted the engraver's burr and bent to the bronze, and the thin whine of it went up into the ring, and the first letters of Lemuel's name came up bright on the shoulder of the bell, among nine decades of brown.\n\n\"I'm running out of bell,\" Odile said, without looking up. She said it every few months. It was a sort of joke. \"One day I'll have to start on the hook.\"\n\n## The Magpie Comes In\n[\nPlace: #The Dock Ring\nCharacters: @@Dagny Ostrova @@Pim Ostrova @@Kas Ostrova\nWhen: same day\n]\n\nThe _Magpie_ came in an hour later with eighty tonnes of Caul ice in her tank-frame and a crack in her forward window that had been there so long it had a name. Her pilot came through the inner lock with his helmet under his arm and the whole of the Dock Ring's gossip already on his face.\n\n\"Who?\"\n\n\"Adeyemi. Gallery Ten.\"\n\n\"Lock?\"\n\n\"Lock.\"\n\nKasimir Ostrova closed his eyes for a moment. He was nine years younger than his sister and twice as handsome and he owed money, at a conservative estimate, to a third of the Dock Ring, none of which ever seemed to cost him a night's sleep. But he had grown up in Gallery Six the same as her, and he had seen the same locks.\n\n\"Uncle Kas,\" said Pim, and was swept up and hung upside down by the ankles, which at twelve he was too old for and permitted anyway.\n\n\"How's the run?\" Dagny said.\n\n\"Cold. Long. The usual.\" Kas set Pim the right way up. \"Quiet out there, though. Quieter than it was.\"\n\n\"Quiet how?\"\n\n\"Caul Deep's still shut. You know they closed the Deep in the spring — the big shaft, the one that took nine hundred on a shift. Nobody's working it. Beacon's dark, gantries are up. Funny to fly past. Used to be you'd see the lights of it from a thousand klicks out like a city.\" He shrugged. \"They said the vein ran out. Sent all the Deep crews home through the Throat. Contracts bought out, every one. Lucky bastards.\"\n\n\"I never heard of the Company buying out a contract in my life,\" said Dagny.\n\n\"Well. You never heard of a vein running out, neither. It's a season of firsts.\" Kas grinned at Pim. \"Come on. I'll buy you a noodle. I've been paid.\"\n\n\"You've been paid and you owe Marguerite at the noodle stall forty.\"\n\n\"Then I'll buy him a noodle and her a smile, and we'll all be square.\" He put an arm round his sister's shoulders and squeezed, briefly, hard. \"Sorry about Lemuel, Dag.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" she said. \"Me too.\"\n\nBehind them, Odile's engraver whined on in the bronze.\n\n## Fifty-Two Days\n[\nPlace: #The Galleries\nCharacters: @@Dagny Ostrova @@Pim Ostrova @@Joss Teague @@Ruth Achterberg:phone\nItems: ^The Tally\nWhen: +1 day\n]\n\nThe announcement came at eleven the next morning, on every screen on Lantern at once.\n\nDagny was in the canteen on Gallery Six with Joss and two of the Wick crew, trying to work out how many hours the Speakers could find between them to stand the Adeyemi girl's debt, and getting a number that was not enough. The screen on the canteen wall was usually showing either the Company's safety notices or a football match from somewhere in the Concord that had been played eleven years before and brought through the Throat on a data-run. It went dark in the middle of a goal, and then it showed the Company's mark, the two letters H and A leaning on each other like tired men, and then it showed Ruth Achterberg.\n\nThe canteen went quiet in the way Lantern went quiet for the bell.\n\nThe Director was sitting at a desk with nothing on it. She was a lean, grey woman of sixty in a dark Company suit, and she wore at her throat, on a fine chain, the brass disk of the Director's seal, which every child on Lantern had been told was the key to everything. She did not look cruel. She looked like someone who had been awake all night doing arithmetic and had come out of it with an answer she disliked but would not apologise for.\n\n\"People of the Sabra Concern,\" she said. \"I am speaking to you today with news that affects every one of us.\n\n\"As you know, the Throat which connects this system to the Concord is not a fixed thing. It breathes. Its throat-watch station has measured that breathing for the whole of this colony's history. Three weeks ago the station recorded the beginning of what our physicists call a closing phase. Throats which enter a closing phase do not reopen on any timescale that is useful to us. The best estimate, from the Concord's own literature, is somewhere between six and eight hundred years.\n\n\"The Throat will remain passable for fifty-two days. After that, this system will be cut off.\"\n\nSomebody in the canteen laughed, the high bark of a person who has been hit. Nobody else made a sound.\n\n\"The Halden-Ash Company has a duty to its shareholders and to its staff,\" said the Director. \"It cannot maintain a concern which it cannot reach. It will therefore withdraw from the Sabra system before the Throat closes. The carrier _Covenant_, now at our fore dock, will carry essential staff and their families and such Company assets as can be moved. I have asked the department heads to prepare those lists.\n\n\"I want to speak directly to the indentured population of Lantern.\" She looked into the lens. Dagny would remember that afterwards: that she did not look away. \"Your contracts are held by the Company. The Company does not abandon what it holds. Your contracts will remain in force, in trust, for the full term of the closure, and will be honoured when the Throat reopens. Lantern's systems — its air, its water, its heat, its light — will continue to be administered by the Tally, which has kept you safe for ninety years and which will keep you safe after we are gone. The Tally does not need us. It needs only that you go on working, as you always have, and it will go on keeping you alive.\n\n\"I know that this is hard news. I wish that I had better. Thank you.\"\n\nThe screen went back to the Company's mark. Then, after a moment, to the football match, which had been paused at the instant before the goal and now went on as if nothing had happened, the ball crossing the line, the crowd eleven years ago and four hundred light-years away going wild.\n\n## Standing on a Table\n[\nPlace: #The Galleries\nCharacters: @@Dagny Ostrova @@Pim Ostrova @@Joss Teague\nWhen: same day\n]\n\nWhat happened in the canteen in the next ten minutes Dagny remembered afterwards only in pieces. A man on Gallery Six put his chair through the screen and the screen went on showing football in a web of cracks. Two women began to fight over nothing, over a bench, and had to be pulled apart. Old Mister Okonkwo the schoolteacher sat with his hands flat on the table, saying, \"Six hundred years, six hundred years,\" as if it were a sum that would come out differently if he said it often enough. Somebody shouted that the Company would take the fuel. Somebody shouted that the Company would take the water. Somebody shouted that they should go up to the Upper Wards now, all of them, and see how many lists the department heads had made when they had their own windows broken.\n\nAnd Pim was beside her, she didn't know when he had come in, saying, \"Mum. Mum. In trust. What's in trust mean?\"\n\nShe understood it, she realised, before she understood anything else. It was Lantern; you learned the Company's language before you learned your prayers. _Held in trust_ meant that the Company was leaving, and the debt was staying. It meant that every chit on every wrist on Lantern would go on counting down, hour by hour, against the air, for six hundred years, and that if the Throat ever opened again somebody in a Company suit would come through it with the books in their hand to see how much was owed. It meant that the Adeyemi girl's twelve hundred would be eighteen hundred by the time she was grown and nine thousand by the time she died, and would go to whoever came after her.\n\nIt meant that the lock on Gallery Nine would go on doing what it had done to Lemuel, with nobody even in the Counting House to blame for it.\n\nShe found she was standing on a table. She did not remember getting up there. The canteen was looking at her, the way the Wick crews looked at her when the lamp went wrong and someone had to say a number out loud.\n\n\"All right,\" Dagny said. Her voice came out level, which surprised her. \"All right. Shut up and listen. I'm a Speaker for the Wick crews, and from tonight the Wick crews are going to want to talk to the other crews. Pumps, farms, docks, stills. Everyone who wears a chit. Fifty-two days is a long time. It's long enough for the Company to pack. It's long enough for us to make it listen first.\"\n\n\"Listen to what?\" somebody shouted.\n\n\"To the one thing it hasn't said.\" She held up her wrist, with the chit on it, so that the canteen could see the blue numbers. \"She said the Tally keeps us alive. It doesn't. We keep it alive. We fed it ninety years. If the Company's going, the Company can take its ledger with it, or it can switch the bloody thing off before it goes. Either way, nobody on this rock is going to owe air to an empty office for six hundred years.\"\n\nThere was a silence. Then the man who had broken the screen began to clap, slowly, and others took it up; and Joss, below her, looked up with his wet red face and said, very quietly, so that only she could hear: \"Dag. You know what you just did.\"\n\nShe did. She had just volunteered.\n\n[\nPlace: #The Counting House\nCharacters: @@Teodor Basu\nWhen: same day\n]\n\nOn the top floor of the Counting House, behind the only clean window on Lantern, Teodor Basu, Comptroller of the Sabra Concern, watched the feed from the Gallery Six canteen camera with the sound off. He watched the woman on the table. He watched her hold up her wrist.\n\nHe ran her number. Ostrova, Dagny. Wick crews. Indentured nineteen years. Balance minus six hundred and eighty-one, including a tampering fee of two hundred posted last night at Lock Nine-Forty.\n\nHis hand, on the desk, was shaking. He put the other one over it until it stopped.\n\nThen he opened a file that was not on any list the Director had asked for, and looked for a long time at a number that was not a balance at all, and was nine hundred, and closed it again.","wordCount":3823,"createdAt":1793145334003},{"id":"MaMC8fahlujHTcQXtDprm","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"e2oBYFVEZ5GlPkKSEnkkB","text":"The call came down the spine at the end of second shift, when Dagny Ostrova was forty metres up the Wick with her face in a collimator housing and her knees locked round a rung that had been warm for ninety years.\n\n\"Gallery Nine,\" said the speaker on her collar. It was Joss, below her on the crawl, and his voice had the flatness it got when something was already too late and he hadn't decided to know it yet. \"Lock Nine-Forty. Man in it. It's gone to recovery.\"\n\nShe was moving before he finished. You did not think about a lock in recovery. You thought about it afterwards, at the bell, with a drink in you.\n\nGoing down the Wick was going down only in the sense that the rock's spin pulled you, gently at first and then less gently, toward the curved floor of the world. At the axis there was no weight at all, only the lamp: three kilometres of magnetic bottle and shielding and slow white fire, with the crawls and catwalks wrapped round it like ivy round a pole. Its light fell on everything. It fell on the terraced Galleries that climbed the inner wall in every direction, on the water tanks and the strip-farms and the tin roofs, on the long seam of the Upper Wards where the Company people lived with real glass in their windows. On Lantern you could look up from anywhere and see someone's house above you, upside down, lit from the side by the same lamp that lit yours. Children were taught early not to drop things from high terraces. It was the first rule. The second was to watch your balance.\n\nDagny dropped through the spoke-shaft hand over hand, letting her weight build, and hit the Gallery Nine landing at a run with Joss three seconds behind her, wheezing. A crowd had already made a half-ring round Lock Nine-Forty. Nobody was touching it. On Lantern, nobody touched a lock in recovery, because the Tally logged who touched what.\n\nThe lock was a steel bottle three metres long, set between the Gallery Nine pressure zone and the Gallery Ten one, so that if either breached the other would hold. You walked into it, the door behind you sealed, the Tally read the chit on your wrist, and if you were in credit the door in front of you opened. If you were not, it didn't. And because the Company had built Lantern on the principle that air was the dearest thing in the Sabra system and must be recovered wherever it could be, a lock with an unauthorised occupant began, after ninety seconds, to draw its air back into the main for reclamation.\n\nLemuel Adeyemi was in the lock. Dagny knew him a little. Pump-fitter, Gallery Ten. Fifties. A cough. A wife who had died two winters back of the same cough, and whose name was on the bell. He was standing with both palms flat on the inner door's window, and his mouth was open, and his breath was making less fog on the glass than it should have.\n\n\"How long?\" Dagny said.\n\n\"Two minutes in recovery,\" somebody said. \"His shift got docked. Pump fault on Ten, they docked the whole crew a half-day. It posted on the hour. He was already in the lock when it posted.\"\n\nDagny looked at the panel by the door. The little screen showed his number and his balance, in the Company's blue, and the balance was in brackets, which was how the Tally wrote a debt it had stopped being patient with. Minus nine hundred and four. The dimming line on Lantern was minus nine hundred.\n\nFour. He was four over the line. Four was a meal. Four was an hour of water.\n\n\"Transfer,\" she said, and slapped her own chit onto the panel. \"I'll stand it. Transfer from Ostrova, nine-oh-six-two-two, to—\"\n\nThe panel chimed politely. TRANSFERS BETWEEN ACCOUNTS POST AT THE HOUR. PLEASE SEE A COUNTER.\n\n\"There's no counter on Nine at shift-end,\" Joss said behind her. \"There's no counter on Nine ever.\"\n\n\"Then get me the override.\"\n\n\"Dag. The override's in the Counting House.\"\n\nShe knew that. Everyone knew that. She said it anyway because it was the next thing to say, and because the alternative was to stand and watch, and the crowd was already standing and watching. Inside the lock Lemuel had seen her. He took one hand off the glass and made a small shape with it, a sort of apology, as if he were sorry to be making a scene.\n\n\"Torch,\" said Dagny.\n\n\"It's a pressure door. It'll take—\"\n\n\"Torch, Joss.\"\n\nHe had one on his belt, because Wick crews carried everything. She lit it and put the flame to the hinge side of the outer door and the steel went cherry-red and then white and began, very slowly, to run. The crowd made a noise behind her. The panel spoke again, in the same tone: TAMPERING WITH COMPANY PRESSURE EQUIPMENT IS CHARGED AT TWO HUNDRED CREDITS PER INCIDENT TO THE ACCOUNT OF THE TAMPERER. It read her chit through her glove. She watched her own balance drop by two hundred in the corner of the screen and kept cutting.\n\nIt took four minutes. A man can live for four minutes in a lock in recovery; Dagny had seen it. What a man cannot do is live for four minutes in a lock in recovery after he has already been in it for three. By the time the hinge gave and Joss got a pry-bar into the gap and the two of them hauled the door back on its seal, there was nothing in the lock but cold, thin air, rushing out past them into the gallery with a sound like a long breath let go, and Lemuel Adeyemi on his knees with his forehead against the inner door, as though he had decided at the end to pray to it.\n\nDagny went in and turned him over. His eyes were open. His lips were the colour of the shadow under a terrace. She put two fingers on his neck because she had been trained to and because people were watching, and then she took them away.\n\nBehind her the panel on the wall chimed a third time. Lemuel's number went grey, and under it, small and blue and patient, the Tally posted his last entry: ACCOUNT CLOSED BY DEATH. BALANCE TRANSFERRED TO ESTATE. And then, because Lemuel had no estate but a cough and a bunk and a wife's name on a bell, it posted the next one, which Dagny had seen a hundred times and which never stopped being the worst thing she had ever read: ESTATE INSOLVENT. BALANCE TRANSFERRED TO NEXT OF KIN.\n\n\"Who's his kin?\" she said.\n\nNobody answered. Then a girl at the front of the crowd, eight or nine, with a chit on a cord round her neck and her hair cut short against lice, said, \"Me.\"\n\n## Next of Kin\n[\nPlace: #The Galleries\nCharacters: @@Dagny Ostrova @@Joss Teague\nWhen: same day\n]\n\nJoss sat on the landing with the torch in his lap and wept the way big men did on Lantern, quietly and with his face turned to the wall. Dagny did not weep. She went to the girl, whose name was Abi and who was Lemuel's sister's child, and she knelt so that their faces were level, and she looked at the chit.\n\nMinus nine hundred and four, plus the girl's own, which was the ordinary minus three hundred that a Lantern child had by eight from being fed and housed and breathed. Minus twelve hundred and four. Three hundred over the line. The girl's door would be dimmed by morning. Her ration would stop at the next posting.\n\n\"You've got people on Ten?\"\n\n\"My aunt's at the farms. She's on nights.\"\n\n\"Go to her. Tell her the Wick crews will stand it. Tell her Dagny Ostrova said. Tomorrow I'll put it through a counter.\"\n\nThe girl looked at her with the flat, measuring look of every Lantern child who had already learned that adults said things. Then she went.\n\n\"You haven't got twelve hundred,\" said Joss, when she'd gone.\n\n\"The crews have.\"\n\n\"The crews have got a hole in the bottom of the bucket same as you, Dag.\"\n\n\"Then we'll stand it with the hole.\" She stood. Her knees hurt. Her balance, in the corner of her own eye, was smaller by two hundred for a door that had not saved anyone. \"Get him covered. I'll take his name to Odile.\"\n\n## The Bell\n[\nPlace: #The Dock Ring\nCharacters: @@Dagny Ostrova @@Odile Marchbank @@Pim Ostrova @@Kas Ostrova\nItems: ^The Mercy Bell\nWhen: same day\n]\n\nThe bell of the _Ardent Mercy_ hung at the inner lock of the Dock Ring, on a hook of black iron that had been drilled into the rock the year Lantern was first pressurised. It was not large. It came up to Dagny's hip. It had been cast in the shipyards at Tannet Reach for a colony carrier that had crossed the Throat ninety-one years before, and the carrier had been cut up for her hull plate within a decade of arrival, as such ships always were; but the bell had been kept, because it was bad luck to melt a ship's bell, and because somebody on that first crossing had started the habit of ringing it for the dead.\n\nThere had been thirty-one dead on the crossing. Their names were cut round the lip in a neat, old-fashioned hand. After them came Lantern's dead, in a hand that changed every generation as the dockmasters did, round the lip and up the waist and onto the shoulder and in among the decoration on the crown, so that the bronze was now more letter than metal. From the length of the lock you could not see the names. You could only see that the bell was a strange colour, a dull brown furred all over with scratches, like an old animal.\n\nOdile Marchbank was waiting for her. She had heard; everyone on Lantern heard everything inside an hour, it was the one thing the rock did faster than the Tally. She was seventy-four and three-quarters deaf and she had kept the bell for thirty years, and her hands, which shook when she held a cup, went still as stone when they held the engraver.\n\n\"Adeyemi,\" Dagny said. \"Lemuel. Ten.\"\n\nOdile nodded. She didn't need the spelling; she had cut his wife. She took the bell-rope, which was a length of braided hauler cable, and she rang the bell once.\n\nIt was not a loud sound. Lantern swallowed noise; the rock was too big and too full of air and machinery. But the bell had a voice that went a long way anyway, low and a little sour, and all along the Dock Ring people stopped what they were doing at the sound of it. Hauler crews in the gantries. Customs men at their desks. The women who ran the noodle-stalls along the ring road. They stopped and stood, a breath's worth, and then went on.\n\nDagny felt a small hand slide into hers. She didn't look down.\n\n\"You're supposed to be at school,\" she said.\n\n\"School finished,\" said Pim. \"Everyone came out when they heard the lock. Mister Okonkwo said it was a teaching moment.\" He was twelve, and thin, and he collected bolts, and he had her husband's eyebrows, which she had never got used to. \"Was it Mister Adeyemi from Ten?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Did you try?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe thought about that. \"How much was he over?\"\n\n\"Four.\"\n\nPim did not say anything else. He held her hand while Odile fitted the engraver's burr and bent to the bronze, and the thin whine of it went up into the ring, and the first letters of Lemuel's name came up bright on the shoulder of the bell, among nine decades of brown.\n\n\"I'm running out of bell,\" Odile said, without looking up. She said it every few months. It was a sort of joke. \"One day I'll have to start on the hook.\"\n\n## The Magpie Comes In\n[\nPlace: #The Dock Ring\nCharacters: @@Dagny Ostrova @@Pim Ostrova @@Kas Ostrova\nWhen: same day\n]\n\nThe _Magpie_ came in an hour later with eighty tonnes of Caul ice in her tank-frame and a crack in her forward window that had been there so long it had a name. Her pilot came through the inner lock with his helmet under his arm and the whole of the Dock Ring's gossip already on his face.\n\n\"Who?\"\n\n\"Adeyemi. Gallery Ten.\"\n\n\"Lock?\"\n\n\"Lock.\"\n\nKasimir Ostrova closed his eyes for a moment. He was nine years younger than his sister and twice as handsome and he owed money, at a conservative estimate, to a third of the Dock Ring, none of which ever seemed to cost him a night's sleep. But he had grown up in Gallery Six the same as her, and he had seen the same locks.\n\n\"Uncle Kas,\" said Pim, and was swept up and hung upside down by the ankles, which at twelve he was too old for and permitted anyway.\n\n\"How's the run?\" Dagny said.\n\n\"Cold. Long. The usual.\" Kas set Pim the right way up. \"Quiet out there, though. Quieter than it was.\"\n\n\"Quiet how?\"\n\n\"Caul Deep's still shut. You know they closed the Deep in the spring — the big shaft, the one that took nine hundred on a shift. Nobody's working it. Beacon's dark, gantries are up. Funny to fly past. Used to be you'd see the lights of it from a thousand klicks out like a city.\" He shrugged. \"They said the vein ran out. Sent all the Deep crews home through the Throat. Contracts bought out, every one. Lucky bastards.\"\n\n\"I never heard of the Company buying out a contract in my life,\" said Dagny.\n\n\"Well. You never heard of a vein running out, neither. It's a season of firsts.\" Kas grinned at Pim. \"Come on. I'll buy you a noodle. I've been paid.\"\n\n\"You've been paid and you owe Marguerite at the noodle stall forty.\"\n\n\"Then I'll buy him a noodle and her a smile, and we'll all be square.\" He put an arm round his sister's shoulders and squeezed, briefly, hard. \"Sorry about Lemuel, Dag.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" she said. \"Me too.\"\n\nBehind them, Odile's engraver whined on in the bronze.\n\n[\nPlace: #The Galleries\nCharacters: @@Dagny Ostrova @@Pim Ostrova @@Joss Teague @@Ruth Achterberg:phone\nItems: ^The Tally\nWhen: +1 day\n]\n\nThe announcement came at eleven the next morning, on every screen on Lantern at once.\n\nDagny was in the canteen on Gallery Six with Joss and two of the Wick crew, trying to work out how many hours the Speakers could find between them to stand the Adeyemi girl's debt, and getting a number that was not enough. The screen on the canteen wall was usually showing either the Company's safety notices or a football match from somewhere in the Concord that had been played eleven years before and brought through the Throat on a data-run. It went dark in the middle of a goal, and then it showed the Company's mark, the two letters H and A leaning on each other like tired men, and then it showed Ruth Achterberg.\n\nThe canteen went quiet in the way Lantern went quiet for the bell.\n\nThe Director was sitting at a desk with nothing on it. She was a lean, grey woman of sixty in a dark Company suit, and she wore at her throat, on a fine chain, the brass disk of the Director's seal, which every child on Lantern had been told was the key to everything. She did not look cruel. She looked like someone who had been awake all night doing arithmetic and had come out of it with an answer she disliked but would not apologise for.\n\n\"People of the Sabra Concern,\" she said. \"I am speaking to you today with news that affects every one of us.\n\n\"As you know, the Throat which connects this system to the Concord is not a fixed thing. It breathes. Its throat-watch station has measured that breathing for the whole of this colony's history. Three weeks ago the station recorded the beginning of what our physicists call a closing phase. Throats which enter a closing phase do not reopen on any timescale that is useful to us. The best estimate, from the Concord's own literature, is somewhere between six and eight hundred years.\n\n\"The Throat will remain passable for fifty-two days. After that, this system will be cut off.\"\n\nSomebody in the canteen laughed, the high bark of a person who has been hit. Nobody else made a sound.\n\n\"The Halden-Ash Company has a duty to its shareholders and to its staff,\" said the Director. \"It cannot maintain a concern which it cannot reach. It will therefore withdraw from the Sabra system before the Throat closes. The carrier _Covenant_, now at our fore dock, will carry essential staff and their families and such Company assets as can be moved. I have asked the department heads to prepare those lists.\n\n\"I want to speak directly to the indentured population of Lantern.\" She looked into the lens. Dagny would remember that afterwards: that she did not look away. \"Your contracts are held by the Company. The Company does not abandon what it holds. Your contracts will remain in force, in trust, for the full term of the closure, and will be honoured when the Throat reopens. Lantern's systems — its air, its water, its heat, its light — will continue to be administered by the Tally, which has kept you safe for ninety years and which will keep you safe after we are gone. The Tally does not need us. It needs only that you go on working, as you always have, and it will go on keeping you alive.\n\n\"I know that this is hard news. I wish that I had better. Thank you.\"\n\nThe screen went back to the Company's mark. Then, after a moment, to the football match, which had been paused at the instant before the goal and now went on as if nothing had happened, the ball crossing the line, the crowd eleven years ago and four hundred light-years away going wild.","wordCount":3071,"createdAt":1793145334401},{"id":"RGiIevFhwkzS3Q5gZj6md","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"e2oBYFVEZ5GlPkKSEnkkB","text":"The call came down the spine at the end of second shift, when Dagny Ostrova was forty metres up the Wick with her face in a collimator housing and her knees locked round a rung that had been warm for ninety years.\n\n\"Gallery Nine,\" said the speaker on her collar. It was Joss, below her on the crawl, and his voice had the flatness it got when something was already too late and he hadn't decided to know it yet. \"Lock Nine-Forty. Man in it. It's gone to recovery.\"\n\nShe was moving before he finished. You did not think about a lock in recovery. You thought about it afterwards, at the bell, with a drink in you.\n\nGoing down the Wick was going down only in the sense that the rock's spin pulled you, gently at first and then less gently, toward the curved floor of the world. At the axis there was no weight at all, only the lamp: three kilometres of magnetic bottle and shielding and slow white fire, with the crawls and catwalks wrapped round it like ivy round a pole. Its light fell on everything. It fell on the terraced Galleries that climbed the inner wall in every direction, on the water tanks and the strip-farms and the tin roofs, on the long seam of the Upper Wards where the Company people lived with real glass in their windows. On Lantern you could look up from anywhere and see someone's house above you, upside down, lit from the side by the same lamp that lit yours. Children were taught early not to drop things from high terraces. It was the first rule. The second was to watch your balance.\n\nDagny dropped through the spoke-shaft hand over hand, letting her weight build, and hit the Gallery Nine landing at a run with Joss three seconds behind her, wheezing. A crowd had already made a half-ring round Lock Nine-Forty. Nobody was touching it. On Lantern, nobody touched a lock in recovery, because the Tally logged who touched what.\n\nThe lock was a steel bottle three metres long, set between the Gallery Nine pressure zone and the Gallery Ten one, so that if either breached the other would hold. You walked into it, the door behind you sealed, the Tally read the chit on your wrist, and if you were in credit the door in front of you opened. If you were not, it didn't. And because the Company had built Lantern on the principle that air was the dearest thing in the Sabra system and must be recovered wherever it could be, a lock with an unauthorised occupant began, after ninety seconds, to draw its air back into the main for reclamation.\n\nLemuel Adeyemi was in the lock. Dagny knew him a little. Pump-fitter, Gallery Ten. Fifties. A cough. A wife who had died two winters back of the same cough, and whose name was on the bell. He was standing with both palms flat on the inner door's window, and his mouth was open, and his breath was making less fog on the glass than it should have.\n\n\"How long?\" Dagny said.\n\n\"Two minutes in recovery,\" somebody said. \"His shift got docked. Pump fault on Ten, they docked the whole crew a half-day. It posted on the hour. He was already in the lock when it posted.\"\n\nDagny looked at the panel by the door. The little screen showed his number and his balance, in the Company's blue, and the balance was in brackets, which was how the Tally wrote a debt it had stopped being patient with. Minus nine hundred and four. The dimming line on Lantern was minus nine hundred.\n\nFour. He was four over the line. Four was a meal. Four was an hour of water.\n\n\"Transfer,\" she said, and slapped her own chit onto the panel. \"I'll stand it. Transfer from Ostrova, nine-oh-six-two-two, to—\"\n\nThe panel chimed politely. TRANSFERS BETWEEN ACCOUNTS POST AT THE HOUR. PLEASE SEE A COUNTER.\n\n\"There's no counter on Nine at shift-end,\" Joss said behind her. \"There's no counter on Nine ever.\"\n\n\"Then get me the override.\"\n\n\"Dag. The override's in the Counting House.\"\n\nShe knew that. Everyone knew that. She said it anyway because it was the next thing to say, and because the alternative was to stand and watch, and the crowd was already standing and watching. Inside the lock Lemuel had seen her. He took one hand off the glass and made a small shape with it, a sort of apology, as if he were sorry to be making a scene.\n\n\"Torch,\" said Dagny.\n\n\"It's a pressure door. It'll take—\"\n\n\"Torch, Joss.\"\n\nHe had one on his belt, because Wick crews carried everything. She lit it and put the flame to the hinge side of the outer door and the steel went cherry-red and then white and began, very slowly, to run. The crowd made a noise behind her. The panel spoke again, in the same tone: TAMPERING WITH COMPANY PRESSURE EQUIPMENT IS CHARGED AT TWO HUNDRED CREDITS PER INCIDENT TO THE ACCOUNT OF THE TAMPERER. It read her chit through her glove. She watched her own balance drop by two hundred in the corner of the screen and kept cutting.\n\nIt took four minutes. A man can live for four minutes in a lock in recovery; Dagny had seen it. What a man cannot do is live for four minutes in a lock in recovery after he has already been in it for three. By the time the hinge gave and Joss got a pry-bar into the gap and the two of them hauled the door back on its seal, there was nothing in the lock but cold, thin air, rushing out past them into the gallery with a sound like a long breath let go, and Lemuel Adeyemi on his knees with his forehead against the inner door, as though he had decided at the end to pray to it.\n\nDagny went in and turned him over. His eyes were open. His lips were the colour of the shadow under a terrace. She put two fingers on his neck because she had been trained to and because people were watching, and then she took them away.\n\nBehind her the panel on the wall chimed a third time. Lemuel's number went grey, and under it, small and blue and patient, the Tally posted his last entry: ACCOUNT CLOSED BY DEATH. BALANCE TRANSFERRED TO ESTATE. And then, because Lemuel had no estate but a cough and a bunk and a wife's name on a bell, it posted the next one, which Dagny had seen a hundred times and which never stopped being the worst thing she had ever read: ESTATE INSOLVENT. BALANCE TRANSFERRED TO NEXT OF KIN.\n\n\"Who's his kin?\" she said.\n\nNobody answered. Then a girl at the front of the crowd, eight or nine, with a chit on a cord round her neck and her hair cut short against lice, said, \"Me.\"\n\n## Next of Kin\n[\nPlace: #The Galleries\nCharacters: @@Dagny Ostrova @@Joss Teague\nWhen: same day\n]\n\nJoss sat on the landing with the torch in his lap and wept the way big men did on Lantern, quietly and with his face turned to the wall. Dagny did not weep. She went to the girl, whose name was Abi and who was Lemuel's sister's child, and she knelt so that their faces were level, and she looked at the chit.\n\nMinus nine hundred and four, plus the girl's own, which was the ordinary minus three hundred that a Lantern child had by eight from being fed and housed and breathed. Minus twelve hundred and four. Three hundred over the line. The girl's door would be dimmed by morning. Her ration would stop at the next posting.\n\n\"You've got people on Ten?\"\n\n\"My aunt's at the farms. She's on nights.\"\n\n\"Go to her. Tell her the Wick crews will stand it. Tell her Dagny Ostrova said. Tomorrow I'll put it through a counter.\"\n\nThe girl looked at her with the flat, measuring look of every Lantern child who had already learned that adults said things. Then she went.\n\n\"You haven't got twelve hundred,\" said Joss, when she'd gone.\n\n\"The crews have.\"\n\n\"The crews have got a hole in the bottom of the bucket same as you, Dag.\"\n\n\"Then we'll stand it with the hole.\" She stood. Her knees hurt. Her balance, in the corner of her own eye, was smaller by two hundred for a door that had not saved anyone. \"Get him covered. I'll take his name to Odile.\"\n\n## The Bell\n[\nPlace: #The Dock Ring\nCharacters: @@Dagny Ostrova @@Odile Marchbank @@Pim Ostrova @@Kas Ostrova\nItems: ^The Mercy Bell\nWhen: same day\n]\n\nThe bell of the _Ardent Mercy_ hung at the inner lock of the Dock Ring, on a hook of black iron that had been drilled into the rock the year Lantern was first pressurised. It was not large. It came up to Dagny's hip. It had been cast in the shipyards at Tannet Reach for a colony carrier that had crossed the Throat ninety-one years before, and the carrier had been cut up for her hull plate within a decade of arrival, as such ships always were; but the bell had been kept, because it was bad luck to melt a ship's bell, and because somebody on that first crossing had started the habit of ringing it for the dead.\n\nThere had been thirty-one dead on the crossing. Their names were cut round the lip in a neat, old-fashioned hand. After them came Lantern's dead, in a hand that changed every generation as the dockmasters did, round the lip and up the waist and onto the shoulder and in among the decoration on the crown, so that the bronze was now more letter than metal. From the length of the lock you could not see the names. You could only see that the bell was a strange colour, a dull brown furred all over with scratches, like an old animal.\n\nOdile Marchbank was waiting for her. She had heard; everyone on Lantern heard everything inside an hour, it was the one thing the rock did faster than the Tally. She was seventy-four and three-quarters deaf and she had kept the bell for thirty years, and her hands, which shook when she held a cup, went still as stone when they held the engraver.\n\n\"Adeyemi,\" Dagny said. \"Lemuel. Ten.\"\n\nOdile nodded. She didn't need the spelling; she had cut his wife. She took the bell-rope, which was a length of braided hauler cable, and she rang the bell once.\n\nIt was not a loud sound. Lantern swallowed noise; the rock was too big and too full of air and machinery. But the bell had a voice that went a long way anyway, low and a little sour, and all along the Dock Ring people stopped what they were doing at the sound of it. Hauler crews in the gantries. Customs men at their desks. The women who ran the noodle-stalls along the ring road. They stopped and stood, a breath's worth, and then went on.\n\nDagny felt a small hand slide into hers. She didn't look down.\n\n\"You're supposed to be at school,\" she said.\n\n\"School finished,\" said Pim. \"Everyone came out when they heard the lock. Mister Okonkwo said it was a teaching moment.\" He was twelve, and thin, and he collected bolts, and he had her husband's eyebrows, which she had never got used to. \"Was it Mister Adeyemi from Ten?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Did you try?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe thought about that. \"How much was he over?\"\n\n\"Four.\"\n\nPim did not say anything else. He held her hand while Odile fitted the engraver's burr and bent to the bronze, and the thin whine of it went up into the ring, and the first letters of Lemuel's name came up bright on the shoulder of the bell, among nine decades of brown.\n\n\"I'm running out of bell,\" Odile said, without looking up. She said it every few months. It was a sort of joke. \"One day I'll have to start on the hook.\"\n\n## The Magpie Comes In\n[\nPlace: #The Dock Ring\nCharacters: @@Dagny Ostrova @@Pim Ostrova @@Kas Ostrova\nWhen: same day\n]\n\nThe _Magpie_ came in an hour later with eighty tonnes of Caul ice in her tank-frame and a crack in her forward window that had been there so long it had a name. Her pilot came through the inner lock with his helmet under his arm and the whole of the Dock Ring's gossip already on his face.\n\n\"Who?\"\n\n\"Adeyemi. Gallery Ten.\"\n\n\"Lock?\"\n\n\"Lock.\"\n\nKasimir Ostrova closed his eyes for a moment. He was nine years younger than his sister and twice as handsome and he owed money, at a conservative estimate, to a third of the Dock Ring, none of which ever seemed to cost him a night's sleep. But he had grown up in Gallery Six the same as her, and he had seen the same locks.\n\n\"Uncle Kas,\" said Pim, and was swept up and hung upside down by the ankles, which at twelve he was too old for and permitted anyway.\n\n\"How's the run?\" Dagny said.\n\n\"Cold. Long. The usual.\" Kas set Pim the right way up. \"Quiet out there, though. Quieter than it was.\"\n\n\"Quiet how?\"\n\n\"Caul Deep's still shut. You know they closed the Deep in the spring — the big shaft, the one that took nine hundred on a shift. Nobody's working it. Beacon's dark, gantries are up. Funny to fly past. Used to be you'd see the lights of it from a thousand klicks out like a city.\" He shrugged. \"They said the vein ran out. Sent all the Deep crews home through the Throat. Contracts bought out, every one. Lucky bastards.\"\n\n\"I never heard of the Company buying out a contract in my life,\" said Dagny.\n\n\"Well. You never heard of a vein running out, neither. It's a season of firsts.\" Kas grinned at Pim. \"Come on. I'll buy you a noodle. I've been paid.\"\n\n\"You've been paid and you owe Marguerite at the noodle stall forty.\"\n\n\"Then I'll buy him a noodle and her a smile, and we'll all be square.\" He put an arm round his sister's shoulders and squeezed, briefly, hard. \"Sorry about Lemuel, Dag.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" she said. \"Me too.\"\n\nBehind them, Odile's engraver whined on in the bronze.\n\n## Fifty-Two Days\n[\nPlace: #The Galleries\nCharacters: @@Dagny Ostrova @@Pim Ostrova @@Joss Teague @@Ruth Achterberg:phone\nItems: ^The Tally\nWhen: +1 day\n]\n\nThe announcement came at eleven the next morning, on every screen on Lantern at once.\n\nDagny was in the canteen on Gallery Six with Joss and two of the Wick crew, trying to work out how many hours the Speakers could find between them to stand the Adeyemi girl's debt, and getting a number that was not enough. The screen on the canteen wall was usually showing either the Company's safety notices or a football match from somewhere in the Concord that had been played eleven years before and brought through the Throat on a data-run. It went dark in the middle of a goal, and then it showed the Company's mark, the two letters H and A leaning on each other like tired men, and then it showed Ruth Achterberg.\n\nThe canteen went quiet in the way Lantern went quiet for the bell.\n\nThe Director was sitting at a desk with nothing on it. She was a lean, grey woman of sixty in a dark Company suit, and she wore at her throat, on a fine chain, the brass disk of the Director's seal, which every child on Lantern had been told was the key to everything. She did not look cruel. She looked like someone who had been awake all night doing arithmetic and had come out of it with an answer she disliked but would not apologise for.\n\n\"People of the Sabra Concern,\" she said. \"I am speaking to you today with news that affects every one of us.\n\n\"As you know, the Throat which connects this system to the Concord is not a fixed thing. It breathes. Its throat-watch station has measured that breathing for the whole of this colony's history. Three weeks ago the station recorded the beginning of what our physicists call a closing phase. Throats which enter a closing phase do not reopen on any timescale that is useful to us. The best estimate, from the Concord's own literature, is somewhere between six and eight hundred years.\n\n\"The Throat will remain passable for fifty-two days. After that, this system will be cut off.\"\n\nSomebody in the canteen laughed, the high bark of a person who has been hit. Nobody else made a sound.\n\n\"The Halden-Ash Company has a duty to its shareholders and to its staff,\" said the Director. \"It cannot maintain a concern which it cannot reach. It will therefore withdraw from the Sabra system before the Throat closes. The carrier _Covenant_, now at our fore dock, will carry essential staff and their families and such Company assets as can be moved. I have asked the department heads to prepare those lists.\n\n\"I want to speak directly to the indentured population of Lantern.\" She looked into the lens. Dagny would remember that afterwards: that she did not look away. \"Your contracts are held by the Company. The Company does not abandon what it holds. Your contracts will remain in force, in trust, for the full term of the closure, and will be honoured when the Throat reopens. Lantern's systems — its air, its water, its heat, its light — will continue to be administered by the Tally, which has kept you safe for ninety years and which will keep you safe after we are gone. The Tally does not need us. It needs only that you go on working, as you always have, and it will go on keeping you alive.\n\n\"I know that this is hard news. I wish that I had better. Thank you.\"\n\nThe screen went back to the Company's mark. Then, after a moment, to the football match, which had been paused at the instant before the goal and now went on as if nothing had happened, the ball crossing the line, the crowd eleven years ago and four hundred light-years away going wild.\n\n[\nPlace: #The Galleries\nCharacters: @@Dagny Ostrova @@Pim Ostrova @@Joss Teague\nWhen: same day\n]\n\nWhat happened in the canteen in the next ten minutes Dagny remembered afterwards only in pieces. A man on Gallery Six put his chair through the screen and the screen went on showing football in a web of cracks. Two women began to fight over nothing, over a bench, and had to be pulled apart. Old Mister Okonkwo the schoolteacher sat with his hands flat on the table, saying, \"Six hundred years, six hundred years,\" as if it were a sum that would come out differently if he said it often enough. Somebody shouted that the Company would take the fuel. Somebody shouted that the Company would take the water. Somebody shouted that they should go up to the Upper Wards now, all of them, and see how many lists the department heads had made when they had their own windows broken.\n\nAnd Pim was beside her, she didn't know when he had come in, saying, \"Mum. Mum. In trust. What's in trust mean?\"\n\nShe understood it, she realised, before she understood anything else. It was Lantern; you learned the Company's language before you learned your prayers. _Held in trust_ meant that the Company was leaving, and the debt was staying. It meant that every chit on every wrist on Lantern would go on counting down, hour by hour, against the air, for six hundred years, and that if the Throat ever opened again somebody in a Company suit would come through it with the books in their hand to see how much was owed. It meant that the Adeyemi girl's twelve hundred would be eighteen hundred by the time she was grown and nine thousand by the time she died, and would go to whoever came after her.\n\nIt meant that the lock on Gallery Nine would go on doing what it had done to Lemuel, with nobody even in the Counting House to blame for it.\n\nShe found she was standing on a table. She did not remember getting up there. The canteen was looking at her, the way the Wick crews looked at her when the lamp went wrong and someone had to say a number out loud.\n\n\"All right,\" Dagny said. Her voice came out level, which surprised her. \"All right. Shut up and listen. I'm a Speaker for the Wick crews, and from tonight the Wick crews are going to want to talk to the other crews. Pumps, farms, docks, stills. Everyone who wears a chit. Fifty-two days is a long time. It's long enough for the Company to pack. It's long enough for us to make it listen first.\"\n\n\"Listen to what?\" somebody shouted.\n\n\"To the one thing it hasn't said.\" She held up her wrist, with the chit on it, so that the canteen could see the blue numbers. \"She said the Tally keeps us alive. It doesn't. We keep it alive. We fed it ninety years. If the Company's going, the Company can take its ledger with it, or it can switch the bloody thing off before it goes. Either way, nobody on this rock is going to owe air to an empty office for six hundred years.\"\n\nThere was a silence. Then the man who had broken the screen began to clap, slowly, and others took it up; and Joss, below her, looked up with his wet red face and said, very quietly, so that only she could hear: \"Dag. You know what you just did.\"\n\nShe did. She had just volunteered.","wordCount":3670,"createdAt":1793145334280},{"id":"RV8Wzspx9OQr3q6uktJ7u","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"e2oBYFVEZ5GlPkKSEnkkB","text":"The call came down the spine at the end of second shift, when Dagny Ostrova was forty metres up the Wick with her face in a collimator housing and her knees locked round a rung that had been warm for ninety years.\n\n\"Gallery Nine,\" said the speaker on her collar. It was Joss, below her on the crawl, and his voice had the flatness it got when something was already too late and he hadn't decided to know it yet. \"Lock Nine-Forty. Man in it. It's gone to recovery.\"\n\nShe was moving before he finished. You did not think about a lock in recovery. You thought about it afterwards, at the bell, with a drink in you.\n\nGoing down the Wick was going down only in the sense that the rock's spin pulled you, gently at first and then less gently, toward the curved floor of the world. At the axis there was no weight at all, only the lamp: three kilometres of magnetic bottle and shielding and slow white fire, with the crawls and catwalks wrapped round it like ivy round a pole. Its light fell on everything. It fell on the terraced Galleries that climbed the inner wall in every direction, on the water tanks and the strip-farms and the tin roofs, on the long seam of the Upper Wards where the Company people lived with real glass in their windows. On Lantern you could look up from anywhere and see someone's house above you, upside down, lit from the side by the same lamp that lit yours. Children were taught early not to drop things from high terraces. It was the first rule. The second was to watch your balance.\n\nDagny dropped through the spoke-shaft hand over hand, letting her weight build, and hit the Gallery Nine landing at a run with Joss three seconds behind her, wheezing. A crowd had already made a half-ring round Lock Nine-Forty. Nobody was touching it. On Lantern, nobody touched a lock in recovery, because the Tally logged who touched what.\n\nThe lock was a steel bottle three metres long, set between the Gallery Nine pressure zone and the Gallery Ten one, so that if either breached the other would hold. You walked into it, the door behind you sealed, the Tally read the chit on your wrist, and if you were in credit the door in front of you opened. If you were not, it didn't. And because the Company had built Lantern on the principle that air was the dearest thing in the Sabra system and must be recovered wherever it could be, a lock with an unauthorised occupant began, after ninety seconds, to draw its air back into the main for reclamation.\n\nLemuel Adeyemi was in the lock. Dagny knew him a little. Pump-fitter, Gallery Ten. Fifties. A cough. A wife who had died two winters back of the same cough, and whose name was on the bell. He was standing with both palms flat on the inner door's window, and his mouth was open, and his breath was making less fog on the glass than it should have.\n\n\"How long?\" Dagny said.\n\n\"Two minutes in recovery,\" somebody said. \"His shift got docked. Pump fault on Ten, they docked the whole crew a half-day. It posted on the hour. He was already in the lock when it posted.\"\n\nDagny looked at the panel by the door. The little screen showed his number and his balance, in the Company's blue, and the balance was in brackets, which was how the Tally wrote a debt it had stopped being patient with. Minus nine hundred and four. The dimming line on Lantern was minus nine hundred.\n\nFour. He was four over the line. Four was a meal. Four was an hour of water.\n\n\"Transfer,\" she said, and slapped her own chit onto the panel. \"I'll stand it. Transfer from Ostrova, nine-oh-six-two-two, to—\"\n\nThe panel chimed politely. TRANSFERS BETWEEN ACCOUNTS POST AT THE HOUR. PLEASE SEE A COUNTER.\n\n\"There's no counter on Nine at shift-end,\" Joss said behind her. \"There's no counter on Nine ever.\"\n\n\"Then get me the override.\"\n\n\"Dag. The override's in the Counting House.\"\n\nShe knew that. Everyone knew that. She said it anyway because it was the next thing to say, and because the alternative was to stand and watch, and the crowd was already standing and watching. Inside the lock Lemuel had seen her. He took one hand off the glass and made a small shape with it, a sort of apology, as if he were sorry to be making a scene.\n\n\"Torch,\" said Dagny.\n\n\"It's a pressure door. It'll take—\"\n\n\"Torch, Joss.\"\n\nHe had one on his belt, because Wick crews carried everything. She lit it and put the flame to the hinge side of the outer door and the steel went cherry-red and then white and began, very slowly, to run. The crowd made a noise behind her. The panel spoke again, in the same tone: TAMPERING WITH COMPANY PRESSURE EQUIPMENT IS CHARGED AT TWO HUNDRED CREDITS PER INCIDENT TO THE ACCOUNT OF THE TAMPERER. It read her chit through her glove. She watched her own balance drop by two hundred in the corner of the screen and kept cutting.\n\nIt took four minutes. A man can live for four minutes in a lock in recovery; Dagny had seen it. What a man cannot do is live for four minutes in a lock in recovery after he has already been in it for three. By the time the hinge gave and Joss got a pry-bar into the gap and the two of them hauled the door back on its seal, there was nothing in the lock but cold, thin air, rushing out past them into the gallery with a sound like a long breath let go, and Lemuel Adeyemi on his knees with his forehead against the inner door, as though he had decided at the end to pray to it.\n\nDagny went in and turned him over. His eyes were open. His lips were the colour of the shadow under a terrace. She put two fingers on his neck because she had been trained to and because people were watching, and then she took them away.\n\nBehind her the panel on the wall chimed a third time. Lemuel's number went grey, and under it, small and blue and patient, the Tally posted his last entry: ACCOUNT CLOSED BY DEATH. BALANCE TRANSFERRED TO ESTATE. And then, because Lemuel had no estate but a cough and a bunk and a wife's name on a bell, it posted the next one, which Dagny had seen a hundred times and which never stopped being the worst thing she had ever read: ESTATE INSOLVENT. BALANCE TRANSFERRED TO NEXT OF KIN.\n\n\"Who's his kin?\" she said.\n\nNobody answered. Then a girl at the front of the crowd, eight or nine, with a chit on a cord round her neck and her hair cut short against lice, said, \"Me.\"\n\n## Next of Kin\n[\nPlace: #The Galleries\nCharacters: @@Dagny Ostrova @@Joss Teague\nWhen: same day\n]\n\nJoss sat on the landing with the torch in his lap and wept the way big men did on Lantern, quietly and with his face turned to the wall. Dagny did not weep. She went to the girl, whose name was Abi and who was Lemuel's sister's child, and she knelt so that their faces were level, and she looked at the chit.\n\nMinus nine hundred and four, plus the girl's own, which was the ordinary minus three hundred that a Lantern child had by eight from being fed and housed and breathed. Minus twelve hundred and four. Three hundred over the line. The girl's door would be dimmed by morning. Her ration would stop at the next posting.\n\n\"You've got people on Ten?\"\n\n\"My aunt's at the farms. She's on nights.\"\n\n\"Go to her. Tell her the Wick crews will stand it. Tell her Dagny Ostrova said. Tomorrow I'll put it through a counter.\"\n\nThe girl looked at her with the flat, measuring look of every Lantern child who had already learned that adults said things. Then she went.\n\n\"You haven't got twelve hundred,\" said Joss, when she'd gone.\n\n\"The crews have.\"\n\n\"The crews have got a hole in the bottom of the bucket same as you, Dag.\"\n\n\"Then we'll stand it with the hole.\" She stood. Her knees hurt. Her balance, in the corner of her own eye, was smaller by two hundred for a door that had not saved anyone. \"Get him covered. I'll take his name to Odile.\"\n\n## The Bell\n[\nPlace: #The Dock Ring\nCharacters: @@Dagny Ostrova @@Odile Marchbank @@Pim Ostrova @@Kas Ostrova\nItems: ^The Mercy Bell\nWhen: same day\n]\n\nThe bell of the _Ardent Mercy_ hung at the inner lock of the Dock Ring, on a hook of black iron that had been drilled into the rock the year Lantern was first pressurised. It was not large. It came up to Dagny's hip. It had been cast in the shipyards at Tannet Reach for a colony carrier that had crossed the Throat ninety-one years before, and the carrier had been cut up for her hull plate within a decade of arrival, as such ships always were; but the bell had been kept, because it was bad luck to melt a ship's bell, and because somebody on that first crossing had started the habit of ringing it for the dead.\n\nThere had been thirty-one dead on the crossing. Their names were cut round the lip in a neat, old-fashioned hand. After them came Lantern's dead, in a hand that changed every generation as the dockmasters did, round the lip and up the waist and onto the shoulder and in among the decoration on the crown, so that the bronze was now more letter than metal. From the length of the lock you could not see the names. You could only see that the bell was a strange colour, a dull brown furred all over with scratches, like an old animal.\n\nOdile Marchbank was waiting for her. She had heard; everyone on Lantern heard everything inside an hour, it was the one thing the rock did faster than the Tally. She was seventy-four and three-quarters deaf and she had kept the bell for thirty years, and her hands, which shook when she held a cup, went still as stone when they held the engraver.\n\n\"Adeyemi,\" Dagny said. \"Lemuel. Ten.\"\n\nOdile nodded. She didn't need the spelling; she had cut his wife. She took the bell-rope, which was a length of braided hauler cable, and she rang the bell once.\n\nIt was not a loud sound. Lantern swallowed noise; the rock was too big and too full of air and machinery. But the bell had a voice that went a long way anyway, low and a little sour, and all along the Dock Ring people stopped what they were doing at the sound of it. Hauler crews in the gantries. Customs men at their desks. The women who ran the noodle-stalls along the ring road. They stopped and stood, a breath's worth, and then went on.\n\nDagny felt a small hand slide into hers. She didn't look down.\n\n\"You're supposed to be at school,\" she said.\n\n\"School finished,\" said Pim. \"Everyone came out when they heard the lock. Mister Okonkwo said it was a teaching moment.\" He was twelve, and thin, and he collected bolts, and he had her husband's eyebrows, which she had never got used to. \"Was it Mister Adeyemi from Ten?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Did you try?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe thought about that. \"How much was he over?\"\n\n\"Four.\"\n\nPim did not say anything else. He held her hand while Odile fitted the engraver's burr and bent to the bronze, and the thin whine of it went up into the ring, and the first letters of Lemuel's name came up bright on the shoulder of the bell, among nine decades of brown.\n\n\"I'm running out of bell,\" Odile said, without looking up. She said it every few months. It was a sort of joke. \"One day I'll have to start on the hook.\"\n\n[\nPlace: #The Dock Ring\nCharacters: @@Dagny Ostrova @@Pim Ostrova @@Kas Ostrova\nWhen: same day\n]\n\nThe _Magpie_ came in an hour later with eighty tonnes of Caul ice in her tank-frame and a crack in her forward window that had been there so long it had a name. Her pilot came through the inner lock with his helmet under his arm and the whole of the Dock Ring's gossip already on his face.\n\n\"Who?\"\n\n\"Adeyemi. Gallery Ten.\"\n\n\"Lock?\"\n\n\"Lock.\"\n\nKasimir Ostrova closed his eyes for a moment. He was nine years younger than his sister and twice as handsome and he owed money, at a conservative estimate, to a third of the Dock Ring, none of which ever seemed to cost him a night's sleep. But he had grown up in Gallery Six the same as her, and he had seen the same locks.\n\n\"Uncle Kas,\" said Pim, and was swept up and hung upside down by the ankles, which at twelve he was too old for and permitted anyway.\n\n\"How's the run?\" Dagny said.\n\n\"Cold. Long. The usual.\" Kas set Pim the right way up. \"Quiet out there, though. Quieter than it was.\"\n\n\"Quiet how?\"\n\n\"Caul Deep's still shut. You know they closed the Deep in the spring — the big shaft, the one that took nine hundred on a shift. Nobody's working it. Beacon's dark, gantries are up. Funny to fly past. Used to be you'd see the lights of it from a thousand klicks out like a city.\" He shrugged. \"They said the vein ran out. Sent all the Deep crews home through the Throat. Contracts bought out, every one. Lucky bastards.\"\n\n\"I never heard of the Company buying out a contract in my life,\" said Dagny.\n\n\"Well. You never heard of a vein running out, neither. It's a season of firsts.\" Kas grinned at Pim. \"Come on. I'll buy you a noodle. I've been paid.\"\n\n\"You've been paid and you owe Marguerite at the noodle stall forty.\"\n\n\"Then I'll buy him a noodle and her a smile, and we'll all be square.\" He put an arm round his sister's shoulders and squeezed, briefly, hard. \"Sorry about Lemuel, Dag.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" she said. \"Me too.\"\n\nBehind them, Odile's engraver whined on in the bronze.","wordCount":2410,"createdAt":1793145334536},{"id":"_tSP0GmuQwGwSlZIyddL2","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"YoW2-AGKrhEXdDCLO-hxU","text":"Basu was waiting for her at the foot of the Wick when the _Magpie_ docked, and he did not need to ask. He looked at her face and nodded, and then he said, \"Come up. Please. There's something I can show you now. I couldn't before.\"\n\nHe took her up into the Counting House by the service stair, past the offices, to a narrow room at the back of the comptroller's floor where there was nothing but a wall of old screens and a chair. There were no windows. On every screen there were figures.\n\n\"The throat-watch station,\" he said. \"Its raw feed. Not the summaries that come to the Director's office. The raw readings, every six hours, from the instruments. They come to my floor first, for the accounts — the Company charges the Concern for the station's running, so I log its output. Nobody's ever looked at them but me and the man who sends them. A man called Roach. He's been out there eleven years.\" He sat down in the chair and began touching screens, bringing up columns. \"I'm not a physicist. But I can read a trend. I've read nothing but trends for thirty years. I went back through every reading for the last six months, last night, after I left you.\"\n\nHe turned the chair round to face her.\n\n\"There is no closing phase,\" said Teodor Basu. \"There never was. The Throat's breathing is exactly what it's been for ninety-one years. Regular as a clock. The numbers in the Director's announcement — the ones she said came from the throat-watch — they aren't in the throat-watch's feed. They aren't anywhere. Somebody in the Director's office made them up.\" He took his glasses off and rubbed his eyes, carefully, round the cracked lens. \"Roach has been asking for confirmation. Three times. Nobody's answered him.\"\n\nDagny sat down on the floor with her back against the door, because there was nowhere else to sit and her legs had stopped holding her.\n\n\"Then it's not closing,\" she said. \"It's not going to close. We could just— wait. Let them go. And in sixty days the inspectors come through, and—\"\n\n\"It's going to close,\" said Basu.\n\nHe turned back to the screens and touched one, and the columns of throat-watch figures gave way to a single page of text. A manifest entry. She knew the format; she had seen him look at it before, on the bench by the bell.\n\n_Device, Concord ordnance, class T. Shielded bay 4, aft of drive. Received under Security seal._\n\n\"I found out what class T means,\" he said. \"It took me a while. It's not in any Company catalogue. It's in the Concord's own military register, the part that's public, under _throat ordnance_. They were made during the Succession Wars, two hundred years ago, when the Concord was fighting itself through every throat it had. A fleet that was losing would go back through a throat and then—\" he made a small movement with his hand, a closing— \"behind them. So the enemy couldn't follow.\"\n\n\"Close it,\" said Dagny.\n\n\"Collapse it. The register calls it a shear. A throat-shear. It's fired into the mouth of a throat by a ship in transit, and it— the physics is beyond me. It cuts the throat's lips. They fold in. The throat collapses. And it stays collapsed, Ms. Ostrova, for six or seven or eight hundred years, exactly as the Director said in her announcement, because that much of what she said is true: a collapsed throat heals on its own time and no one else's.\" He looked at her. \"The Company bought a shear from a Concord arms broker three months ago. Before the throat-watch said anything at all. Before the Director announced anything at all. The _Covenant_ will carry it into the Throat on the day she leaves, with four hundred and six essential staff aboard, and fire it behind her as she comes out the other side. And the Throat will close on the day the Director said it would, and the inspectors will be on the other side of it, and the nine hundred and four will be under the ice of Caul for ever, and nobody in the Concord will ever know. Except as a line in a Company report. _Repatriated._\"\n\n\"And us.\"\n\n\"And you. Nine thousand people who know what Caul is, sealed in a box with a meter on the air, with your contracts held in trust.\" He spread his hands. \"The Company doesn't need to silence you. The Throat will do it.\"\n\nFor a long time neither of them said anything. Dagny sat against the door with her knees up and her hands over her face and saw, on the inside of her eyelids, the blue of the ice and the woman with her palm on the glass.\n\nThen she took her hands away.\n\n\"Then we stop the shear,\" she said. \"We stop it, and we let the inspectors come.\"\n\nTeodor Basu looked at her for a long moment through his cracked glasses.\n\n\"Yes,\" he said. \"I hoped you would say that.\"","wordCount":854,"createdAt":1793147392567},{"id":"bBm1MWqIsfUqmkQ7edSJK","worldId":"p5RB-gewXUZYlHUKTH5pw","eventId":"e2oBYFVEZ5GlPkKSEnkkB","text":"The call came down the spine at the end of second shift, when Dagny Ostrova was forty metres up the Wick with her face in a collimator housing and her knees locked round a rung that had been warm for ninety years.\n\n\"Gallery Nine,\" said the speaker on her collar. It was Joss, below her on the crawl, and his voice had the flatness it got when something was already too late and he hadn't decided to know it yet. \"Lock Nine-Forty. Man in it. It's gone to recovery.\"\n\nShe was moving before he finished. You did not think about a lock in recovery. You thought about it afterwards, at the bell, with a drink in you.\n\nGoing down the Wick was going down only in the sense that the rock's spin pulled you, gently at first and then less gently, toward the curved floor of the world. At the axis there was no weight at all, only the lamp: three kilometres of magnetic bottle and shielding and slow white fire, with the crawls and catwalks wrapped round it like ivy round a pole. Its light fell on everything. It fell on the terraced Galleries that climbed the inner wall in every direction, on the water tanks and the strip-farms and the tin roofs, on the long seam of the Upper Wards where the Company people lived with real glass in their windows. On Lantern you could look up from anywhere and see someone's house above you, upside down, lit from the side by the same lamp that lit yours. Children were taught early not to drop things from high terraces. It was the first rule. The second was to watch your balance.\n\nDagny dropped through the spoke-shaft hand over hand, letting her weight build, and hit the Gallery Nine landing at a run with Joss three seconds behind her, wheezing. A crowd had already made a half-ring round Lock Nine-Forty. Nobody was touching it. On Lantern, nobody touched a lock in recovery, because the Tally logged who touched what.\n\nThe lock was a steel bottle three metres long, set between the Gallery Nine pressure zone and the Gallery Ten one, so that if either breached the other would hold. You walked into it, the door behind you sealed, the Tally read the chit on your wrist, and if you were in credit the door in front of you opened. If you were not, it didn't. And because the Company had built Lantern on the principle that air was the dearest thing in the Sabra system and must be recovered wherever it could be, a lock with an unauthorised occupant began, after ninety seconds, to draw its air back into the main for reclamation.\n\nLemuel Adeyemi was in the lock. Dagny knew him a little. Pump-fitter, Gallery Ten. Fifties. A cough. A wife who had died two winters back of the same cough, and whose name was on the bell. He was standing with both palms flat on the inner door's window, and his mouth was open, and his breath was making less fog on the glass than it should have.\n\n\"How long?\" Dagny said.\n\n\"Two minutes in recovery,\" somebody said. \"His shift got docked. Pump fault on Ten, they docked the whole crew a half-day. It posted on the hour. He was already in the lock when it posted.\"\n\nDagny looked at the panel by the door. The little screen showed his number and his balance, in the Company's blue, and the balance was in brackets, which was how the Tally wrote a debt it had stopped being patient with. Minus nine hundred and four. The dimming line on Lantern was minus nine hundred.\n\nFour. He was four over the line. Four was a meal. Four was an hour of water.\n\n\"Transfer,\" she said, and slapped her own chit onto the panel. \"I'll stand it. Transfer from Ostrova, nine-oh-six-two-two, to—\"\n\nThe panel chimed politely. TRANSFERS BETWEEN ACCOUNTS POST AT THE HOUR. PLEASE SEE A COUNTER.\n\n\"There's no counter on Nine at shift-end,\" Joss said behind her. \"There's no counter on Nine ever.\"\n\n\"Then get me the override.\"\n\n\"Dag. The override's in the Counting House.\"\n\nShe knew that. Everyone knew that. She said it anyway because it was the next thing to say, and because the alternative was to stand and watch, and the crowd was already standing and watching. Inside the lock Lemuel had seen her. He took one hand off the glass and made a small shape with it, a sort of apology, as if he were sorry to be making a scene.\n\n\"Torch,\" said Dagny.\n\n\"It's a pressure door. It'll take—\"\n\n\"Torch, Joss.\"\n\nHe had one on his belt, because Wick crews carried everything. She lit it and put the flame to the hinge side of the outer door and the steel went cherry-red and then white and began, very slowly, to run. The crowd made a noise behind her. The panel spoke again, in the same tone: TAMPERING WITH COMPANY PRESSURE EQUIPMENT IS CHARGED AT TWO HUNDRED CREDITS PER INCIDENT TO THE ACCOUNT OF THE TAMPERER. It read her chit through her glove. She watched her own balance drop by two hundred in the corner of the screen and kept cutting.\n\nIt took four minutes. A man can live for four minutes in a lock in recovery; Dagny had seen it. What a man cannot do is live for four minutes in a lock in recovery after he has already been in it for three. By the time the hinge gave and Joss got a pry-bar into the gap and the two of them hauled the door back on its seal, there was nothing in the lock but cold, thin air, rushing out past them into the gallery with a sound like a long breath let go, and Lemuel Adeyemi on his knees with his forehead against the inner door, as though he had decided at the end to pray to it.\n\nDagny went in and turned him over. His eyes were open. His lips were the colour of the shadow under a terrace. She put two fingers on his neck because she had been trained to and because people were watching, and then she took them away.\n\nBehind her the panel on the wall chimed a third time. Lemuel's number went grey, and under it, small and blue and patient, the Tally posted his last entry: ACCOUNT CLOSED BY DEATH. BALANCE TRANSFERRED TO ESTATE. And then, because Lemuel had no estate but a cough and a bunk and a wife's name on a bell, it posted the next one, which Dagny had seen a hundred times and which never stopped being the worst thing she had ever read: ESTATE INSOLVENT. BALANCE TRANSFERRED TO NEXT OF KIN.\n\n\"Who's his kin?\" she said.\n\nNobody answered. Then a girl at the front of the crowd, eight or nine, with a chit on a cord round her neck and her hair cut short against lice, said, \"Me.\"\n\n[\nPlace: #The Galleries\nCharacters: @@Dagny Ostrova @@Joss Teague\nWhen: same day\n]\n\nJoss sat on the landing with the torch in his lap and wept the way big men did on Lantern, quietly and with his face turned to the wall. Dagny did not weep. She went to the girl, whose name was Abi and who was Lemuel's sister's child, and she knelt so that their faces were level, and she looked at the chit.\n\nMinus nine hundred and four, plus the girl's own, which was the ordinary minus three hundred that a Lantern child had by eight from being fed and housed and breathed. Minus twelve hundred and four. Three hundred over the line. The girl's door would be dimmed by morning. Her ration would stop at the next posting.\n\n\"You've got people on Ten?\"\n\n\"My aunt's at the farms. She's on nights.\"\n\n\"Go to her. Tell her the Wick crews will stand it. Tell her Dagny Ostrova said. Tomorrow I'll put it through a counter.\"\n\nThe girl looked at her with the flat, measuring look of every Lantern child who had already learned that adults said things. Then she went.\n\n\"You haven't got twelve hundred,\" said Joss, when she'd gone.\n\n\"The crews have.\"\n\n\"The crews have got a hole in the bottom of the bucket same as you, Dag.\"\n\n\"Then we'll stand it with the hole.\" She stood. Her knees hurt. Her balance, in the corner of her own eye, was smaller by two hundred for a door that had not saved anyone. \"Get him covered. I'll take his name to Odile.\"","wordCount":1433,"createdAt":1793145334699}],"tombstones":[],"blobs":[]}